\subsection{Overview}

Subsurface (tile) drainage is one of the best-established engineering interventions for leaching accumulated salts out of irrigated and waterlogged soil profiles, and the assembled evidence---spanning field experiments, lysimeter and soil-column studies, long-term monitoring, and calibrated numerical models across arid, semi-arid, monsoonal, coastal, and cold-climate settings---converges on a coherent picture of what the technology accomplishes and how. Three headline conclusions are strongly supported. First, installing subsurface drainage reliably lowers the water table below the root zone and removes salt from the upper profile, converting saline and waterlogged land into productive cropland, with topsoil salinity reductions commonly of 35--80\% and corresponding crop-yield gains; a global meta-analysis of 891 experiments confirms that drainage significantly reduces soil salinity and increases yield relative to no drainage \parencite{Yang2025}, and a full production-scale field study validates outcomes previously seen only at small scale \parencite{Derby2024}. Second, the rate and completeness of desalinization are governed by a well-characterized set of design and operational parameters---drain spacing, drain depth, envelope/filter, leaching-water quantity, and complementary practices---such that closer spacing, greater depth (up to soil-specific limits), and larger applied leaching volume shorten the desalinization period. Third, the salt exported in drainage water is initially high and declines over years as the profile is flushed, so that salt-balance accounting (salt removed versus salt added) and the temporal trajectory of effluent salinity are central to both agronomic and environmental evaluation. The remainder of this section synthesizes the consolidated findings on reclamation efficacy, the design parameters that control salt removal, drainage-water salinity and salt balance, the vertical and ionic structure of leaching, the hydraulics of efficient leaching and enhancement techniques, saline/brackish water reuse, integration with complementary practices, and the modeling and monitoring tools that underpin these conclusions.

\subsection{Reclamation Efficacy: Soil Desalinization and Crop Response}

Across widely differing environments, subsurface drainage produces substantial and repeatable reductions in root-zone salinity, typically concentrated in the surface layers, together with improved crop performance. In the Northern Great Plains, topsoil salinity in a tile-drained area declined over time while an adjacent undrained area showed little change or a slight increase; drainage alleviated both salinity and wetness constraints, and soybean and corn yields improved within three years, with continued soybean gains expected over at least eight years \parencite{Derby2024}. Comparable field-scale reclamations are documented in India, where average soil salinity in drained blocks fell from 7.18 to 4.62~dS~m$^{-1}$ (an average 35.7\% decrease, ranging 16.0--66.3\% across blocks) while the undrained area rose from 9.00 to 9.54~dS~m$^{-1}$, and crop yields rose by 18.75--27.62\% for wheat, rice, pearl millet, and sorghum \parencite{Sharma2006}. In monsoonal India, drainage combined with monsoon leaching brought initially crop-limiting salinity down to levels suitable for irrigated agriculture, with weighted-average ECe after 13 monsoon leachings of 1.4, 2.1, and 3.4~dS~m$^{-1}$ at 25, 50, and 75~m spacing \parencite{Sharma2000}. Rolling-topography reclamation under unusually wide drain spacing lowered topsoil salinity from 8.4 to about 2.1~dS~m$^{-1}$ and raised paddy yields, with cropping intensity increasing from 143\% to 191\% \parencite{Manjunatha2004}, and multi-scenario evaluation of the Tungabhadra command reduced head- and middle-reach topsoil ECe from roughly 9~dS~m$^{-1}$ to 1.9--3.8~dS~m$^{-1}$, with weaker effect in the tail reach \parencite{Karegoudar2022}.

Reclamation magnitudes are similarly large in China's arid and coastal saline lands. Subsurface pipe drainage reduced 0--80~cm salinity from 35 to 15~g~kg$^{-1}$, whereas open-ditch controls remained at 25--35~g~kg$^{-1}$ \parencite{Wang2020}; a drip-irrigation-plus-subsurface-pipe system cut soil EC from 14.42--15.74 to 2.86--6.91~dS~m$^{-1}$ over three leaching cycles and raised seed cotton yield 52\% \parencite{Heng2021}; and after four years the salinity of the 0--1.0~m layer fell from 27.6 to 4.1--7.2~dS~m$^{-1}$, reclaiming extremely saline soil to moderately saline \parencite{Zhang2021}. One-year monitoring by fixed electromagnetic-induction profiles showed soil apparent EC falling 40.0--62.0\% across depths (mean 49.5\%) and a sharp reduction in spatial heterogeneity \parencite{Yu2025}. Surface-concentrated desalinization is a recurrent signature: 0--20~cm desalination rates exceeded 80\% while deeper layers lagged or even accumulated salt \parencite{Han2023}, and the meta-analysis found the most substantial reduction at depths of 20~cm or less, diminishing with depth and becoming nonsignificant below 80~cm \parencite{Yang2025}. Reductions of comparable magnitude are reported for Turkey (topsoil salinity from 10.3 to 2.0~dS~m$^{-1}$, about 80\% at 20~cm) \parencite{Bahçeci2009}, Tunisia (mean ECe decrease 23.3\%) \parencite{HajAmor2019}, and semi-arid Canada, where combined drainage and windbreak treatment lowered 0--450~mm ECe from 14.08 to 9.58~dS~m$^{-1}$ and raised barley yield up to twentyfold \parencite{Steppuhn2006,Steppuhn2017}. Spatial reclamation is accompanied by homogenization: leaching plus drainage shifted salinity from strong to moderate variability (coefficient of variation from 48.13\% to 28.14\%) and expanded mildly saline area to 76\%, with 7~g~kg$^{-1}$ identified as the critical salinity for the homogenizing tendency \parencite{Yu2015}.

Improved soil physical condition and productivity accompany desalinization. Drainage increased porosity, available water, and infiltration rate (from 5.3 to 13.6--16.0~mm~h$^{-1}$) while lowering bulk density, and reduced SARe and ESP \parencite{Sharma2000}; the meta-analysis reports increased soil permeability and organic matter and decreased CO$_3^{2-}$, HCO$_3^-$, Cl$^-$, SO$_4^{2-}$, Na$^+$, and Mg$^{2+}$ \parencite{Yang2025}. In Egypt, drainage raised yields by 13--16\% across wheat, rice, cotton, and maize and lifted net farm income by 9--44\% depending on region \parencite{Kotb2000}. Where drainage is absent or the water table stays shallow, combined waterlogging and salinity are more damaging than waterlogging alone, and yields fail above 12~dS~m$^{-1}$ \parencite{Kahlown2002}.

\subsection{Design Parameters Governing Salt Removal}

A consistent, quantitatively supported relationship links subsurface-drainage intensity to desalinization: closer drain spacing and greater drain depth shorten the desalinization period and increase salt removal, though depth benefits saturate at soil-specific limits and shallow drains lose effectiveness. A DRAINMOD-based analysis showed the desalinization period was negatively related to drainage intensity; at 1.2~m depth, salinity fell 55.5\% in the first two years at 20~m spacing but only 5.9\% in five years at 200~m spacing, and reaching a slightly saline target within a decade required spacing below 30~m \parencite{Chen2025}. The meta-analysis generalizes this: salinity reduction improves with smaller spacing, greater depth, and longer operation time, with climate- and groundwater-specific layout recommendations---spacing $>$30~m and depth $>$1.2~m in humid climates, 10--20~m spacing with depth $>$1.2~m in arid climates with water table $\leq$1~m \parencite{Yang2025}. Field and column studies repeatedly identify optimum spacings: 15~m gave the best desalination (effective rate 65.8\%, salt-removal ratio 69.6\%) among 10--25~m spacings \parencite{Wang2020}; 20~m spacing at 1.5~m depth optimized root-zone desalination (39.02\% reduction versus control) by multi-objective optimization \parencite{Wang2025c}; 6~m spacing with 40~cm outlet depth maximized average desalination \parencite{Han2023}; and 30~cm partition spacing outperformed wider spacings \parencite{Chen2022}. Smaller spacing also lowers the leaching quota needed for a given efficiency and deepens and stabilizes the water table \parencite{Yang2022c}, while desalination benefits decay once spacing exceeds about 30~m, with some wide-spacing/deep-burial treatments even showing negative desalination \parencite{Zhu2025b}.

Drain depth effects are pronounced but bounded. Pipes shallower than 1.0~m were essentially invalid for discharging salt or controlling the water table \parencite{Qian2021b}, and desalination and salt discharge indices correlated positively with depth \parencite{Qian2021b,Tian2023c,Tian2024}. Yet SaltMod and SahysMod simulations show diminishing returns beyond a threshold: root-zone salinity decreased with depth up to 1.4~m with no further change beyond it \parencite{Bahçeci2007}, and increasing depth from 0.80 to 1.60~m only slightly reduced salinity while increasing discharge \parencite{Bahçeci2006}. For crop-yield optimization under saline irrigation, an intermediate depth is frequently preferred---0.8~m gave the best leaching and highest maize yield \parencite{Feng2017}, relative yield and water-use efficiency peaked near 90--100~cm \parencite{Feng2019}, and reducing depth from 140 to 100--120~cm saved 10--15\% of irrigation water with only a small yield penalty and no significant long-term salinity increase \parencite{Muhammad2021}. Envelope and filter design is a first-order control on discharge and salt leaching: the envelope factor dominated over pipe material, with spun-bonded non-woven geotextile, single layer, and 3.0\% perforation optimal \parencite{Li2022}; double-layer geotextile integrated filtration, anti-clogging, and drainage best (all scenarios $>$95\% salt removal), while triple layers caused filter-cake clogging \parencite{Wang2024d}; and the O$_{90}$/d$_{90}$ ratio predicts clogging and soil loss into pipes \parencite{Elzoghby2021}. Complex stratigraphy modifies the optimum: low-permeability silty interlayers reduce leaching, and pipes should be placed below such interlayers, with desalination increasing linearly with depth and interlayer conductivity and exponentially as spacing decreases \parencite{Guo2024b}; a shallow red-clay layer likewise reduces drainage and salt-removal efficiency \parencite{Tian2024}. Long-term modeling indicates deeper, wider drains desalinize the whole profile better whereas shallow, close drains act mainly in the top 80~cm \parencite{KAMRA1991}, and economic analyses in multiple settings converge on roughly 20~m spacing at 1.2~m depth as the maximum-return layout \parencite{Chen2025}.

\subsection{Drainage-Water Salinity, Salt Balance, and Temporal Decline}

Because tile drains intercept the salt-laden water flushed from the profile, drainage-water salinity is characteristically much higher than irrigation-water salinity and declines over years as the reservoir of stored salt is depleted. Effluent EC exceeding irrigation EC has been documented repeatedly \parencite{Li2022,Karegoudar2022}, with ECdw/ECiw ratios of 11--17 and annual salt export near 29.4~t~ha$^{-1}$ in a high-percolation Iranian sugarcane district \parencite{Akram2013}, and 8{,}000~mg~L$^{-1}$ irrigation water producing 14{,}000~mg~L$^{-1}$ drainage water through evapotranspirative concentration in California \parencite{KARAJEH1994c}. Salt-balance accounting is the standard diagnostic of reclamation: salt removed exceeded salt added in the first year (125.4 versus 119.8~mg), although over the full record (August 1998--April 2002) cumulative salts added ($\approx$452.9~mg) exceeded salts removed ($\approx$372.9~mg) \parencite{Manjunatha2004}, an oasis system exported about 94\% of the imported salt load while discharging 42\% of irrigation water, yielding a positive salt balance \parencite{Askri2022}, and a straw-drainage module removed about 1.7 times the input salt mass \parencite{Lu2019}. Salt removal can, however, fall short: an oasis-scale system removed only 30\% then 15\% of salts owing to mineral and root clogging \parencite{BenAissa2013}, and a sand-clogged gravel filter drained no more than 27\% of the imported salt \parencite{HajAmor2019}. During the irrigation--drainage season, subsurface pipes discharged 3.12--4.29~t~ha$^{-1}$ of salt, accounting for 34--46\% of soil salt-storage change, the remainder leaching to deeper layers or groundwater \parencite{Liu2025c}; over four years pipes removed 40\% of total salt discharge \parencite{Heng2021}.

The temporal decline of effluent salinity is a robust, long-validated pattern. Long-term records from systems aged 0--40 years confirm that most residual salt is removed in less than ten years, with effluent-salinity--drain-age relationships stable across decades \parencite{JOHNSTON1993}. Modeling of Haryana sites indicates 15--50 years to reach equilibrium effluent salinity, so drains may yield poor-quality water during much of their service life; drainage-water salinity is more sensitive to leaching fraction than to drain spacing, and raising the leaching fraction from 0.2 to 0.4 halved the equilibration time \parencite{Kelleners2000}. Field observations show drainage-water salinity first rising (initial mobilization of stored salt) then declining with a lag of about two years \parencite{Manjunatha2004}, decreasing to a mean of 2.6~dS~m$^{-1}$ suitable for winter irrigation \parencite{Sharma2006}, and progressively falling across cropping seasons as profile salinity declined \parencite{Jayawardane2001}. A cumulative-drainage relationship governs leaching: significant linear relations link cumulative drainage water to salt leached (R$^2=0.970$) and an exponential relation links drainage/soil depth to remaining/initial salt (R$^2=0.764$), with about 70\% of salt at 1.0~m depth removed by 339~mm of drainage over three years \parencite{Bahçeci2009}. Salt-mass export is strongly conditioned by geometry and geochemistry: drainage-water salinity and cumulative salt load increase with both drain depth and spacing, depth having the larger effect where groundwater quality declines with depth (an 80~m/4~m system exported roughly 36\% more than a 20~m system) \parencite{GRISMER1993}; deeper impervious layers and aquifer layering raise effluent salinity \parencite{KAMRA1991,KAMRA1994}. At watershed scale, region-wide tile drainage increased tile salt loading from 6 to 359~kg~ha$^{-1}$ while reducing groundwater salt loading, giving a modest net increase in total salt yield (1619 to 1651~kg~ha$^{-1}$) and a 12\% decline in aquifer SO$_4$ concentration, but producing episodic loading pulses that doubled or tripled baseline in-stream loads \parencite{Addab2022}. Nile Delta monitoring shows drainage-water salinity varying strongly in space and time, with secondary drains up to four times more saline than collectors \parencite{ElAgha2020}. The input/output salt-mass ratio stabilizes near 0.4, and higher application efficiency (lower leaching fraction) enhances salt buildup \parencite{KARAJEH1994c}.

\subsection{Vertical Redistribution and Ion-Specific Leaching}

Leaching under subsurface drainage is vertically stratified and ionically selective, a structure with direct implications for where and how fast reclamation occurs. Desalinization is strongest at the surface and weakens with depth: upper $>$ middle $>$ lower layer ordering is reported for continuous-head leaching \parencite{Chen2022}, deeper layers may resist or reverse (negative desalination in the 60--80~cm layer even as 0--20~cm exceeds 80\%) \parencite{Han2023}, and reduction magnitude is consistently greater in surface than subsoil layers \parencite{Narjary2024,Tian2024}. Soil texture modulates this: permeable sandy-loam and loam profiles sustain EC reduction while clayey sublayers limit leaching and can show negative EC change at depth \parencite{Qin2025}. Ion mobility follows characteristic sequences. Under heavy rainfall the order was Na$^+$ $>$ Cl$^-$ $>$ SO$_4^{2-}$ $>$ Mg$^{2+}$ \parencite{Yu2016}, and leaching efficiency was highest for Cl and Na (2018 leaching ratios of 91\% and 88\% at 0--20~cm), intermediate and irrigation-sensitive for Mg and SO$_4$, and lowest for Ca, which persists largely as precipitated gypsum ($>$90\% of surface Ca), whereas $>$90\% of Na is free Na$^+$ \parencite{Qian2025}. The chemical composition of drainage water resembles groundwater more than the leached-out salt \parencite{Qian2025}, and drainage significantly reduced chloride and sulfate in the profile \parencite{Sharma2000}.

A frequently observed complication is re-accumulation. Seasonal dynamics follow a three-phase ``wash-out--recovery--accumulation'' pattern, with re-accumulation positively correlated with reproductive growth (r~$=0.83$), Cl$^-$ and Na$^+$ leached downward, SO$_4^{2-}$ retained in the middle layer, and HCO$_3^-$ dominant near the surface; some wide-spacing treatments showed a 5--8~g~kg$^{-1}$ mid-layer increase signaling secondary-salinization risk \parencite{Zhu2025b}. Late-stage re-salinization occurred despite effective spring leaching, more severely under controlled than free drainage \parencite{Dou2022}, and surface salt accumulation reappears during dry, low-drainage periods \parencite{Yu2016,Shi2021b}. The direction of salt movement itself can be reoriented toward the drain, changing from deeper-zone or lateral movement to flow into the open drainage \parencite{Okuda2020}. Salinity distribution around drains is spatially organized: desalinization zones form within about 0.0--0.3~m of the drain \parencite{Lu2019}, the desalination rate is greatest immediately adjacent to the pipe and smallest farthest from it \parencite{Heng2021}, salinity increases with distance from the outlet \parencite{Han2023}, and normalized-salinity analysis places low-salinity zones within about 5~m and high-salinity zones beyond 7--8~m of the drain, with the greatest reduction within 7~m of a deep drain \parencite{Mirlas2003,Benyamini2005}.

\subsection{Leaching Hydraulics and Efficiency Enhancement}

A mechanistic body of work explains why leaching through subsurface drains is often inefficient and how efficiency can be raised, quantifying the non-uniform flow field between drains. Under complete ponding, flow concentrates near the drain: roughly 80\% of flow passes through 20\% of the soil volume, about 40\% of water after 450~h does not contribute to leaching, and desalinizing the full profile required 5400~h versus only 900~h for the top 1~m \parencite{RAO1991}. The flow fractions from successive strips fall steeply from the drain (0.819, 0.134, 0.035, 0.012) \parencite{Youngs2000}. Two families of remedies follow. Progressive/partial flooding leaches the central (poorly flushed) area first: sequential ponding achieved equal desalinization with about 50\% less water \parencite{RAO1991}, and a progressive four-strip schedule used only 1.7\% of the water and 8.3\% of the time of complete flooding (16.4\% water and 25.0\% time for infinitely deep soil), while also cutting evaporation \parencite{Youngs2000}. Low-permeability barriers or surface mulches over the drain redistribute flow toward uniform downward leaching: a barrier reduces wasted near-drain flow, with a 10~m barrier saving 52--82\% of leaching water and a half-extent barrier optimal \parencite{Yu2018}; a low-permeability surface mulch cut leaching timeframe and effluent volume by 64\% and 81\% and raised leaching efficiency, outperforming a buried barrier \parencite{Zhang2022}.

Soil structure and heterogeneity strongly govern efficiency. Macro-pores bypass surface infiltration and weaken the gradient toward the drain, increasing the time to leach to 0.6~m about twelvefold and water use roughly eighteenfold, with the effect worsening at greater macro-pore depth and density \parencite{Xin2016}. Layering can improve leaching relative to uniform soil, yet macro-pores still raise water use by more than an order of magnitude \parencite{Xin2016}. Mass-transfer limitations in dual-domain soils extend the leaching timeframe as trapped salt slowly diffuses from immobile domains; lower mass-transfer coefficient, larger capacity ratio, lower conductivity, wider spacing, and shallower depth all prolong leaching (immobile-domain leaching took 186 days versus 58 for the mobile domain in one case) \parencite{Zhang2024}. Salt movement in these systems is largely advection-dominated \parencite{Yu2018}. Applied-water strategy matters: at equal cumulative leaching water, multiple low-ponding cycles matched fewer high-ponding cycles, desalination increased with repeated cycles (about 64\% per saline-saturation drainage, reaching about 96\% after the fourth cycle), and deeper ponding (300~mm) improved desalting relative to shallow ponding \parencite{Jia2023}; deepening the pipe improved desalination more than widening the rice-husk zone \parencite{Jia2023}. More salt discharged under flood than drip leaching at the same discharge rate, and salt load rose linearly with discharge (R$^2>0.99$) \parencite{Li2022}. In fine-textured soils leaching efficiency is intrinsically low and declines with time, making salt removal a slow process \parencite{MANN1982}. Ditching and deep vertical rotary tillage enhance infiltration and leaching: ditching raised drainage per unit area by 14.65\% and surface (0--20~cm) desalination by 13.40\% where burial depth was under 1.3~m \parencite{Tian2023}, and deep vertical rotary tillage lowered bulk density, enhanced infiltration, and improved desalinization and yield \parencite{Li2026}.

\subsection{Saline and Brackish Water Reuse under Drainage}

A consolidated line of evidence establishes that subsurface drainage enables the safe agricultural reuse of saline and brackish water, provided salt balance is maintained by adequate leaching. Blended saline drainage water up to 9~dS~m$^{-1}$ produced about 90\% wheat yield in semi-arid Indian soils, with cyclic canal/drainage use generally outperforming continuous drainage-water use and long-term use causing only slight soil changes after monsoon leaching \parencite{Sharma2004}. Five-year data show relative wheat yields of 95.8\%, 90.3\%, 83.7\%, and 77.8\% at 6, 9, 12, and 18.8~dS~m$^{-1}$, with monsoon rain and pre-sowing irrigation keeping post-leaching root-zone salinity below 4~dS~m$^{-1}$ and no significant adverse effect on hydraulic conductivity \parencite{Sharma1998}. Conjunctive-use economics favor blending: mixing drainage water (12~dS~m$^{-1}$) with canal water optimized wheat yield at 4~dS~m$^{-1}$ and raised farm income \parencite{AGNIHOTRI1992}. In China, brackish-water leaching under drainage discharged salt exceeding the salt imported in favorable treatments (26.2--153.8~g replacement), with burial depth and irrigation amount controlling replacement and brackish-water salinity having little effect \parencite{Li2023}; saline (6~g~L$^{-1}$) irrigation matched freshwater wheat yields with no annual salt accumulation in the 0--45~cm layer and continued year-on-year salinity decline \parencite{Gao2023}; and drainage kept saline irrigation viable up to 6.25~dS~m$^{-1}$ for maize at 0.8~m depth \parencite{Feng2017}, 4.0--4.4~dS~m$^{-1}$ at 80~cm depth for grain \parencite{Feng2021}, and 8.0~dS~m$^{-1}$ under cut-soiler drainage without extra salt loading \parencite{Neha2022}. Optimal cotton outcomes occurred at brackish salinity of 2--4~g~L$^{-1}$ with 30~m spacing, the preferred depth increasing with salinity \parencite{Li2024}, and maximum acceptable seawater-mixture rates rose with leaching amount (37.27--52.57\% for 80--160~mm) \parencite{Lu2019}. Reuse nonetheless carries sodicity and accumulation risks: cyclic reuse supplying 70\% of summer irrigation caused profile salinity fluctuations and sodic surface soils (SARe~$>$~11), with EC rising from 2 to 6~dS~m$^{-1}$ despite winter-rain leaching \parencite{Mitchell2000}, and drainage-water SAR exceeded effluent SAR during reuse \parencite{Jayawardane2001}. Modeled reuse of poor-quality drain water over subsurface drains caused only marginal deterioration of shallow groundwater \parencite{KARAJEH1994c}. Blending criteria and rice-based reclamation for saline water are codified for the Nile Delta \parencite{Kotb2000}, and drainage/reduced-evaporation/low-EC-water combinations are highlighted as core salinity-control components in a global SICS review \parencite{Cuevas2019}.

\subsection{Integration with Complementary Practices}

Subsurface drainage performs best when integrated with irrigation, tillage, cropping, and hydraulic complements, and several such combinations are well documented. Controlled drainage conserves water and raises yield but trades off leaching: it reduced shallow ditch drainage by about 60\% and buffered salinity via stable deep drainage \parencite{Jia2007}, reduced subsurface drainage from 44.5 to 16.8~cm and the leaching factor to about 0.3 \parencite{Jia2007}, and improved crop water productivity while increasing late-stage re-salinization, with a 50~cm growth-stage depth optimal for sunflower \parencite{Dou2022}. Combined subsurface-pipe-plus-open-ditch systems desalinated effectively (drainage volume ratio about 9:1 favoring ditches), reaching 170\% of a single ditch's efficiency, with optimal pipe depth 90--110~cm, ditch depth 180--210~cm, and collection length 750--850~m \parencite{Yao2024}. Timing drainage to snowmelt and the rainy season kept the water table within 1~m, cut waterlogging by over two-thirds, more than doubled surface salt leaching, and produced residual benefits beyond active drainage periods \parencite{Yu2016}. Autumn/pre-planting irrigation coupled with drainage optimized emergence and raised sunflower yield 9.68--14.70\%, with a recommended 180~mm autumn quota \parencite{Liu2023,Liu2025c}. In cold semi-arid Canada, tall-wheatgrass windbreaks increased snow water equivalent (72.8 versus 25.5~mm) and, combined with drainage, gradually reduced root-zone salinity and multiplied barley yield, while pre-seeding irrigation lifted alfalfa emergence \parencite{Steppuhn2006,Steppuhn2017}. Cut-soiler-based preferential shallow drainage cut salinity 50--60\% over two years and raised pearl-millet and mustard yields, with drainage and salt removal greatest in the rainy season \parencite{Neha2022,Neha2022b}. Gypsum and cover-crop amendment improved soil physical properties and reduced crust strength under saline reuse, though gypsum did not lower EC \parencite{MANN1982,Mitchell2000}, and balanced N--K fertilization (150~kg~N and 50~kg~K~ha$^{-1}$) optimized wheat yield as drainage progressively lowered salinity \parencite{Singh2001b}. Where upward hydraulic pressure or perched conditions dominate, drainage design must adapt: combined drainage with relief wells was the most effective configuration for reducing salinity and lowering the water table within 2--3 years, whereas shallow drainage alone failed under upward pressure \parencite{Benyamini2005,Mirlas2003}. Low-cost alternative technologies---straw-drainage modules performing comparably to conventional pipe systems \parencite{Lu2019} and cut-drain shallow drainage that reduced remaining salt to a third or quarter of the control and raised cotton yield about 18\% \parencite{Okuda2020}---extend the approach to resource-limited settings. Regional coupling of optimized drip irrigation with tailored subsurface drainage reduced district-wide irrigation by 10\% and soil salinity by 15\% while raising yields \parencite{Liu2026}.

\subsection{Modeling, Prediction, and Monitoring Tools}

The quantitative understanding above rests on a mature toolkit of validated models and monitoring methods, which is itself a consolidated finding. Physically based drainage--salinity models reproduce field water tables and salt dynamics well: DRAINMOD and DRAINMOD-S captured water-table fluctuations (NSE up to 0.84) and soil salinity (NSE 0.40--0.92) and were used to derive spacing/depth recommendations \parencite{Chen2025,Tian2023,Tian2023c,Tian2024,Davoodi2019,Li2024}. HYDRUS variants (1D/2D/3D) and their couplings simulated water--salt transport, drainage mass, and crop response accurately across brackish-leaching, forest, tillage, and controlled-drainage settings \parencite{Li2023,Dou2022,Wang2024c,Li2026,Jia2023,Liu2022}, and coupled HYDRUS--EPIC and AquaCrop/DRAINMOD-S frameworks reliably reproduced soil water--salt, yield, and evapotranspiration for evaluating long-term saline-water irrigation \parencite{Feng2019,Feng2021,Li2024}. Salt-and-water-balance models---SaltMod, SahysMod, SWMS-2D, and SALTIRSOIL\_M---predicted root-zone salinity, drain discharge, and effluent salinity closely enough for management use \parencite{Bahçeci2006,Bahçeci2007,Yao2017,Qian2021b,Abdelkrim2021}, and finite-element and analytical solutions for reactive-solute transport, dual-domain mass transfer, and barrier/mulch schemes matched observations and yielded design guidelines \parencite{KAMRA1991,KAMRA1994,Zhang2022,Zhang2024,Youngs2000}. Watershed-scale SWAT-Salt tracked ion loading through tile, groundwater, and runoff pathways and linked local tile behavior to downstream salinity \parencite{Addab2022,Liu2026}, while long-validated empirical effluent-quality relationships remain reliable across decades \parencite{JOHNSTON1993,Kelleners2000}. On the monitoring side, electromagnetic-induction and apparent-EC surveys with geostatistical and time-specific-regression methods enable rapid, nondestructive, depth-resolved assessment of drainage-system efficacy and salinity mapping \parencite{Narjary2024,Song2023,Yu2025,Singh2020d,Mirlas2003}, and emerging IoT-plus-LSTM digital-twin systems achieved high-accuracy soil-EC and water-content forecasting (test R$^2$ up to 0.97) for coordinated irrigation--drainage control \parencite{Qin2025}. Comprehensive field characterizations of irrigation-induced salinity and shallow-groundwater conditions provide the baseline against which these tools are calibrated \parencite{Gates2002}. Collectively, this modeling and monitoring capability is what allows the design and salt-balance principles summarized above to be transferred with confidence to new sites and management scenarios.

9.body:
\subsection{Overview}

A large body of plot-, field-, watershed-, and regional-scale evidence converges on a consistent picture of how agronomic management governs nitrate-nitrogen (NO$_3^-$-N) concentrations and loads in agricultural subsurface (tile) drainage. Across the U.S. Corn Belt and comparable humid systems in Canada, Europe, and Asia, drainage nitrate loss is driven jointly by nitrogen (N) input, crop and rotation choice, the amount and timing of drainage flow, and soil processing of N, so that no single practice is universally decisive but several act in predictable directions \parencite{Dinnes2002}. The strongest and most repeatable levers are the amount of N applied relative to crop demand, the presence of a living or perennial crop to take up residual N during the drainage season, and the fraction of the year in which fields drain. Under conventional maize and maize--soybean systems, flow-weighted NO$_3^-$-N concentrations commonly exceed the 10~mg~L$^{-1}$ drinking-water standard: more than 70\% of site-years in one twelve-state synthesis exceeded it \parencite{Liu2021}, and corn, corn--soybean, and soybean site-years averaged 14.0, 13.5, and 12.1~mg~L$^{-1}$ across a continental database \parencite{Hertzberger2019}.

Several headline findings are well supported. First, drainage nitrate concentration and load increase with N application rate, while yield plateaus near the economic optimum, so that reducing rate toward the agronomic/economic optimum lowers loss with modest yield penalty \parencite{Jaynes2001,Helmers2012b,Zhao2016,Hertzberger2019}. Second, cover crops---especially winter cereal rye---and perennial or diversified rotations are among the most consistent and largest in-field reducers of drainage nitrate, frequently cutting loads by one-third to more than one-half \parencite{Ruffatti2019,OBrien2022,PrezaFontes2021,Daigh2015b,Gentry2025}. Third, shifting N from fall to spring or split/side-dress timing, and using soil-test-based rate programs, reduces loss but with smaller and less consistent effects than rate or cropping-system changes \parencite{Randall2003,Dinnes2002,Jaynes2004}. Fourth, tillage exerts weak and inconsistent control over nitrate loss relative to precipitation, cropping system, and N rate \parencite{RANDALL1995,Weed1996,Randall2001}. Fifth, hydrology---the amount and seasonal timing of drainage, itself governed by precipitation---is the dominant control on interannual variability, with most annual loss occurring in spring \parencite{Randall2003,Bakhsh2010,RANDALL1995}. Finally, mitigation practices can shift N among loss pathways (leaching, N$_2$O, NH$_3$), so evaluating them on a common environmental basis is increasingly emphasized \parencite{PrezaFontes2023,Menegaz2025}. The subsections below synthesize this evidence by management lever.

\subsection{Nitrogen Application Rate and Residual Soil Nitrate}

Drainage nitrate loss rises with N application rate, and this is the most consistently documented in-field relationship. In a 22-ha Iowa production field, yearly nitrate mass loss was significantly greater for a high-N treatment (48~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$) than for medium (35) and low (29) treatments, and concentrations ranged 5--45~mg~N~L$^{-1}$, exceeding the 10~mg~N~L$^{-1}$ maximum contaminant level except under low N during soybean years \parencite{Jaynes2001}. In north-central Iowa, flow-weighted NO$_3^-$-N concentrations increased with rate in both continuous corn and corn--soybean systems, four-year average losses ranged 22--68~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$, and concentrations at recommended rates commonly exceeded 10~mg~L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Helmers2012b}. Early Iowa work found losses rose with rate (50--200~kg~ha$^{-1}$) but not linearly, identifying a single 150~kg~ha$^{-1}$ application as a favorable compromise between loss and yield \parencite{Azevedo1997}. Increasing N above the agronomic optimum yielded diminishing returns: at 168--252~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ in Illinois, maize yield differed little (15.3, 14.5, 15.1, 14.9~Mg~ha$^{-1}$) while flow-weighted concentration was highest at the lowest rate in one comparison and cumulative loads (14.4--22.8~kg~ha$^{-1}$) were statistically similar \parencite{Li2023b}. A ten-percent rate reduction cut Illinois corn yield about 10\% but did not significantly reduce tile nitrate loss in one study, underscoring that rate response varies with site and hydrology \parencite{Gentry2024}.

Regional and database syntheses formalize these patterns. Across the United States and Canada, maize yield followed a quadratic response peaking at 9.3~Mg~ha$^{-1}$ near 193~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$, drainage N losses increased exponentially with rate, and yield-scaled N loss rose sharply once N surplus exceeded about 23~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$---identifying zero N surplus as a practical target \parencite{Zhao2016}. In the MANAGE drain-concentration database, N rate was the strongest predictor of annual NO$_3^-$-N concentration in corn site-years ($R^2=0.59$), with the $>224$~kg~ha$^{-1}$ category highest (19.2~mg~L$^{-1}$) and no yield gain above 150--224~kg~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Hertzberger2019}. A twelve-state analysis confirmed that higher rates raised yield, load, and concentration, that reducing rate mitigated loss but reduced yield, and that the most cost-effective high-rate scenarios also produced the highest losses \parencite{Liu2021}. A meta-analysis of ~1,000 site-years found dissolved N load increased with rate in continuous corn but not corn--soybean \parencite{Christianson2015b}, and a 62-study synthesis reported each unit rise in fertilizer-N rate produced 0.12~kg~NO$_3^-$-N~ha$^{-1}$ loss, with rate explaining 27\% of nitrate-loss variability \parencite{Chatterjee2020b}. Historical measurements likewise scaled loads with rate---e.g., 41, 55, 93, and 180~kg~ha$^{-1}$ at 20--448~kg~ha$^{-1}$ applied \parencite{Ritter2015}.

Residual soil nitrate (RSN) links rate to leaching risk. Across eight states, RSN was minimally affected by rates below the economic optimum N rate (EONR) and increased sharply only above it, following a plateau-quadratic form; at EONR, RSN was lower for at-plant than split applications (42 vs.\ 62~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$), and RSN did not substantially rise until rates exceeded EONR by more than 30~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Bandura2025}. Long-term Minnesota data showed continuous corn accumulated up to 225~kg~ha$^{-1}$ RSN in the profile during dry years, then flushed to drainage in subsequent wet years \parencite{Randall2001}. Excessive fall N carried multi-year legacies: a very high fall rate produced the highest concentrations and losses each year and continued to affect nitrate, residual N, and crop response for more than four years \parencite{Jaynes2015}. Encouragingly, long-term Iowa monitoring showed corn yields rising 144~kg~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$ over three decades at a nearly constant rate while drainage nitrate stayed flat, indicating improved N-use efficiency without added input but no reduction in loss from yield gains alone \parencite{Clifford2023}. Modeling reinforces the rate--loss coupling: annual precipitation had the greatest effect on N leaching but fertilizer rate increased all N pools \parencite{Li2006}, and yield plateaued while nitrate loss accelerated quadratically as spring N rose from 100 to 200~kg~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Malone2007b}. A cropping-system modeling study found the leaching breakpoint occurred above the agronomic optimum N rate in both continuous maize and maize--soybean, with a larger buffer in the rotation \parencite{Pasley2021}.

\subsection{Nitrogen Timing, Placement, and Enhanced-Efficiency Products}

Shifting N from fall to spring or in-season application generally lowers drainage nitrate, though effects are smaller and less consistent than rate or cropping-system effects. In Minnesota corn--soybean rotations, fall-applied N without nitrapyrin consistently produced the highest concentrations and losses; flow-normalized losses ranked fall $>$ split $>$ spring $=$ fall+nitrapyrin, with reductions relative to fall of 18\% (fall+NP), 17\% (spring), and 13\% (split) in one study \parencite{Randall2003} and 10\%, 14\%, and 6\% (spring+NP) in a companion study \parencite{Randall2005}. Fall N applications produced significantly greater tile nitrate concentration and load than spring applications under corn in Illinois \parencite{Gentry2024}, and spring or side-dress N reduced three-year mean flow-weighted concentrations relative to fall N while maintaining yield \parencite{Pittelkow2017}. Cited Minnesota work found spring application raised N-use efficiency by more than 20\% and reduced annual tile nitrate losses by an average of 36\% relative to fall \parencite{Dinnes2002}. Spring application at the guideline rate produced equal or greater corn yield than a higher fall rate with nitrapyrin \parencite{Vetsch2019}, and fall vs.\ spring effects, while real, were often smaller than effects of weather, drainage volume, crop uptake, and rate \parencite{Liu2021}. Correspondingly, database analyses showed out-of-season and pre-plant timings had higher NO$_3^-$-N concentrations (15.8 and 15.7~mg~L$^{-1}$) than at-plant and side-dress timings \parencite{Hertzberger2019}.

Split and side-dress applications give mixed results. Split fertilization reduced NO$_3^-$-N export at lower rates ($<167$~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$) but not at higher rates \parencite{Liu2021}, and split applications were not significantly different from non-split for annual flow-weighted concentration in a continental synthesis \parencite{Hertzberger2019}. Side-dress timing at V2, V6, or V12 growth stages produced no consistent difference in corn yield or nitrate leaching, with V12 identified as about the earliest reliable sensing stage \parencite{Jaynes2013}. In some cases split N raised loss: a midseason split treatment had consistently higher flow-weighted concentration and greater residual soil nitrate than a single postemergence application \parencite{Jaynes2006}. Notably, at EONR, at-plant applications left less residual N than split (42 vs.\ 62~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$) but showed greater in-season loss, whereas split applications shifted N toward postharvest residual---a nuanced tradeoff \parencite{Bandura2025}. Split-applied urea modestly improved water quality relative to fall and spring anhydrous ammonia (10.0 vs.\ 11.7~mg~L$^{-1}$) without raising yield \parencite{Waring2022}. Variable-rate side-dress N informed by remote sensing lowered observed and simulated concentrations, averaging a 40\% reduction over 15 years attributed mainly to 33\% lower input and timing that avoided peak flow, while maintaining maize yield \parencite{Wilson2020b}. Modeling generally found reducing rate from the recommended level lowered loss with small yield penalties, and that timing affected loss more than yield but without a single optimal timing across sites \parencite{Jeong2018}; watershed simulations found reducing rate 20\% with a 40:60 spring/side-dress split cut nitrate load ~18\% without yield loss \parencite{Singh2023}.

Placement and enhanced-efficiency products offer additional but variable benefits. Fertilizer injection and incorporation showed no significant difference from each other in NO$_3^-$-N export in one synthesis \parencite{Liu2021}, while incorporated and injected applications had higher concentrations than surface applications (14.2, 14.1 vs.\ 10.3~mg~L$^{-1}$) in another \parencite{Hertzberger2019}; injection could increase loss when rate was high \parencite{Jeong2018}. A localized-compaction-and-doming applicator produced 16\% lower flow-weighted concentrations than a spoke injector (16.8 vs.\ 20.1~mg~L$^{-1}$) with similar yield \parencite{Bakhsh2010}. Nitrapyrin generally did not reduce drainage nitrate or improve yield \parencite{Vetsch2019,Pittelkow2017}, though including it with fall N reduced losses 10--18\% in Minnesota rotations \parencite{Randall2003,Randall2005}. Polymer-coated urea increased yield 20\% without drainage but did not consistently outperform non-coated urea for yield \parencite{Nash2015,Nash2015b}. A polymer-coated (ESN) product in Minnesota gave the most robust protection, lowering combined N$_2$O, NH$_3$, and NO$_3^-$ losses 47--49\% below urea and split treatments while maintaining yield, with the largest benefit in wet periods \parencite{Menegaz2025}. Summer fertigation with reduced manure rates cut flow-weighted concentration 16\% versus fall injection (47.7 vs.\ 56.8~mg~L$^{-1}$) and annual load (47.1 vs.\ 55.5~kg~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$) via a treatment effect independent of flow, without reducing silage yield \parencite{Gamble2018}. In Missouri, fall anhydrous ammonia with nitrapyrin matched spring anhydrous ammonia for yield and had similar or lower environmental N losses, making it an environmentally acceptable alternative \parencite{Kaur2024,Kaur2024c}. Watershed-scale DSSAT simulations showed the benefit of switching fall-to-spring N for N-use efficiency was largest when cumulative winter rainfall exceeded 500~mm \parencite{Banger2018}.

\subsection{Fertilizer and Manure Source}

Nitrogen source affects drainage nitrate, with manure generally increasing loss relative to inorganic fertilizer at comparable rates. Under continuous corn in Iowa, liquid swine manure produced significantly greater flow-weighted concentrations and losses than UAN with no yield difference, leading authors to recommend corn--soybean rotation for manure management \parencite{Bakhsh2005}. Fall-applied manure with chisel plow gave the highest concentrations, while spring manure with no-till gave lower concentrations, though fall manure also produced the greatest yields \parencite{Bakhsh2007}. In Canada, liquid-hog manure produced higher soil and drainage nitrate than inorganic fertilizer, with more samples exceeding 10~mg~L$^{-1}$, although yields were equivalent \parencite{Elmi2005}; a carrot study found only a small, nonsignificant manure increase and equivalent yield and quality \parencite{Gordon2005}. In Indiana, annually repeated fall-applied liquid swine manure produced the highest nitrate losses among cropped treatments and was the main practice increasing subsurface losses, with time of manure addition controlling loss more than UAN timing/rate or rotation \parencite{HernandezRamirez2011}. Applying swine manure before both corn and soybean, rather than only before corn, raised concentrations and loads \parencite{Dougherty2020}. Fall manure in chisel-plow plots caused more leaching than spring manure in no-till plots \parencite{Rekha2011}. In syntheses, organic fertilizers boosted yields but increased NO$_3^-$-N losses relative to inorganic sources at comparable rates \parencite{Liu2021}, though one meta-analysis found organic sources raised yield without significantly greater dissolved N load \parencite{Christianson2015b}. Among sources, anhydrous ammonia had the highest maximum N$_2$O loss, ammonium nitrate the highest nitrate loss, and urea the highest NH$_3$ loss \parencite{Chatterjee2020b}. Modeling predicted manure gave lower leaching than UAN and spring lower than fall, with fall manure to both crops the highest long-term loss (37.2~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$) \parencite{Malone2007,Malone2007b}.

\subsection{Cover Crops}

Cover crops---particularly winter cereal rye---are among the most consistent and largest in-field reducers of drainage nitrate. In continuous corn in Illinois, split N plus a cereal rye cover crop reduced flow-weighted concentration and load by 35\% and 37\% relative to pre-plant N, and yield-scaled losses by about 33\%, without yield penalty \parencite{PrezaFontes2021}. A companion study found in-season N plus rye decreased leaching 37\% and, on an environmental-damage-cost basis, matched pre-plant N despite shifting losses toward N$_2$O \parencite{PrezaFontes2023}. Cover crops proved more consistent and effective than shifting fertilizer timing: added to spring and fall systems, rye cut flow-weighted concentration 39\% and 38\% and load 40\% and 47\%, reaching 61--64\% reductions in a wet year, and averaged 66\% lower loading than a zero-N control \parencite{Ruffatti2019}. A no-till rye system reduced nitrate concentrations and lowered losses 59\% and 67\% relative to no-till with oat or no-till alone, and altered the load--drainage slope, without reducing yield \parencite{OBrien2022}. Rye after corn significantly reduced mean annual concentration and load under soybean, contributing to a 43\% decrease in cumulative four-year load (from 115 to 65~kg~ha$^{-1}$) \parencite{Gentry2024}. Continuous corn with winter rye had a four-year mean of 5.6~mg~L$^{-1}$, lower than rotation systems, with losses reduced in years of low-to-normal precipitation and high cover-crop growth \parencite{Daigh2015b}. Split N plus rye also gave the lowest dissolved N$_2$O and nitrate loads among fertilized treatments, cutting cumulative N load 56\% versus pre-plant N \parencite{Oliveira2025}. In two Indiana watersheds, cover crops reduced field-scale NO$_3^-$-N losses 58--99\% in most years, pooling to a 65\% reduction, and reduced NH$_4^+$-N losses 45\% \parencite{Vincent2025}.

Cover-crop effectiveness depends on biomass and growing conditions. Biomass and N uptake were low under poor conditions, weakening reductions \parencite{PrezaFontes2021}; an oat cover crop had limited, inconsistent effects, reducing concentration only in a high-biomass year \parencite{OBrien2022}. Cereal rye biomass varied 1.37--5.94~Mg~ha$^{-1}$ with biomass N of 23.5--45.7~kg~ha$^{-1}$, highest under late termination \parencite{Gentry2025}, and growth improved when seeding shifted from post-harvest drilling to broadcasting into standing crop \parencite{Waring2024}. Winter triticale captured 44--164~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ and reduced profile soil nitrate 33--53~kg~ha$^{-1}$, with 60--100\% of capture occurring by mid-May and minimal residual N below 60~cm \parencite{Nance2007}. Rye maintained higher soil water storage than conventional systems and raised evapotranspiration without a yield disadvantage \parencite{Qi2011}. Some studies noted modest corn yield reductions under rye \parencite{Ruffatti2019,Dougherty2020}, but many report no penalty \parencite{OBrien2022,PrezaFontes2021,Gentry2025}. Modeling estimated a winter cover crop reduced tile nitrate yield 34\% at watershed scale \parencite{David2015}, cut predicted loss 31\% under pre-plant UAN without yield loss \parencite{Malone2007b}, and combined with a 20\% rate cut and split N reduced load 32\% \parencite{Singh2023}; a winter rye cover crop could reduce Chesapeake N loading 11.2~kg~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$ \parencite{Woodbury2018}. Under future climate, cover-crop reductions in nitrate loss were projected to persist but may not fully keep pace with rising baseline loss, so cover cropping alone may only partially mitigate climate impacts \parencite{Gupta2023}. At watershed scale, cover crops on 35.5\% of cropland cut subbasin nitrate load 15.5\% and raised median N-use efficiency from 0.49 to 1.02, targeting leaching-dominated areas \parencite{Makhtoumi2025}.

\subsection{Tillage and Residue Management}

Tillage exerts weak and inconsistent control over drainage nitrate loss compared with precipitation, cropping system, and N rate. Over an 11-year Minnesota study, drain flow was higher under no-till than conventional tillage, but nitrate losses were only slightly higher under conventional tillage, and higher no-till flow did not translate into higher nitrate flux \parencite{RANDALL1995}. An 11-year average showed moldboard plow and no-till had similar losses (43 and 41~kg~ha$^{-1}$), and cropping system affected loss far more than tillage \parencite{Randall2001}. In Iowa, tillage only slightly influenced soil nitrate, no-till and ridge-till usually had the lowest drainage concentrations and losses, and crop rotation had greater influence than tillage \parencite{Weed1996}. Tillage had little influence on nitrate losses within a corn--soybean rotation, and continuous corn had higher losses regardless of tillage \parencite{Kanwar1997}. Regional syntheses found conventional and reduced tillage did not differ significantly in drainage N load \parencite{Zhao2016}, though conventional tillage had greater drain discharge while conservation tillage had the highest NO$_3^-$-N concentration (16.1~mg~L$^{-1}$) \parencite{Hertzberger2019}. Modeling likewise found tillage intensity had negligible effects on yield and N$_2$O, with greater intensity slightly reducing drainage flow and N loss \parencite{Wang2016b}.

Where soil-health benefits of reduced tillage accrue, effects on nitrate remain equivocal. No-till improved soil organic matter, aggregate stability, and water-holding capacity in Michigan, but nitrate loads were lower under no-till only in some water years (9.6 vs.\ 18.3~kg~ha$^{-1}$ in one year, higher in another), providing no affirmative evidence that improved soil health reduced leaching in all years, and yields were lower under no-till \parencite{Masri2024}. No-till increased soil organic carbon (0.12~Mg~C~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$), reduced the economic optimum N rate by 28~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$, and raised returns \$172~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Cassman2025}. Conservation treatments raised soil organic matter and aggregate stability over the long term, and cover crops reduced concentration in both tillage systems (e.g., no-till 8.0 vs.\ 4.4~mg~L$^{-1}$ with rye) \parencite{Waring2024}. Residue and stover management had limited effect on nitrate: residue removal had little impact on concentrations or yields \parencite{Dougherty2020}, 50\% stover harvest reduced simulated drainage N loss only 1.1~kg~ha$^{-1}$ while post-emergence N timing reduced it far more \parencite{Malone2019}, and 38\% stover removal did not significantly change water quality but raised sediment and nutrient losses, offsettable with bioenergy crops \parencite{Guo2018}. Continuous corn with residue removal plus a rye cover crop achieved similar or lower nitrate losses than other row crops \parencite{Daigh2015b}. Reduced tillage had little effect on nitrate but increased dissolved-P loss in one Iranian study \parencite{Farahani2020}.

\subsection{Crop Rotation, Diversification, and Perennial Systems}

Cropping system exerts strong control on drainage nitrate, generally exceeding tillage and often rivaling N rate. Continuous corn consistently lost more nitrate than corn--soybean or perennial systems: continuous-corn plots had much larger losses than rotation plots \parencite{Weed1996}, and continuous corn had higher concentrations and losses than corn--soybean, leading authors to judge continuous corn environmentally unsustainable \parencite{Kanwar1997}. Maize--soybean rotations produced greater yield and lower yield-scaled N loss than continuous maize \parencite{Zhao2016}, and rotation shifted the leaching response with a larger buffer above the agronomic optimum \parencite{Pasley2021}. Changing a southeastern Indiana site from continuous corn to corn--soybean, reducing rate, and adding a cover crop lowered flow-weighted concentration from 28 to 8~mg~L$^{-1}$ over 14 years \parencite{Ritter2015}. Rotation changes reduced simulated tile nitrate loads, more on silt loam than sandy loam \parencite{Ahmed2007b}. More diverse rotations delivered large reductions with maintained yield: a three-year rotation (corn--soybean--wheat/double-crop soybean) had a flow-weighted mean of 4.2~mg~L$^{-1}$ versus 8.0 for a control, with average load 4.9 versus 12.5~kg~ha$^{-1}$, and exceeded 10~mg~L$^{-1}$ in only 1\% of samples---demonstrating sustainable intensification \parencite{Gentry2025}.

Perennial and prairie systems are the most effective at limiting drainage nitrate. Prairie systems had concentrations $\le$1.4~mg~L$^{-1}$, 2--195 times below row crops, and yields 1--11\% of row-crop losses \parencite{Daigh2015b}. Restored prairie grass consistently reduced losses with concentrations near rainfall levels \parencite{HernandezRamirez2011}, and perennial alfalfa and CRP plots had 0.3--4~mg~L$^{-1}$ versus 14--40 for row crops, with four-year Iowa losses of 217, 204, and 202~kg~ha$^{-1}$ for corn systems versus 7 and 4 for alfalfa and CRP \parencite{Randall2001}. Perennial plots had the lowest concentration and loading among treatments \parencite{Waring2024}. However, the benefit of perennials can be short-lived on conversion back to row crops: nitrate losses were low only in the first year, then similar to row crops, as residual soil nitrate rose sharply, though former-alfalfa treatments averaged 20\% less drain flow \parencite{Huggins2001}. Simulated novel perennial systems (intermediate wheatgrass, alfalfa) cut tile nitrate loads ~91--92\% in perennial years, and converting all corn--soybean area to alfalfa or wheatgrass reduced watershed losses 78--81\% \parencite{Wilson2023}. Bioenergy perennials also reduce loss: strategically implemented miscanthus cut total N leaching and DIN export 19--26\% under future climate \parencite{Ferin2023}; replacing maize with switchgrass could reduce Chesapeake N loading 18.2--18.9~kg~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$ \parencite{Woodbury2018}; and bioenergy crops on marginal land slightly reduced watershed nutrient losses \parencite{Guo2018}. Diversified rotations, cover crops, perennials, wetlands, bioreactors, and drainage control were all recognized as complementary reducers \parencite{Dinnes2002}. Drainage often raised soybean and corn yield modestly (e.g., +6\% soybean, +8\% corn without N) \parencite{Cassman2025}, though on Fargo clay drainage sometimes reduced yield in dry years, and corn--soybean consistently outyielded continuous corn \parencite{Acharya2019b}. Rotation raised corn yield without changing leaching over six years \parencite{Bakhsh2005}, and organic rotations offered water-timing benefits with sometimes comparable yields \parencite{Logsdon2021}.

\subsection{Drainage Water Management and Controlled Drainage}

Managing the drainage system itself---controlled drainage, subirrigation, and water-table control---reduces drainage volume and nitrate export, though with pathway tradeoffs. Simulated drainage water management reduced annual drain flow ~56\% and drain-flow nitrate loss ~46\% for both continuous corn and corn--soybean, but increased nitrate in surface runoff 3--4~kg~ha$^{-1}$, so net reduction reaching surface water was smaller (32--38\% when combined with reduced rate) \parencite{Ale2012b}. A subirrigation/controlled-drainage system in Ohio lowered deep-profile soil nitrate 46\%, raised corn and soybean yields 19\% and 64\%, and increased N uptake, reducing nitrate available for export \parencite{Fisher1999}. In Missouri, free and managed drainage raised corn yield 15--21\% over no drainage, with managed drainage averaging a further 4\% \parencite{Nash2015}, though managed drainage did not consistently outyield free drainage across years \parencite{Nash2015b}. In Chinese paddy fields, raising the water-table control level in the first week after fertilization reduced total N losses ~9.5\%, mainly via reduced subsurface outflow, making combined controlled irrigation and controlled drainage effective \parencite{He2019b}. Controlled drainage slightly increased corn yield and reduced drainage flow, N loss, and concentration, but increased N$_2$O \parencite{Wang2016b}. Controlled drainage was repeatedly identified as a supplementary mitigation option where in-field management is insufficient \parencite{HernandezRamirez2011}. A caveat: on an existing field system, drainage water management did not reduce load because water moved laterally to an adjacent free-draining system \parencite{David2015}. In arid maize systems, demand-based irrigation with intermittent leaching (threshold ~11~g~kg$^{-1}$ in the 60--100~cm layer) controlled salinity and sustained yield, whereas insufficient leaching cut yield ~86.8\% over 12 years \parencite{Liu2024}.

\subsection{Irrigation and Vegetable Systems}

In irrigated and specialty systems, water application governs nitrate leaching. Sprinkler irrigation produced lower total nitrate and dissolved P than flood and furrow irrigation and, combined with biochar, reduced nitrate concentration, making sprinkler an appropriate water-conserving practice \parencite{Farahani2020}. Greater absolute nitrate loss under furrow than sprinkler reflected lower furrow efficiency \parencite{Hurtado2011}. In drip-irrigated mulched vegetables in Florida, increasing irrigation volume and N above recommendations raised nitrate leaching 32--66\% across crops, and drainage lysimeters and soil cores agreed while ceramic suction cups underestimated leaching, especially under wetter treatments \parencite{Zotarelli2007}. Soil amendments had localized effects: phosphogypsum decreased and refuse lime increased flow-weighted concentrations in some drainage events, without significant differences in total seasonal loss \parencite{Hurtado2011}.

\subsection{Hydrologic and Climatic Controls}

Hydrology---drainage amount and its seasonal timing, driven by precipitation---is the dominant control on interannual and among-plot variability in nitrate loss. Flow-weighted concentrations rose sharply in wet years following dry periods, and losses were lowest in dry years because of low drain flow; the tillage effect was small relative to precipitation \parencite{RANDALL1995}. Minnesota drainage averaged 38~mm during a dry period but 507~mm during a wet period; corn--soybean plots ranged from $<$50~mm and 7--18~mg~L$^{-1}$ in dry years to $>$350~mm and ~35~mg~L$^{-1}$ in a wet year \parencite{Randall2001}. Loss is strongly seasonal: 62\% of annual drainage and ~68--70\% of annual nitrate loss occurred in April--June in Minnesota \parencite{Randall2003}, with 71\% of drainage and 73--77\% of loss in April--June in a companion study \parencite{Randall2005}. In Iowa, ~60\% of tile flow and nitrate leaching occurred March--May \parencite{Bakhsh2007}, and the March--June share of seasonal nitrate loss ranged 25--99\% across years \parencite{Bakhsh2010}. Spatiotemporal variability produced three- to fourfold differences in flow and nitrate loss even under identical management \parencite{Bakhsh2010}. Concentrations were significantly affected by seasonal rainfall and crop conditions \parencite{Bakhsh2005}, nitrate load and drainage were tightly correlated (0.86--0.99) while precipitation correlations were weaker \parencite{Masri2024}, and nitrate loads were linearly correlated with cumulative annual drainage, with management altering the slope \parencite{OBrien2022}. Regionally, relative precipitation had a strong exponential effect: at 148\% of mean precipitation, drainage N loss reached 67.5~kg~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Zhao2016}, and wetter conditions were associated with greater dissolved N losses, though very high rates could overshadow precipitation \parencite{Christianson2015b}. Even with no N applied, nitrate loss remained substantial (~8.3~mg~L$^{-1}$), reflecting background soil N mineralization \parencite{Waring2022}, and non-growing-season mineralization from soil and residues is an important nitrate source \parencite{Gentry2024}. Most drainage and nitrate loss occurred outside the growing season in cool humid systems \parencite{Gordon2005}. Long-term monitoring is essential: short 5--10-year windows can show apparent trends differing from the full record \parencite{Clifford2023}, and tile systems respond within 6--14 months but excessive fall N legacies persist for years \parencite{Jaynes2015}. Weather also modulates enhanced-efficiency products, with ESN lowering N$_2$O chiefly in wetter periods \parencite{Menegaz2025}.

\subsection{Watershed-Scale Programs, Soil Testing, and Nitrogen-Use Efficiency}

Watershed-scale implementation of soil-test-based N programs and prioritized conservation confirms in-field mechanisms at landscape scale. Adopting the late-spring nitrate test (LSNT) across a 400-ha Iowa subbasin reduced drainage nitrate concentration by at least 30\%, associated with reduced fertilizer use, while corn yields matched non-limiting strips \parencite{Jaynes2004}. LSNT at watershed scale produced significant crop responses, with stalk nitrate and SPAD useful indicators, and was effective for reducing loss though it may raise producer risk \parencite{Karlen2005}. Modeling reproduced the LSNT-driven concentration decrease, giving long-term losses of 20.4, 22.2, and 27.3~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ for LSNT, single spring, and single fall applications, with early-season weather explaining 90\% of interannual LSNT-rate variation \parencite{Malone2010}. Soil-test programs (PSNT, LSNT) generally reduced residual soil nitrate and loss versus yield-goal management \parencite{Dinnes2002}, and changing timing and reducing rate through soil testing cut concentrations 30\% in a central Iowa subbasin \parencite{Ritter2015}. Field-level prioritization tools scaled cover crops and grassland conversion, raising watershed N-use efficiency and cutting nitrate load, with conservation tillage least effective \parencite{Makhtoumi2025}. Alternative farming practices reduced drainage 41\% and concentration from 17.2 to 8.2~mg~L$^{-1}$, cutting nitrate losses 59--62\% and staying below 10~mg~L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Oquist2007}. Because 85\% of a Minnesota watershed's nitrate load came from tile drainage, substantial perennialization was needed for a 30--50\% watershed reduction \parencite{Wilson2023}. Adoption remains a constraint: farmers commonly used soil testing but were less familiar with cover crops, controlled drainage, wetlands, and bioreactors, with financial, operational, and social barriers \parencite{David2015}. N-use efficiency and partial factor productivity were highest for side-dress and lowest for very high fall N \parencite{Jaynes2015}, and EONR at an N:corn price ratio of 5.6 is proposed as a sustainability standard balancing profit and environmental risk \parencite{Bandura2025}.

\subsection{Process Understanding, Simulation Models, and Loss-Pathway Tradeoffs}

Plant uptake mechanistically underlies most management effects: plots with plants had lower soil and vadose-water nitrate than barren plots at 30--90~cm, confirming that living cover removes nitrate \parencite{Bosompemaa2021}. Process-based models---RZWQM, DRAINMOD-N II, DNDC, DSSAT, Daisy, APSIM, SWAT, CoupModel---reproduce tile flow, nitrate loss, and yield with satisfactory-to-excellent skill and are used to extrapolate management scenarios \parencite{Jeong2018,Ahmed2007b,Ale2012b,Pasley2021,Motarjemi2021,Conrad2009}. A modified DNDC captured discharge and leaching magnitudes across fertilizer treatments \parencite{Li2006}; Daisy simulated cumulative discharge and nitrate leaching well (NSE 1.00 and 0.87) though daily fluxes and frost/thaw periods were harder \parencite{Motarjemi2021}; DRAINMOD-N II reproduced monthly losses and quantified that ~20\% rate reduction cut losses 10--17\% \parencite{Ale2012b}; and regression equations explained 76--95\% of variation in yield, drainage, concentration, and loss \parencite{Malone2007}. RZWQM applications identified reduced rate and spring/split timing as effective, with 150~kg~ha$^{-1}$ single spring N favorable \parencite{Azevedo1997,Malone2007b}. Climate-adaptation modeling projected declining yield and rising drainage N loss under warming, with an optimum rate near 120~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ balancing economics and environment, and identified cultivar, planting date, tillage, and controlled drainage effects \parencite{Wang2016b}.

Finally, mitigation practices redistribute N among loss pathways, so single-pathway evaluation can mislead. In-season N plus rye cut leaching 37\% but raised annual N$_2$O 26\%, leaving total N loss and environmental-damage cost (519--612~US\$~ha$^{-1}$ among fertilized treatments) statistically unchanged versus pre-plant N \parencite{PrezaFontes2023}. Split N plus rye had the lowest dissolved N$_2$O and nitrate loads, showing stacked practices need not trade one pathway for another \parencite{Oliveira2025}, and no consistent nitrate--N$_2$O tradeoff emerged across tillage and cover-crop systems \parencite{OBrien2022}. ESN protected against N$_2$O, NH$_3$, and NO$_3^-$ simultaneously (47--49\% lower combined loss) while maintaining productivity \parencite{Menegaz2025}. N$_2$O emissions rose nonlinearly with N rate, with a threshold near 120~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Wang2016b}, and each unit of fertilizer-N produced 0.01~kg~N$_2$O-N and 0.12~kg~NO$_3^-$-N loss, with subsurface-drained fields having the highest mean N$_2$O loss \parencite{Chatterjee2020b}. Emerging monitoring links crop performance to losses: early-season NDVI was negatively correlated with cumulative N$_2$O, suggesting remote sensing can flag poorly performing fields \parencite{Fontes2019}. Economic framings increasingly accompany these tradeoffs, from environmental-damage-cost accounting \parencite{PrezaFontes2023} to soil-health return analyses valuing no-till and drainage \parencite{Cassman2025} and switchgrass reference prices for N reduction \parencite{Woodbury2018}. The consistent conclusion across scales is that combining two or more practices---rate optimization, in-season timing, cover crops or diversified rotation, and drainage management---works in harmony to reduce nitrate loss most cost-effectively, since in-field management alone is often insufficient \parencite{Dinnes2002,Singh2023}.

2.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Simulation models are the principal tools for quantifying subsurface (tile) drainage flow and nitrate-N loss and for extrapolating short field experiments across weather, soils, cropping systems, and management. The body of calibration and validation studies synthesised here spans several model families: the process-based DRAINMOD suite (DRAINMOD, DRAINMOD-N, DRAINMOD-N\,II, DRAINMOD 5.1/6.1, and the coupled DRAINMOD-DSSAT); the Root Zone Water Quality Model and its variants (RZWQM, RZWQM2, RZWQM2-P, the RZWQM--DSSAT hybrid, and RZ-SHAW); the watershed-scale Soil and Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) with successive tile-drainage and nitrogen routines, together with the related APEX model; cropping-system and biogeochemical models (APSIM, DNDC, \textit{ecosys}, HERMES, WHCNS, HYDRUS, FLUSH, CERES-Maize, and PNM); field-scale nutrient models such as ADAPT; and a growing set of statistical, regression, and machine-learning surrogates \parencite{Ma2001,David2009,Luo2010,Fang2012,Negm2014,Smith2020}.

Across this literature a consistent picture emerges. After site-specific calibration, models reproduce annual and cumulative subsurface drainage and nitrate-N loss well---frequently within roughly $\pm5$--$20\%$ of observations---and they generally capture the direction and relative magnitude of management effects even when absolute values are imperfect \parencite{Feyereisen2007,Youssef2006,Singh2020b,Dayyani2010}. Performance is strongest at the annual scale, weaker at the monthly scale, and often unsatisfactory at the daily scale \parencite{Liang2018,Ikenberry2017,Marjerison2016,Ojeda2018}. Nitrate-N loss is typically predicted less accurately than drainage flow, and crop nitrogen uptake, residual soil N, and grain yield are recurrent weak points \parencite{Ma2007,Ahuja2007,Malone2007c}. Cold-season snowmelt and freeze--thaw dynamics are a widespread source of error, motivating dedicated model developments for northern climates \parencite{Wang2006c,Dayyani2010b,Yang2007,Salazar2009}. Finally, calibrated models converge on robust management conclusions: nitrogen application rate is the dominant control on nitrate loss, drainage design (depth and spacing) and controlled drainage measurably reduce losses, and winter cover crops reduce nitrate export to tile drains \parencite{Davis2000,Moriasi2013,Fang2012,Malone2017}.

\subsection{The DRAINMOD family of drainage and nitrogen models}

\subsubsection{Hydrology and water-table dynamics}

DRAINMOD and its nitrogen extensions are the most widely tested drainage models, and they simulate water-table depth, drain outflow, and drainage volume with generally good accuracy after calibration. For a Normania clay loam in southern Minnesota, DRAINMOD-N reproduced annual total tile flow with a mean deviation of 1.38\,cm, absolute deviation of 2.07\,cm, and $R^2 = 0.97$ \parencite{Zhao2000}. In North Carolina, simulated and observed daily water-table depths agreed with mean absolute errors of 11.8--13.5\,cm and modelling efficiencies (EF) of 0.70--0.76, and monthly subsurface drainage volumes gave EF of 0.64--0.67 across plots \parencite{Youssef2006}. In Minnesota, DRAINMOD-N\,II achieved Nash--Sutcliffe efficiencies of 0.74--0.99 during calibration and 0.59--0.97 during validation for drainage and nitrate loss, and predicted monthly drainage as ``very good'' during calibration and ``good'' during validation \parencite{Luo2010,Ale2013}. Comparable performance was reported for cold and humid Sweden, where DRAINMOD-N\,II matched monthly drain outflows with no clear over- or under-prediction bias and predicted 12 of 14 significant snow events with snow cover on 82\% of measured days \parencite{Salazar2009}.

The model has also been transferred to newly reclaimed, waterlogged land in India. In Punjab, DRAINMOD-N\,II calibration and validation for drain-flow depth gave RMSE of 8.88 and 9.66, PBIAS of 15.41\% and 19.52\%, and NSE of 0.53 and 0.47 \parencite{Dar2022}, while a DRAINMOD v6.1 application to rice systems reported water-table-depth RMSE of 7.5\,cm (calibration) and 8.16\,cm (validation), with observed and simulated mean water-table depths of 46.13 versus 48.92\,cm and 53.53 versus 55.42\,cm in the two seasons, and non-significant $t$-test differences \parencite{Dar2025}. For no-till fields in Canada, DRAINMOD-N\,II reproduced monthly drain outflows closely, though nitrate losses were somewhat overestimated in the wheat season and underestimated in the soybean season and were poorly predicted in winter and early spring under the available inputs \parencite{Garmdareh2018}.

Cold-climate performance was substantially improved by DRAINMOD 5.1, which added freeze--thaw and snowmelt components. It produced much smaller monthly drainage errors than the original model, which had overestimated outflow in colder months, and it matched the timing and magnitude of drainage events more closely \parencite{Wang2006c}. A subsequent application simulated daily water-table depth ($R^2 = 0.81$--$0.91$), daily drain flow ($R^2 = 0.84$--$0.88$), and monthly drain flow ($R^2 = 0.95$--$0.97$) under both free drainage and sub-irrigation \parencite{Dayyani2010b}.

\subsubsection{Nitrate-N loss and crop yield}

Nitrate simulation with the DRAINMOD suite is generally satisfactory at annual and cumulative scales but weaker at monthly and daily resolution. In Minnesota, annual nitrate loss was reproduced with a mean deviation of 2.16\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ and $R^2 = 0.98$, while corn yield was captured less precisely ($R^2 = 0.69$) \parencite{Zhao2000}. In North Carolina, monthly nitrate-N loss was poorly matched (EF of 0.48 for the calibration plot and near zero or negative for others), but annual losses were well predicted for most plots (yearly EF up to 0.92) and cumulative six-year losses were predicted very accurately, with errors of only $-1.3$ to $4.0\%$ \parencite{Youssef2006}. Indian applications gave nitrogen-load NSE of 0.82--0.84 with cumulative predicted losses of 32.15 and 28.77\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ against observed 33.07 and 26.88\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Dar2022}, and daily nitrate-N loads with NSE and $R^2$ of 0.66--0.70 (calibration) and 0.88 (validation), with mean daily concentrations of 13.64 versus 12.9 and 11.09 versus 11.19\,mg\,L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Dar2025}. DRAINMOD-N reproduced nitrate-N losses in surface runoff with high accuracy but was less accurate for drain-outflow losses, tending to overestimate them during colder months and freeze--thaw periods; it nonetheless gave reasonable predictions across tillage and intercropping treatments not explicitly represented as inputs \parencite{Yang2007}. Two-year total nitrate-N loss errors of 1.32--1.40\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ (calibration) and $-1.64$ to 0.97\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ (validation) were reported for DRAINMOD 5.1 in Canada \parencite{Wang2006c}, and monthly nitrate-N losses were matched with $R^2 = 0.90$--$0.94$ under free drainage and sub-irrigation \parencite{Dayyani2010b}. Simulated annual nitrogen process rates (mineralisation, nitrification, denitrification, volatilisation, deposition) fell within reported literature ranges \parencite{Salazar2009}, and studies in Minnesota consistently indicated a nitrogen budget surplus, implying scope to reduce fertiliser rates and thereby drainage nitrate losses \parencite{Ale2013}.

Recent work has extended the DRAINMOD framework to simultaneous nitrogen and phosphorus dynamics. In Ohio, DRAINMOD simulated mean annual subsurface discharge (28.4 versus measured 27.6\,cm) and monthly nitrate-N load (overall NSE 0.63) with ``good'' accuracy, and monthly dissolved reactive phosphorus (DRP) load reasonably (NSE 0.63), but total phosphorus was simulated poorly (validation NSE $-0.82$; cumulative TP underpredicted by 19.2\%); simulated macropore flow contributed about 4\% of annual discharge \parencite{Askar2025}. By contrast, the deterministic DRAINMOD-GLEAMS model underpredicted atrazine concentrations and loads in tile outflow, especially in spring and fall \parencite{Rudra2005}.

\subsubsection{DRAINMOD-DSSAT and process integration}

Coupling DRAINMOD hydrology with the DSSAT crop models produced a whole-system tool that jointly simulates hydrology, water quality, and crop growth on drained land. In Iowa, DRAINMOD-DSSAT predicted annual subsurface drainage very well (annual NSE 0.95, monthly 0.83; cumulative ten-year drainage underpredicted by only 2.2\%), annual nitrate-N losses across high, medium, and low N treatments (NSE 0.87, 0.93, 0.94), and crop yield with absolute error below 8\% in 27 of 30 plot-years \parencite{Negm2014}. A companion study under free and controlled drainage reported monthly drainage-flow NSE of 0.81 (free) and 0.63 (controlled) and monthly nitrate-N NSE of 0.76 and 0.66, with measured and simulated controlled-drainage reductions of 24.6\% and 27.1\% for drainage and 34.8\% and 33.5\% for nitrate loss \parencite{Negm2017}. In east-central Illinois, DRAINMOD-DSSAT achieved very good calibration and validation of monthly drainage, monthly nitrate loss, and yield, with cumulative nitrate-N loss over 1992--1998 of 262.8\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ against a measured 263.8\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Singh2020b}; a further evaluation at the same site found the model simulated monthly drainage more closely than RZWQM2, matched RZWQM2 for monthly nitrate loss, and achieved excellent crop-yield performance, with both models agreeing that split spring pre-plant plus side-dress N reduces nitrate loss without lowering corn yield \parencite{Singh2022c}.

\subsection{RZWQM, RZWQM2, and root-zone hybrids}

The Root Zone Water Quality Model has been evaluated across many U.S. states and internationally, reproducing water movement, crop growth, nitrogen dynamics, and pesticide behaviour reasonably well, with water balance identified as the key mediator whose errors propagate into plant water stress and nitrogen availability \parencite{Ma2001}. In central Iowa the model reproduced year-to-year variation in tile flow and nitrogen loading ($r^2 = 0.74$ and $0.71$), matched average annual tile flow exactly (13.5\,cm simulated and measured), and predicted soil-water storage and water-table depth within experimental replicate RMSEs; however, N loading was somewhat over-predicted (21.3 versus 17.6\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$) and plant growth, N uptake, and soybean N fixation remained weak \parencite{Ma2007}. RZWQM adequately captured tillage effects, simulating rising flow-weighted nitrate concentration from no-till through moldboard plow, and crop-rotation effects, with lower drain flow and nitrate concentration under corn--soybean than continuous corn \parencite{Ma2007b}. It reproduced yearly drain flow closely (225.3 versus 222.5\,mm; EF 0.99) and nitrate-N loss reasonably (EF 0.8), and indicated that the fraction of applied N lost in drainage rose from 7 to 16\% as N rate increased from 30 to 180\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Bakhsh2004}. A dynamic soil-microbial formulation reproduced ten-year annual nitrogen losses across treatments (e.g., 348 versus 369\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$) and annual tile flow (RMSE 3.4\,cm, ME 0.89); notably, disabling microbial growth gave very similar N-loss results ($r^2 = 0.94$) and improved residual soil nitrate simulation \parencite{Ma2008}.

Two consistent lessons run through the RZWQM literature. First, measured soil hydraulic properties yield much better simulations of soil water, water table, and nitrate loss than default texture-class parameters, with lateral saturated conductivity in the tile layer (101--130\,cm) identified as the single most sensitive parameter for drain flow and nitrate loss \parencite{Ma2007c,Ahuja2007}. Second, integrating field research with even imperfect models improves quantitative understanding and exposes knowledge gaps \parencite{Ahuja2007}. RZWQM2 has since been calibrated with PEST to predict monthly tile flow, nitrate loss, and yield under free and controlled drainage \parencite{Fang2012}, applied to estimate N$_2$O emissions and cover-crop effects over nine years (observed and simulated flow-weighted nitrate-concentration reductions of 60\% and 54\%; Tier III emission factors of 0.032 and 0.041) \parencite{Gillette2018}, and extended to phosphorus as RZWQM2-P, which performed well for subsurface discharge (calibration NSE 0.66, validation 0.76) and satisfactorily for total phosphorus but unsatisfactorily for DRP because of difficulties simulating post-fertilisation and ponded conditions \parencite{BinShokrana2023b}. A hybrid RZWQM--DSSAT model reasonably simulated tile flow and nitrogen load in a subbasin different from the calibration basin \parencite{Singer2011}, and the model performed adequately for soil moisture and tomato yield but less well for evapotranspiration and water-use efficiency in China \parencite{Zhang2022b}. A distinctive cross-site test in Canada calibrated RZWQM at one soil series and validated it at another: calibration total tile flow was within 5\% and yields within 1\% of observations, but at the independent validation site observed daily flows were 41\% higher than simulated and yields were underpredicted \parencite{Ahmed2007}. The RZWQM--SHAW coupling (RZ-SHAW) has been used specifically for cold-season processes, simulating snow depth, winter soil temperature, and winter subsurface drainage satisfactorily and partitioning the 30-year snowmelt and rainfall contributions to winter and spring drainage \parencite{Li2024b,Li2025}.

\subsection{SWAT and watershed-scale tile-drainage routines}

At the watershed scale, SWAT reproduces streamflow well but nitrate flux less accurately, and it has undergone successive tile-drainage and nitrogen-routine developments. In an Illinois watershed, SWAT simulated monthly streamflow with NSE of 0.85 (calibration) and 0.69 (validation) but achieved weaker riverine nitrate performance, underestimating annual nitrate flux by 27\% and overestimating soybean N fixation \parencite{Hu2007}. Incorporating the Hooghoudt steady-state and Kirkham tile equations produced a Modified SWAT with daily and monthly streamflow NSE of 0.76 and 0.85 (calibration), and demonstrated that tile drainage substantially lowers soil water while raising streamflow \parencite{Moriasi2012}. The Revised SWAT partitioned water and nutrient budgets well, matched observed corn yield (8.6\,t\,ha$^{-1}$), and improved monthly tile flow and nitrate-N prediction over standard SWAT by 48\% and 28\%, respectively, avoiding the unrealistic curve-number values otherwise needed to force adequate tile flow \parencite{Moriasi2013,Moriasi2013b}. Updated routines have repeatedly improved fidelity: the SWAT\_HKdc drawdown routine reduced simulated tile-flow flashiness and matched literature-based annual tile flow of 85--172\,mm (8.5--16.2\% of precipitation), with tile nitrate contributing 66\% of total nitrogen export in one year \parencite{Boles2015}; the SWAT-tile routine outperformed SWAT-684 for nitrate load at both field and watershed scales, driven by transport- and lag-related parameters \parencite{Kim2023}; and new tile and nitrogen modules improved daily and monthly streamflow and nitrate-load simulation in Iowa \parencite{Qi2025}. Comparative work in Germany found both curve-number and Green--Ampt infiltration schemes reproduced discharge and tile flow reasonably (daily streamflow NSE 0.50/0.42 and 0.45/0.39; daily tile-flow NSE $\approx$0.33--0.36), with tile flow plus groundwater accounting for 91--98\% of total flow \parencite{Bauwe2016}. Studies also documented representative tile-flow parameter sets \parencite{Guo2018b,Brighenti2023} and the value of soft data and multi-criteria constraints, showing that removing tile drainage drastically reduced simulated streamflow, total nitrogen, and total phosphorus while barely affecting evapotranspiration or yield \parencite{Nelson2018}.

Several watershed applications expose structural and partitioning limitations. Across the Western Lake Erie Basin, three SWAT models performed well at the outlet but varied upstream, tended to over-predict discharge yet under-predict phosphorus in tile drainage and nitrogen in surface runoff, and disagreed on surface-versus-tile partitioning despite an observed subsurface dominance \parencite{Apostel2021}. Modified nitrate algorithms improved daily fluctuations and made monthly nitrate-load performance good to very good, but daily nitrate-concentration NSE remained unsatisfactory, indicating a need for more physically based soil-nitrogen routines \parencite{Ikenberry2017}. A landscape-based SWAT\_LS produced far more behavioural parameter sets than SWAT2005 (1{,}829 versus 377) and added riparian denitrification, estimating present nitrate removal of 67--257\,t\,yr$^{-1}$ (4.3--16.5\%) rising to 367--1{,}322\,t\,yr$^{-1}$ if all riparian zones were undrained \parencite{Hoang2017}. The related APEX/NTT framework simulated annual tile discharge, DRP, and crop yields satisfactorily across 24 edge-of-field sites, though corn yield and particulate phosphorus were less well captured \parencite{Guo2020b}. A multi-model intercomparison in an Illinois watershed found that most models simulated corn and soybean yields within 12\% and annual drainage flux within observations (28.2--31.2 versus 30.2\,cm, except one model at 20.3\,cm), and predicted nitrate-N loss of 21--28 against a measured 28\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$, but denitrification estimates diverged widely (3.8--21\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$), with models that underestimated riverine nitrate tending to predict high denitrification \parencite{David2009}. SWAT calibrated for soil moisture and yield across Iowa, Ohio, and Illinois further showed that long-term drought stress caused much larger yield reductions than aeration stress \parencite{Wang2016}.

\subsection{Cropping-system and biogeochemical models}

A diverse set of cropping-system and biogeochemical models has been calibrated for tile-drained conditions. APSIM has been evaluated repeatedly in the U.S. Midwest: a modified version simulated yearly drain flow within error criteria for 38 of 44 observations and yearly nitrate-N loss for 30 of 45, correctly predicting higher drainage and nitrate loss under continuous corn than corn--soybean and a 38\% cover-crop reduction in nitrate loss over a 41-year simulation \parencite{Malone2007c}. In Indiana, APSIM gave very good corn stover yield (concordance 0.96), satisfactory grain yield (0.85), and acceptable annual drainage (0.63--0.75), performing better in corn--soybean than continuous corn, with daily drainage poor and under-predicted events concentrated in winter and early spring \parencite{Ojeda2018}. A heavily customised APSIM reproduced biomass, yield, soil temperature, soil water, and subsurface drainage in Iowa (biomass RRMSE 0.13--0.14), aided by an improved soil-temperature routine \parencite{Dietzel2016}, and an APSIM-based analysis of water-table dynamics across the United States achieved $R^2 = 0.68$, $d = 0.90$, and RRMSE of 33\%, attributing 91\% of water-table variance to interannual weather versus 7\% to management \parencite{Elli2023}.

Biogeochemical models have been adapted to represent tile hydrology explicitly. A revised DNDC reproduced surface runoff ($R^2 = 0.99$), subsurface drainage ($R^2 = 0.97$, ME 0.96), and nitrate leaching ($R^2 = 0.95$, ME 0.79, though about 20\% underestimated) in China \parencite{Deng2011}, and hydrology-focused developments improved DNDC's soil water storage, daily flow, and nitrogen loading to tile drains, performing at least as well as RZWQM2 while running four times faster and capturing controlled-drainage and sub-irrigation effects \parencite{Smith2020}. The oxygen-explicit \textit{ecosys} model validated well for yield and monthly tile flow in Iowa and identified soil oxygen dynamics as the central mediator linking hydrology, biogeochemistry, and crop growth, showing that tile drainage increased subsurface discharge, inorganic nitrogen loss, and yield---especially in wet years \parencite{Ma2025}. Simpler models also performed strongly: HERMES reproduced annual nitrate loss under cover-crop and no-cover-crop treatments (43.8 versus 44.4 and 17.6 versus 18.9\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$) with cumulative EF above 0.95 \parencite{Malone2017}; the WHCNS model captured 16-year annual drainage ($R^2 = 0.92$, NSE 0.90) but was weaker monthly, and recommended a 100--120\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$ rate to keep drainage nitrate near the drinking-water criterion \parencite{Liang2018}; HYDRUS matched soil water, drainage, and nitrate fluxes across drainage systems in rainfed Iran \parencite{Haghnazari2020}; the dual-permeability FLUSH model replicated nitrate concentration dynamics in Finnish clay soils (NSE 0.68 and 0.45) \parencite{Salo2015}; new tile-drainage algorithms in CERES-Maize described water and nitrate movement reasonably, over-predicting tile flow and nitrogen while under-predicting soil nitrate \parencite{Garrison1999}; and the PNM model, requiring minimal calibration and basic inputs, simulated annual drainage and nitrate leaching better than daily or mid-year totals \parencite{Marjerison2016}. Dedicated cover-crop routines (Grosub and RyeGro) correctly ranked rye biomass production and estimated seasonal cumulative drainage and nitrate loss within about $\pm20\%$ for corn--soybean rotations \parencite{Feyereisen2006,Feyereisen2007}.

\subsection{ADAPT and related field-to-watershed nitrate models}

The ADAPT model has been calibrated extensively in Minnesota for monthly subsurface drainage and nitrate loss. On a Webster clay loam it matched drainage better than nitrate loss, under-predicting drainage in wet months and nitrate in some wet years following drought \parencite{Davis2000}. On commercial fields it achieved calibration NSE of 0.75 (flow) and 0.56 (nitrate) and validation NSE up to 0.81 and 0.73, predicting annual tile flow of 23.2\% of precipitation (measured 25.3\%) and annual nitrate loss of 59.6\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$, equivalent to about 46\% of applied N \parencite{Nangia2008,Nangia2010c}. Watershed-scale ADAPT applications reproduced monthly flow and nitrate trends and were used to isolate climate from management, concluding that nitrate export responded more to an increasingly wet climate than to management, with recent wet conditions raising discharge by 51--70\% relative to historical climates \parencite{Nangia2010,Nangia2010b}. A related spatial-process model explained 67--75\% of the variability in flow and nitrate for two Minnesota watersheds and showed nitrate losses rising with the proportion of tile-drained cropland \parencite{Gowda2007}. The linked DRAIN-WARMF model improved daily and monthly flow over WARMF alone, especially during spring snowmelt (validation daily $R^2 = 0.76$, monthly 0.97), and simulated monthly nitrate loss satisfactorily (validation $R^2 = 0.86$), slightly under-predicting cumulative losses \parencite{Dayyani2010}.

\subsection{Statistical, regression, and machine-learning approaches}

Alongside process models, simpler data-driven tools have proven capable of predicting drainage and nitrate export. Artificial neural networks outperformed deterministic alternatives: radial-basis-function (RBF) networks predicted daily tile outflow and nitrate-N concentration better than feedforward back-propagation (FBP) networks, which underestimated tile flow and were reliable only at monthly resolution \parencite{Sharma2003,Rudra2005}. Regression tools have been developed as efficient alternatives to mechanistic models. In North Carolina a regression tool estimated annual drainage flow (adjusted $R^2 \geq 0.91$) and annual nitrate loss (adjusted $R^2 \geq 0.88$) from readily available variables, reproducing drainage-water-management reductions of 6.3--6.6\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$ close to the DRAINMOD-simulated 7.2--8.6\,kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$, with discrepancies concentrated in extreme-rainfall years \parencite{Negm2016}. In Denmark a parsimonious model linked annual tile flow to annual precipitation, validated across 38 drainage stations, and was more accurate at catchment than field scale while explaining a large share of nitrate-N export \parencite{Frederiksen2023}. Regression equations derived from DRAINMOD-N\,II output in Minnesota related nitrate loss to fertiliser rate and growing-season precipitation ($r^2$ of 0.53--0.56), and predicted that a full shift to split applications would reduce nitrate loads to surface waters by 28\%, rising to 41\% with a 30\% rate reduction \parencite{Wilson2020}. A multivariable regression in Iowa explained much of the observed variation in yields, tile flow, and nitrate loss and confirmed reductions from spring versus fall N application \parencite{Ahuja2007}. Machine-learning surrogates such as Cubist and SVM-RBF matched or exceeded the physically based RZ-SHAW model for winter subsurface drainage while training far faster, although RZ-SHAW remained preferable for extrapolation to future climates because it explicitly represents snow, freezing, and evaporation \parencite{Li2025}.

\subsection{Recurring performance patterns across models}

\subsubsection{Temporal aggregation}

A near-universal finding is that predictive skill improves with temporal aggregation. Annual and cumulative drainage and nitrate loss are simulated well even where monthly and, especially, daily values are not \parencite{Liang2018,Marjerison2016,Malone2017,Ojeda2018}. Monthly nitrate-load simulations routinely meet accepted performance criteria while daily nitrate concentrations do not \parencite{Ikenberry2017,Qi2025}, drainage predictions tend to be slightly more accurate than nitrate predictions at the same scale \parencite{Luo2010}, and statistical models are more accurate at catchment than field scale \parencite{Frederiksen2023}.

\subsubsection{Cold-season and snowmelt limitations}

Errors concentrate in late winter and early spring across model families. Prediction errors were mainly associated with snowmelt and freeze--thaw flow events \parencite{Luo2010}; models under-predicted early-spring drainage during snowmelt or over-predicted drainage in colder months \parencite{Nangia2008,Yang2007}; SWAT-based watershed models had difficulty partitioning flow between subsurface drainage and surface runoff under ice blockage and freeze--thaw \parencite{Nangia2010}; and RyeGro predicted late-winter/early-spring drainage and nitrate loss too early when snow cover was present \parencite{Feyereisen2007}. These limitations directly motivated cold-climate developments such as the freeze--thaw and snowmelt components of DRAINMOD 5.1 and DRAIN-WARMF and the RZ-SHAW coupling \parencite{Wang2006c,Dayyani2010b,Dayyani2010,Li2024b}.

\subsubsection{Parameterization and sensitivity}

Calibration studies repeatedly identify soil-hydraulic and water-flow parameters as controlling tile-drain and nitrate predictions. Measured soil hydraulic properties substantially outperformed default texture-class values \parencite{Ma2007c,Ahuja2007}, with lateral saturated conductivity in and just above the tile layer the most sensitive parameter \parencite{Ma2007c} and Brooks--Corey parameters, saturated conductivity, and drainable porosity most sensitive for tile flow \parencite{Ahmed2007}. Soil field capacity was the single most sensitive parameter for nitrate loss and flow-weighted concentration in HERMES and RZWQM, through its control on denitrification and mineralisation \parencite{Malone2017,Gillette2018}. Precipitation was the most sensitive driver of nitrogen loading, while slope governed sediment and particulate-N loss and fertiliser rate governed nitrate leaching \parencite{Deng2011}; moisture and decay-rate limiters strongly affected nitrate loads in FLUSH \parencite{Salo2015}; and drain-depth and drain-spacing parameters (DDRAIN, SDRAIN) were moderately sensitive for streamflow in SWAT \parencite{Moriasi2012}. Constraining models with soft data and multiple objectives lowered nominal efficiencies but produced more realistic water budgets and yields \parencite{Nelson2018}, and companion soil-moisture calibration was shown to be essential for accurate yield simulation \parencite{Wang2016}. Auxiliary tools such as the modified Checkbook water-balance model further improved in-field soil-moisture-deficit estimates under shallow water tables by adding upward flux and subsurface drainage \parencite{Kolars2019}.

\subsection{Management insights from calibrated models}

\subsubsection{Nitrogen rate and timing}

The most robust cross-model conclusion is that nitrogen application rate is the dominant control on nitrate-N loss and offers far greater reduction potential than altering drainage design. In Minnesota, reducing N application from 200 to 100\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ cut nitrate loss by 67\% (from 33.8 to 11.1\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$) with no predicted yield penalty \parencite{Moriasi2013}, and long-term ADAPT and DRAINMOD simulations reached the same ranking of rate over spacing and depth \parencite{Davis2000,Ale2013}. Simulated nitrate loss rose steeply with N rate in Iowa and Minnesota \parencite{Bakhsh2004,Zhao2000}, and SWAT predicted riverine nitrate reductions of 10--43\% for fertiliser cuts of 10--50\% \parencite{Hu2007}. Beyond total rate, shifting applications from fall to spring or to split timing consistently reduced losses: fall-to-spring switches cut watershed nitrate by 33--35\% \parencite{Gowda2007,Nangia2010}, and spring and split applications reduced N loads by 22--29\% and 37--46\% relative to fall depending on rate and precipitation \parencite{Wilson2020}. Split spring pre-plant plus side-dress N reduced nitrate loss without affecting corn yield in Illinois \parencite{Singh2020b,Singh2022c}, and reducing the N rate from 245 to 140\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ lowered N loss by roughly 40\% with little yield effect \parencite{Fang2012}.

\subsubsection{Drain depth and spacing}

Calibrated models consistently show that shallower drains and wider spacing reduce tile flow and nitrate loss, generally at modest yield cost. Reducing drain depth from 1.5 to 0.9\,m lowered simulated tile flow from 207 to 191\,mm and nitrate loss from 34.0 to 29.4\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$, and widening spacing from 27 to 60\,m reduced flow from 205 to 182\,mm and nitrate loss from 33.8 to 28.4\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Moriasi2013}. Widening spacing from 27 to 40\,m cut nitrate loss from about 45 to 22\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ with only a 7\% yield reduction \parencite{Nangia2010c}, and shallower depths reduced nitrate loss more than wider spacing for fine-textured soils \parencite{Davis2000}. Field and simulation studies in Illinois confirmed that plots with 18.3 and 24.4\,m spacing had lower drain flow and better nitrate retention than 12.2\,m spacing while maintaining or increasing yield \parencite{Hwang2024}. Scenario analyses further indicate optimal designs balancing yield and losses---for example, 1.3\,m depth with 42\,m spacing in India \parencite{Dar2025}, 28\,m spacing for a Minnesota clay loam \parencite{Zhao2000}, 1.2\,m depth with 24\,m spacing for the northern Corn Belt \parencite{Luo2010}, and wider spacing being environmentally preferable for nitrate under warming \parencite{Ziaeifar2025}. Increasing tile depth raised tile flow and, under physically consistent routines, tile nitrate load, whereas an inconsistent routine failed to reproduce this relationship \parencite{Kim2023,Bauwe2016}.

\subsubsection{Controlled drainage, cover crops, and rotation}

Controlled drainage (drainage water management) emerges as an effective nitrate-loss mitigation practice. Simulations show it produces the largest reductions in drainage volume and nitrate loss among alternative practices \parencite{Luo2010,Ma2007b}, cutting tile flow and nitrogen by about 30\% when the outlet is raised in fall and winter \parencite{Ahuja2007} and reducing nitrate loss by 22--39\%, with the effect increasing with rainfall and mostly diverted to lateral seepage and crop uptake \parencite{Fang2012,Negm2017}. DRAINMOD-N\,II projected long-term nitrate-load reductions of 26--35\% across rotations in North Carolina and Illinois, and an efficient approximate method reproduced these effects within 3\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ in most years \parencite{Skaggs2012,Negm2016}. A large multi-site synthesis found controlled drainage had no statistically significant average effect on corn yield (10.62 versus 10.53\,Mg\,ha$^{-1}$), but strongly modulated yield by moisture regime---raising yield 4--14\% in dry seasons and lowering it 4--10\% in wet seasons ($R^2 = 0.67$ against dry/wet stress) \parencite{Youssef2023}. A rainfall-probability-index approach for controlled-drainage and sub-irrigation systems in Louisiana reduced excess and deficit soil water and raised relative maize yields \parencite{COOPER1988}. Winter cover crops were another consistently beneficial practice: simulated winter rye or wheat reduced annual nitrogen loads to tile drains by roughly 19--28\% \parencite{Singer2011}, and HERMES, RZWQM, and APSIM each captured the cover-crop reduction in nitrate loss and flow-weighted concentration \parencite{Malone2017,Gillette2018,Malone2007c}. Crop rotation and tillage effects were also reproduced, with lower drain flow and nitrate loss under corn--soybean than continuous corn \parencite{Ma2007b,Malone2007c}.

\subsection{Climate-change projections from calibrated drainage models}

Once validated, drainage models are widely applied to project climate-change impacts, and their results form a coherent body of evidence on future drainage and nitrate dynamics. In cold and humid regions, warming is projected to increase winter subsurface drainage and shift its timing: under RCP8.5, RZ-SHAW and machine-learning models projected large end-of-century increases in winter drainage and a reversal making February the peak and April the lowest month, accompanied by reduced snow cover, warmer soils, and higher winter evaporation \parencite{Li2025}. In Denmark, ensemble projections indicated higher subsurface drainage on sandy loam and increased autumn/winter rainfall \parencite{Rasmussen2018}, while in France the annual maximum discharge and peak flows changed significantly under all scenarios, with the drainage season shortening and the network saturating earlier under the most severe scenario \parencite{Jeantet2022}. Elsewhere, projections diverge with local precipitation trends: drainage and nitrate fluxes increased under warming in rainfed Iran (growing-season drainage up 25--112\% and nitrate fluxes up 4--125\%) \parencite{Haghnazari2020}, whereas in Michigan drainage discharge and water tables were projected to decline and optimal drain spacing to widen \parencite{Dialameh2025}. Nitrate losses were projected to decrease slightly under both scenarios in one Iranian study, with wider spacing preferred environmentally \parencite{Ziaeifar2025}, while a Minnesota analysis concluded that an increasingly wet climate, combined with higher fertiliser rates and greater corn acreage, was the primary driver of rising nitrate export since the 1970s \parencite{Nangia2010b}.

Climate change is also projected to alter crop production and phosphorus losses in drained systems. Corn yields generally declined under future scenarios, driven mainly by heat and drought stress, with corn more affected than soybean \parencite{Adhikari2020,Dialameh2025,Kanber2019}; delayed planting increased canola yield by 20--64\% and water-use efficiency as a key adaptation \parencite{Asgari2022}. In Ontario, combined warming and CO$_2$ enrichment were projected to raise corn and soybean yields by 14--16\% and, through reduced subsurface-drainage phosphorus transport, to lower total dissolved reactive phosphorus loss by 11\% despite higher surface losses \parencite{Wang2021}, and biochar and groundwater-depth management interacted with climate to affect drainage nitrogen losses in China \parencite{Zhang2022b}. APSIM projected slightly deeper future water tables in the U.S. Midwest, with interannual weather variability accounting for the great majority of water-table variance \parencite{Elli2023}. Purpose-built tools such as the loosely coupled drainage--climate interface integrate climatological data with DRAINMOD-based simulation to support county-level evaluation of drainage depth and spacing under current and future climate \parencite{Adhikari2020}.

6.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Subsurface (tile) drainage is a dominant conduit for nitrate-nitrogen (NO$_3$-N) transport from agricultural land, and the way a drainage system is designed and operated exerts strong, quantifiable control over how much nitrate reaches shallow groundwater, drain water, and downstream surface waters. The evidence assembled here spans continuously monitored field plots, drainage-district and watershed outlets, isotopic and gaseous tracer studies, and process-based simulation across North America, Europe, Asia, Australasia, and irrigated arid systems. Several conclusions are consistently supported. First, nitrate is the overwhelmingly dominant form of nitrogen exported in tile discharge, and tile flow itself typically accounts for a majority of watershed water and NO$_3$-N export \parencite{Williams2015,Grose2022,Hayashi1999}. Second, nitrate loads are governed first and foremost by hydrology: annual and event loads scale tightly with drainage flow, so that the great majority of export occurs during high-flow periods of the non-growing season \parencite{Kladivko2021,ROSSI1991,Bauwe2020,Ikenberry2014,Speir2021}. Third, drainage design (drain spacing and depth) and drainage \emph{management} (controlled drainage, subirrigation, and shallow drainage) modulate both flow volume and nitrate delivery, with managed strategies consistently reducing tile NO$_3$-N losses relative to conventional free drainage, primarily by reducing drainage volume \parencite{Cooke2012,Nash2015d,Elmi2004,Smith2019,Ross2016}. Fourth, edge-of-field practices (riparian buffers, integrated buffer zones, denitrification beds) and in-field management (cover crops, fertilizer rate and timing) provide additional, well-quantified reductions \parencite{Jaynes2014,Carstensen2021,Kaspar2007,Chaplot2004}. This section synthesizes the magnitudes, mechanisms, and management responses that the literature has established, together with the greenhouse-gas trade-offs and the modeling tools used to predict them.

\subsection{Nitrate Concentrations and Loads Under Conventional Free Drainage}

\subsubsection{Concentration magnitudes and drinking-water exceedances}

Tile-drainage water in intensively cropped landscapes carries persistently elevated nitrate. In three Iowa drainage districts, tile water averaged about 13 mg\,L$^{-1}$ NO$_3$-N (range 2.3--21.0), exceeding the 10 mg\,L$^{-1}$ drinking-water standard roughly 85--90\% of the time, with annual loads above 33 kg\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ \parencite{Schilling2012b}; a companion drainage-district study reported outlet concentrations of 0--29.3 mg\,L$^{-1}$ averaging 11.1--14.7 mg\,L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Schilling2013}. Across three tile-drained Iowa catchments, the flow-weighted mean was 17.1 mg\,L$^{-1}$ with 74.2\% of samples exceeding the 10 mg\,L$^{-1}$ maximum contaminant level (MCL) \parencite{Ikenberry2014}. Early Iowa work similarly found flow-weighted NO$_3$-N of 21.0 ppm and individual samples of 2.3--44.2 ppm \parencite{BAKER1975}. Elsewhere, drain-water NO$_3$-N averaged about 10 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$ (nitrate being $>$90\% of total N) in Japan \parencite{Hayashi1999}, 6.6 $\pm$ 4.4 mg\,L$^{-1}$ (2.3--17.3) in eastern Canada \parencite{Smith2011}, and 14.3 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$ (volume-weighted) in a dryland Washington catchment \parencite{Kelley2017}. Very high concentrations characterize some European and irrigated systems: Italian drainage water averaged 128, 216, and 94 mg\,NO$_3$\,L$^{-1}$ across three years, mostly exceeding the 90 mg\,NO$_3$\,L$^{-1}$ surface-water limit \parencite{ROSSI1991}; Polish drainage water usually exceeded 70 mg\,L$^{-1}$ (maximum 98.5) \parencite{Dragon2016}; and Iranian drain effluent ranged from 0.5 to 28.6 mg\,L$^{-1}$ \parencite{DarziNaftchali2017}. In a German tile-drained catchment, 15\% of high-frequency data exceeded the drinking-water limit despite low soil-surface N surplus \parencite{Bauwe2020}. By contrast, a long-term Indiana experiment kept annual NO$_3$-N generally in the 4--9 mg\,L$^{-1}$ range, below 10 mg\,L$^{-1}$, through reduced fertilizer rates and cover cropping \parencite{Kladivko2021,Kladivko2004}. In organic (peat) soils, drainage NO$_3$-N was even higher, averaging 14.8--42.7 mg\,L$^{-1}$ \parencite{MILLER1979}.

\subsubsection{Load magnitudes and variability}

Annual nitrate loads are highly variable and strongly tied to water yield. Iowa tile systems exported roughly 15--40 kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ \parencite{Kelley2017,Schilling2012b,Ikenberry2014}, with early measurements spanning 0--93 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ and losses "highly variable and strongly dependent on the amount of water lost" \parencite{BAKER1975}. An Ontario field exported 39.1 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ over the monitoring period (flow-weighted 7.0 mg\,L$^{-1}$) \parencite{Smith2011}; an Indiana site accumulated 30.7 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{Williams2022}. Italian losses averaged about 214 kg\,NO$_3$\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ ($\approx$50 kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$) \parencite{ROSSI1991}. In Japanese onion systems an estimated 179 kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$ (58\% of applied N) was leached by subsurface drainage \parencite{Hayashi1999}, and Latvian sites lost on average 14.3\% of applied mineral fertilizer N (net 18.90 kg\,ha$^{-1}$), representing an economic loss of about 61 EUR\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ \parencite{Siksnane2017}. Under irrigated arid conditions, nitrate movement below the root zone reached 44--153 kg\,NO$_3$-N\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ depending on crop and fertilization \parencite{ROBBINS1980}. These magnitudes reflect that NO$_3$-N losses to subsurface drains arise chiefly from asynchrony between mineralization and crop uptake and from large pools of potentially mineralizable soil organic N \parencite{Cambardella1999}.

\subsection{Hydrologic Control of Nitrate Export}

\subsubsection{Flow--load coupling and high-flow dominance}

The single most robust finding across scales is that nitrate load is controlled by drainage discharge. Annual nitrate-N losses were strongly related to annual drainage flow, with differences among drain spacings well explained by flow ($p<.001$) \parencite{Kladivko2021}. Annual discharge had a strong positive relationship with annual NO$_3$-N losses at both plot and ditch scales in Germany, leading the authors to conclude that hydrology is the key control on nitrate export \parencite{Bauwe2020}. In Italy, NO$_3$ losses correlated strongly with drainage water ($r=0.93$) but only weakly with precipitation ($r=0.45$) \parencite{ROSSI1991}, and Canadian drain NO$_3$-N concentrations rose with flow rate ($R^2=0.94$) \parencite{Smith2011}. High-flow periods dominate export: more than half of total nitrate export in Iowa catchments occurred during the highest 10\% of daily discharges, and nearly all during the upper half of daily flows \parencite{Ikenberry2014}; in Indiana watersheds, 79--95\% of NO$_3$-N export occurred at the 60th--100th percentile of flows \parencite{Speir2021}; and in Iowa BMP catchments the highest 50\% of flows carried most of the load \parencite{Law2022}. High-frequency monitoring of a drained North Carolina field likewise showed the highest flows accounting for a disproportionate share of nitrate loss \parencite{Liu2020}.

\subsubsection{Seasonality}

Because loads track flow, they are strongly seasonal. About 70\% of annual drainage and nitrate-N load occurred in the November--April fallow season in Indiana \parencite{Kladivko2021,Kladivko2004}. Nitrate losses in Italy peaked in winter and early spring \parencite{ROSSI1991}, and in Iowa most NO$_3$-N losses occurred when crops were absent (November--May) \parencite{Cambardella1999}. Watershed and tile NO$_3$-N loading occurred mainly during larger winter and spring flow events \parencite{Williams2015}, and seasonal patterns extended to loads highest in late spring and fall \parencite{Chaplot2004}. Short high-drainage episodes can dominate: 21 kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$ was lost in only 11 days during snowmelt in Japan \parencite{Hayashi1999}. Concentration seasonality is also common---lowest in summer and highest in fall in Canada \parencite{Smith2011}, and declining to $\leq$5 mg\,L$^{-1}$ in late summer and $<$2 mg\,L$^{-1}$ during extended dry periods in Iowa \parencite{Schilling2013}. In dryland Washington systems, sudden early-winter increases in tile discharge and nitrate followed about 150--160 mm of fall precipitation that saturated the profile and mobilized high-nitrate soil water \parencite{Kelley2017,Keller2008}. Summer concentration spikes during corn years had minimal effect on annual loads because drainage was low then \parencite{Kladivko2021}.

\subsubsection{Concentration--discharge and hysteresis behavior}

High-frequency records reveal characteristic concentration--discharge (C--Q) signatures. Annual NO$_3$-N export is largely chemostatic---yields increase proportionately with runoff \parencite{Speir2021}---and persistent chemostasis (log-log slope magnitude $<$0.04) has been documented in Illinois tile drainage \parencite{Hu2024}. Watershed NO$_3$-N concentration increased with discharge up to the 75th flow percentile and then reached an asymptote in Ohio \parencite{Williams2015}. Seasonally, chemostatic fall and spring behavior can shift to source or transport limitation in winter and summer \parencite{Speir2021}. Storm-event hysteresis is predominantly counterclockwise (anti-clockwise): 77\% of storms in Indiana \parencite{Speir2021}, most of 22 delineated events in North Carolina \parencite{Liu2020}, and $>$75\% of events (annual mean hysteresis index $-0.34$) in a long-term Ohio record \parencite{Liu2022b}, indicating dominance of distal nitrate sources during delivery. Flushing versus dilution was more evenly split, with the flushing index near zero overall and strong seasonality (dilution in winter/summer, flushing in spring/fall) \parencite{Liu2022b,Speir2021}. The Ohio analysis concluded that intensive subsurface drainage, rather than deep groundwater, was the dominant factor shaping nitrate C--Q relationships, and that the Richards--Baker flashiness index tracked the history of drainage installation \parencite{Liu2022b}. At the event scale, total storm nitrate load is controlled by the same factors that dictate tile runoff, which in turn emerges from a threshold in above- and below-tile antecedent storage and soil-water mobilization \parencite{Cain2022}. Storm nitrate yields were strongly driven by event runoff ($R^2$ up to 0.97), while antecedent precipitation was a poor predictor \parencite{Speir2021}. In some settings nitrate load, but not concentration, was related to hydrograph metrics \parencite{Williams2022}, and tile discharge showed threshold behavior once soil-moisture deficit exceeded $-17\pm3$ mm and water table rose above $0.84\pm0.03$ m \parencite{Williams2022}.

\subsubsection{Rapid tile response and preferential flow}

Tile nitrate responds within hours of water inputs. After irrigation, tile nitrate rose sharply within hours, stayed high for days, then dropped abruptly \parencite{Mohanty1998}, and modeling indicated preferential flow intercepted by the drain originated close to and above the tile \parencite{Mohanty1998}. Bypass (macropore) flow was identified as a major mechanism carrying most leached nitrogen in Japan \parencite{Hayashi1999}. In cracking clay soils, summer drought produced nitrate-rich salt crusts that were rapidly mobilized to tile drains by crack flow during the first post-drought rains \parencite{Colombani2016}. Pulses or waves of water with differing NO$_3$-N concentrations moving through the profile were invoked to explain abrupt concentration changes in tile water \parencite{BAKER1975}. Higher-drainage catchments routed more event water (peak event-water fractions of 50\% versus 12\%) and produced larger, earlier solute pulses than low-drainage catchments \parencite{Kennedy2012}.

\subsection{Drainage Design: Drain Spacing and Depth}

Drain spacing and depth are established design controls on both drainage volume and nitrate loss. More closely spaced drains produce greater drainage per unit area and greater nitrate-N loss than wider spacings \parencite{Kladivko2021,Kladivko2004}; in Indiana the 5-m spacing generally yielded the most drainage and the 20-m the least, and relative flow differences among spacings narrowed as the system aged \parencite{Kladivko2004,Kladivko2021}. Importantly, nitrate \emph{concentrations} generally did not vary consistently with spacing---loads differences were driven by flow \parencite{Kladivko2021,Kladivko2004}. Modeling in China showed nitrogen loss increasing with drain spacing (as a second-degree parabola) and linearly with drain depth, and increasing with wider/higher filters \parencite{Tao2021}. Shallower drains were more effective at removing excess water but caused more nitrate loss, and in an Iranian heavy soil spacings $>$40 m could not combat waterlogging; the D0.40L50 configuration was judged best from combined nitrate and water-loss viewpoints \parencite{Hashemi2020}. In salt-affected coastal clay soils, wider spacing enhanced the risk of nitrate leaching and groundwater pollution relative to narrower spacing, which removed nitrate faster and added less to groundwater \parencite{Singh2002,Singh2007,Singh2001c}. A meta-analysis found greater relative reductions in tile discharge under drainage water management with narrower spacing and deeper drains \parencite{Ross2016}.

\subsection{Managed Drainage: Controlled Drainage, Subirrigation, and Shallow Drainage}

Managed drainage strategies consistently reduce nitrate loss compared with conventional free drainage, and the literature establishes both the magnitude and the mechanism.

\subsubsection{Controlled drainage and drainage water management}

In Illinois, annual NO$_3$ loads from managed systems were always less than from free drainage, with flow reductions of 35--96\% and NO$_3$ load reductions of 37--79\% (averaging 61\% across sites) \parencite{Cooke2012}. A synthesis reported drainage water management reducing tile flow by about 46\% and tile NO$_3$-N loads by about 45--48\%, with the caveat that surface runoff can increase and that reductions arise mainly from reduced tile discharge rather than enhanced denitrification \parencite{Ross2016}. Reviews likewise cite controlled drainage reducing NO$_3$-N exports by about 48\% \parencite{Ward2018}. In Missouri claypan soils, managed drainage cut annual water drained by at least 73\% and reduced annual NO$_3$-N loss by 78--85\% in two of four years (e.g., 3.8 versus 25.5 kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$, an 85\% reduction) \parencite{Nash2015d}. In China, controlled drainage significantly reduced groundwater NO$_3$-N, NH$_4$-N, and total phosphorus, with the highest NO$_3$-N reduction reaching 73.2\% \parencite{Cui2016}. Submerged pipe (raised-outlet) operation reduced NO$_3$-N losses by 24\% and 51\% at 20-cm and 40-cm submergence, respectively, relative to free outflow \parencite{Tao2021}.

\subsubsection{Subirrigation and water-table management}

Subirrigation (SI) lowered soil-profile NO$_3$-N by up to 50\% relative to free drainage and reduced drainage-water NO$_3$-N concentrations by 55--73\% \parencite{Elmi2004}. Seasonal mean drainage-effluent NO$_3$-N fell by 74\%, 55\%, and 64\% across three years, and losses dropped from 7.2 to 1.5 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ in one year \parencite{Elmi2004}. SI reduced residual soil nitrate by 19--45\% and produced more rapid nitrate decline with depth, with water-quality benefits accruing even when SI outflow occasionally exceeded free-drainage outflow---implicating enhanced denitrification and reduced loading rather than merely reduced volume \parencite{Elmi2002}. Water-table control that elevated the water table enhanced denitrification and N$_2$O evolution while reducing potential N transport to surface water, denitrification increasing as the water table became shallower \parencite{KLIEWER1995}. Controlled drainage--subirrigation combined with a cover crop reduced five-year tile NO$_3$ concentration and loss by about 47\% (from 9.45 to 4.99 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$ and 102 to 53.6 kg\,N\,ha$^{-1}$) \parencite{Drury2014}.

\subsubsection{Shallow drainage}

Comparative trials show shallow drainage reduces flow and load strongly. In Canada, shallow drainage reduced flow by 38--64\% relative to deep/conventional drainage (controlled drainage reduced flow by 24--37\%), and controlled drainage reduced NO$_3$-N loading by 42.3--58.2\% in two of three years \parencite{Smith2019}. Shallow drainage had the lowest mean nitrate concentration and lowest loading among three systems and exceeded the drinking-water guideline least often \parencite{Smith2011b,Smith2019}, though shallow drainage sometimes reduced yields whereas controlled drainage trended toward higher yields \parencite{Smith2019,Smith2011b}. Locally repairing or blocking a drainage section measurably altered whole-system runoff and, when drains were blocked to simulate controlled drainage, reduced ammonia and bacteria concentrations \parencite{Wesström2015,RITTER1995}.

\subsection{Fertilizer, Cropping, and Cover-Crop Controls on Nitrate Loss}

Nitrogen input rate is a strong lever on nitrate load but has a weaker, less consistent influence on concentration than hydrology does. Modeling showed that changing N application by $-60$\% to $+40$\% did not affect flow or sediment but strongly affected NO$_3$-N loads, with 20/40/60\% input cuts reducing loads by 23/49/94\% \parencite{Chaplot2004}. Fertilizer rate above recommendations pushed weekly drainage NO$_3$-N above 10 mg\,L$^{-1}$ and increased annual losses to 4--64 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ in mineral soils \parencite{MILLER1979}. Yet several long-term records found that reducing fertilizer rate did not immediately reduce nitrate export---precipitation, not fertilizer rate, predicted annual flux at a Washington catchment \parencite{Kelley2017}, and NO$_3$-N temporal patterns were unrelated to fertilizer timing or amount in Iowa \parencite{Cambardella1999}, consistent with soil-N legacy effects that may delay management responses by years to decades \parencite{Bauwe2020}. Latvian data showed no correlation between fertilizer incorporated and N lost \parencite{Siksnane2017}. Incidental loss within 30 days of fertilizer application nonetheless accounted for 52\% of cumulative tile NO$_3$-N load at one Indiana site \parencite{Williams2022}, and fall soil NO$_3$-N was the strongest control on leachate NO$_3$-N ($r^2=0.76$) in North Dakota, where careful N management returned leachate and groundwater NO$_3$-N toward pre-irrigation levels \parencite{Derby2009}.

Nitrogen source and timing generally had modest tile-drainage effects: manure versus urea did not differ in NO$_3$-N losses over multiple years \parencite{Randall2000}, and a large ($\sim$1,000 site-year) analysis found no significant difference in tile N export by application timing or method \parencite{Ward2018}. Isotopic evidence, however, showed pulsed, fertilizer-driven shifts in the NO$_3$ source signature during the corn growing season \parencite{Hu2024}. Cropping system matters strongly: replacing corn--soybean with pasture reduced NO$_3$-N losses to $<$3.5 t\,yr$^{-1}$ \parencite{Chaplot2004}; potato rotations produced the highest losses, and a winter-wheat cover after potato cut flow-weighted nitrate by 30\% to below 10 mg\,L$^{-1}$ \parencite{RITTER1995}; and alfalfa in irrigated rotations contributed substantial residual nitrate to subsequent crops \parencite{ROBBINS1980}. Cover crops are among the best-quantified in-field practices: a rye winter cover crop reduced flow-weighted NO$_3$ concentrations by 59\% and loads by 61\% over four years, chiefly by lowering concentration rather than drainage volume, whereas a permanent gamagrass strip did not significantly reduce losses \parencite{Kaspar2007}. Cover crops reduced tile NO$_3$-N by 21--38\% (concentration) and 14--16\% (load) in Canada while modestly increasing drainage volume \parencite{Drury2014}, and reviews report cover-crop NO$_3$-N reductions of 13--94\% \parencite{Ward2018}. Long-term declines in Indiana NO$_3$-N were attributed to combined reductions in fertilizer rate, rotation and tillage changes, and a cover crop acting as a trap crop \parencite{Kladivko2004,Kladivko2021}.

\subsection{Sources, Transport Pathways, and Transformations}

\subsubsection{Isotopic source apportionment and denitrification}

Dual-isotope studies indicate that drainage nitrate is often derived largely from organic sources (manure and soil organic matter) via nitrification, with denitrification not always dominant. In Ontario, $\delta^{15}$N and $\delta^{18}$O relationships lacked the 2:1 denitrification slope, pointing to organic sources and continued mineralization/nitrification through all seasons \parencite{Smith2011,Smith2011b}. Controlled incubation and field isotope work in Illinois quantified oxygen-isotope exchange during nitrification (exchange fractions of 59\% fertilized versus 0\% non-fertilized) and showed tile-drainage NO$_3$ isotopes were highly responsive to discharge and fertilizer input, with strong $\delta^{15}$N--$\delta^{18}$O coupling in unfertilized periods \parencite{Hu2024}. Where denitrification is active, it is detectable isotopically and geochemically: along Polish groundwater flow paths nitrate declined while sulfate, hardness, excess N$_2$, $\delta^{15}$N (9.9--23.5 per mil), and $\delta^{18}$O (7.9--17.2 per mil) increased \parencite{Dragon2016}. Nested French watersheds showed $^{15}$N enrichment as nitrate declined, and nitrate budgets implied 34--82\% retention dominated by underground (not benthic) denitrification \parencite{Billy2011,Billy2013}. In Iowa drainageways, denitrification in slowly permeable soils could remove essentially all upland groundwater nitrate, providing a natural treatment system \parencite{Schilling2007}, and deep unoxidized loess had the highest denitrification potential, with a large lifetime subsoil NO$_3$ removal capacity \parencite{Cambardella1999}. In arid Idaho, comparison of root-zone leaching with drainage concentrations (mean 3.2 ppm) suggested considerable denitrification and/or dilution at the water-table interface \parencite{ROBBINS1980}.

\subsubsection{Preferential flow, hydrograph sources, and legacy}

Tile discharge is dominated by pre-event water. Three-component separation showed tile flow was 65\% groundwater and 29\% shallow soil water, with precipitation only 6\% \parencite{Williams2022}. At the watershed scale, shallow groundwater supplied 86\% of tile flow, and tile drains carried the highest NO$_3$-N of any source, contributing over 99\% of watershed-scale nitrate export during a monitoring budget \parencite{Grose2022}. Tile drainage acts as a rapid conduit that limits infiltration of solutes into the subsoil: for a 100 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ urea scenario, 39\% of nitrate-N was leached through tile drains rather than reaching the subsoil, and without drainage nitrate could reach the profile bottom and groundwater \parencite{Filipovic2015}. Repair of a Swedish drainage system increased the proportion of infiltrated NO$_3$-N delivered directly to the stream via the tile outlet rather than diffusely via groundwater \parencite{Wesström2015}. Landscape position strongly differentiates groundwater chemistry, with oxic, high-nitrate uplands (8.5--12 mg\,L$^{-1}$) contrasting with reduced, low-nitrate drainageways ($<$0.5 mg\,L$^{-1}$) \parencite{Schilling2007}. Nitrate concentrations were consistently higher in tile drainage water than in water below the root zone and decreased with depth below the drains \parencite{Randall2000}, while subsurface-drainage NO$_3$-N averaged only 20\% of shallow-groundwater concentrations and lagged by 1--2 years \parencite{Derby2009}. Because losses stem from asynchronous mineralization and uptake and large mineralizable-N pools, land-management effects may only become visible after years to decades owing to soil-N legacies \parencite{Cambardella1999,Bauwe2020}. Rapid nitrification of applied ammonium ($>$90\% converted to NO$_3$ within 72 h) provides a ready nitrate supply for leaching \parencite{Hu2024}.

\subsection{Watershed-Scale Integration and Downstream Fate}

Tile drainage is the primary pathway controlling watershed water and nitrogen export in drained landscapes. In Ohio, tile drainage accounted for 56\% of annual watershed discharge (37--74\%) and 44--82\% (mean 62\%) of watershed NO$_3$-N export, with strong month-to-month coupling between tile and watershed loads \parencite{Williams2015}. Tile drains contributed an average of 57\% of streamflow and dominated watershed NO$_3$-N export in Indiana, where three large county drains disproportionately delivered 69\% of watershed nitrate, obscuring field-scale cover-crop effects \parencite{Grose2022}. Nitrate delivery from intensively drained Iowa landscapes can be spatially and within-season homogeneous, with the largest drains contributing 70--87\% of exported water and nitrate \parencite{Coupe2020}. Scaling drainage-district losses to the receiving Boone River suggested drainage districts could account for essentially all river nitrate export in most years, underscoring drainage-district-scale management \parencite{Ikenberry2014}. Downstream, concentrations decline with drainage area---e.g., from tile outlets averaging $\sim$13 mg\,L$^{-1}$ to 8.4, 7.6, 6.4, and 4.2 mg\,L$^{-1}$ progressively downstream in Iowa \parencite{Schilling2012b}---and nitrate is generally lowest in forested sub-catchments and highest in drained croplands \parencite{Billy2013}. Substantial in-network retention (34--82\%) occurs via underground denitrification \parencite{Billy2011}, but in-stream processing immediately below tile outlets offered little potential for meaningful downstream reduction because its timing was poorly matched to seasonal loading \parencite{Schilling2012b}. Achieving downstream targets was estimated to require about a 55\% load reduction at the tile outlet \parencite{Schilling2012b}. Regional analyses show catchment-outlet nitrate flux driven by both discharge and concentration, whereas tile and open-drain fluxes are overwhelmingly discharge-controlled, and groundwater plus farm-scale drainage inputs explained about 60\% of downstream N-load variability \parencite{Goeller2019}. Long-term monitoring can detect improvement: Czech outlet N-NO$_3$ declined from 1,175 to 587 $\mu$g\,L$^{-1}$ between 2004 and 2018 \parencite{Fraindová2022}, and stacked catchment-scale BMPs reduced NO$_3$-N load by 60.3\%, most strongly at high flow \parencite{Law2022}. Paired-watershed designs at drainage-district tile outfalls are feasible, with minimum detectable change around 8\% for the best control--treatment pair and improved detection when restricted to the March--July drainage period \parencite{Schilling2013}.

\subsection{Edge-of-Field and Downstream Mitigation}

Beyond in-field practices, edge-of-field structures provide well-documented nitrate removal. Redirecting tile flow into a riparian buffer diverted 55\% of tile flow (over 18,000 m$^3$) as infiltration, raised the water table $\sim$35 cm, and removed nitrate at 1.01 $\pm$ 0.07 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$\,m$^{-1}$; chloride showed no gradient, confirming denitrification rather than dilution drove the decline \parencite{Jaynes2014}. Integrated buffer zones reduced drainage total-N loss by 29\% and 71\% at two sites (removal of 77 and 237 g\,N\,m$^{-2}$\,yr$^{-1}$, mostly nitrate), with the filterbed accounting for 70--93\% of NO$_3$ removal; hydraulic short-circuiting via overflow pipes and preferential surface paths limited performance \parencite{Carstensen2021}. Denitrification (woodchip) beds followed Michaelis--Menten kinetics (half-saturation constant 7.2 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$, maximum rate 7.1 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$\,h$^{-1}$ at 23.5\,$^{\circ}$C, $Q_{10}=2.8$), with seasonal removal of 0.01--4.64 mg\,N\,L$^{-1}$\,h$^{-1}$ and very low greenhouse-gas surface emissions \parencite{Ghane2015}. Saturated riparian buffers removed nitrate without increasing N$_2$O emissions, keeping total N$_2$O similar to traditional buffers and below corn--soybean rotations, and reducing indirect downstream N$_2$O \parencite{Davis2019}. Reviews also credit vegetated ditches, gypsum amendment, blind/gravel inlets, and BMP combinations (a SWAT analysis suggested $\sim$60\% pollutant-load reductions) with nutrient mitigation, though effects on dissolved phosphorus can differ from nitrate \parencite{Ward2018}. Ephemeral drainageways can themselves function as natural nitrate treatment systems \parencite{Schilling2007}.

\subsection{Greenhouse-Gas Dimensions of Drainage and Nitrate}

Drainage nitrate is coupled to greenhouse-gas fluxes, both directly and indirectly. Drainage water is commonly supersaturated with dissolved N$_2$O, CH$_4$, and CO$_2$; in Japan, indirect emissions via subsurface drainage were 0.747 kg\,N$_2$O-N, 0.115 kg\,CH$_4$-C, and 132 kg\,CO$_2$-C\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$---equivalent to 4.6\%, 58.3\%, and 2.5\% of direct emissions---with N$_2$O-N/NO$_3$-N ratios (0.00076--0.0105) below the IPCC factor of 0.015 \parencite{Sawamoto2003}. Dissolved N$_2$O emissions were largest in upland-rice plots where leached N was greatest (50--67\% of aboveground emissions), and EF5g values exceeded the IPCC default by more than threefold in some systems \parencite{Minamikawa2010}. The presence of a groundwater table increased total N$_2$O emissions via dissolved pathways even under zero fertilizer-N \parencite{Minamikawa2013}. Two-phase co-evolution of [NO$_3^-$], [N$_2$O], and [CO$_2$] during non-rainy periods traced heterotrophic denitrification, yielding drainage-weighted EF5g of 0.0084 \parencite{Minamikawa2015}. In Canada, dissolved N$_2$O losses in tile drainage were substantive---approaching surface N$_2$O emissions---strongly seasonal (up to 62\% in the non-growing season), and declined along tile length, implying an in-drain sink and likely underestimation of the IPCC drainage coefficient \parencite{Burton2024}. A global synthesis of 52 studies found subsurface drains had the highest mean emission factor among artificial waters, that EFs scale downward with increasing NO$_3$ loading, and that the stationary IPCC EF tends to overestimate N$_2$O production \parencite{Webb2021}.

Conversely, installing subsurface drainage sharply reduces methane in paddy systems by improving aeration: drained fields emitted 71\% less CH$_4$ than non-drained fields (3.5 versus 14.8 g\,CH$_4$\,m$^{-2}$), with faster post-drainage Eh recovery and lower pre-submergence soil moisture \parencite{Shiratori2007}. Drainage lowered methane and CO$_2$ production potential by $\sim$40\% through reduced substrate availability and more oxidative conditions, accelerating rice-straw decomposition \parencite{Furukawa2008}. Management and fertilizer choices modulate N$_2$O: polymer-coated urea reduced soil N$_2$O by 47\% and NH$_3$ volatilization by 73\% versus non-coated urea, whereas tile drainage itself did not affect gaseous N loss in a claypan system \parencite{Nash2015e}. Controlled drainage kept cumulative N$_2$O below 1\% of applied N across drainage types \parencite{Smith2019}, and integrated buffer zones acted as net sinks of waterborne N$_2$O while remaining net CH$_4$ sources \parencite{Carstensen2021}. In saline systems, cumulative N$_2$O rose with wider drain spacing and lower organic-fertilizer replacement (up to 17.6 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ under 0\% organic, 20-m spacing) \parencite{Zhai2023}. Adding tile drainage to diversion terraces and grassed waterways reduced CO$_2$ and N$_2$O emissions relative to non-tiled structures by regulating soil water \parencite{Driscoll2025}. Carbon export from drained fields is dominated by inorganic carbon through tiles ($\sim$96\% of total carbon export; $\sim$1107 kg\,ha$^{-1}$ dissolved carbon leached in one year), so reducing water throughput supports carbon retention \parencite{Schilling2023}.

\subsection{Saline/Sodic Reclamation and Specialized Cropping Systems}

In salt-affected soils, drainage design governs both reclamation and nitrogen dynamics. Sustained subsurface drainage lowered soil salinity from 35 to 4 dS\,m$^{-1}$ at 15-m spacing and improved rice yield (6.5 versus 1.8--1.9 Mg\,ha$^{-1}$ at wide spacings), with narrower spacing favoring nitrate removal and reduced groundwater loading, while wider spacing allowed ammonium losses and impaired yields \parencite{Singh2002,Singh2001c}. Total N loss ranged 3.7--23.5 kg\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ across spacings, and subsurface drainage caused roughly 3--20\% loss of applied N through effluent \parencite{Singh2002}. Discontinuing drainage caused nitrite to accumulate 18--34-fold over three years as salinity inhibited nitrification, whereas functioning drainage promoted microbial activity, nitrification, and ammonium storage on the clay complex \parencite{Singh2007}. In subsurface-drained paddies, adding a canola crop improved soil-N utilization and considerably decreased N losses relative to fallow, keeping health-risk quotients below thresholds \parencite{DarziNaftchali2017}. Combining subsurface drainage with organic--inorganic fertilization improved saline soils, crop yield, and quality: smaller spacing and higher organic fertilizer raised alfalfa and sunflower yields and reduced surface-soil salinity (e.g., 36--46\% reduction at close spacing), though higher organic fertilizer could increase surface salt content \parencite{Zhang2024d,Zhai2023}. Optimization identified 10-m spacing with 75\% organic plus 25\% inorganic N as best for sunflower under TOPSIS evaluation \parencite{Zhu2025}. In irrigated sugarcane, a field N balance attributed 43\% of N input to nitrate drainage loss, 13\% to ammonium drainage loss, 9\% to denitrification, and 31\% to uptake \parencite{Matinzadeh2017}, while sewage-irrigation modeling identified denitrification as the main loss pathway with no soil-profile N accumulation up to $\sim$66 mg\,L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Yang2008}.

\subsection{Modeling Nitrate Dynamics Under Drainage}

Process-based models reproduce drainage nitrate concentrations and loads well after site-specific calibration and are established tools for evaluating management scenarios. DRAINMOD-N II and RZWQM2 both satisfactorily predicted tile NO$_3$-N in Iowa, with DRAINMOD-N II performing slightly better (concentration NSE 0.87 versus 0.76; loss NSE 0.84 versus 0.64) and both yielding threshold N rates close to field observations \parencite{Du2017}. RZWQM2 independently tracked NO$_3$-N concentration across N rates and identified an MCL-meeting application rate consistent with long-term data \parencite{Qi2012}. DRAINMOD-N II reproduced daily nitrate variation in an Iranian heavy soil, though it overestimated total seasonal losses, and it ranked drainage designs by combined nitrate/water-loss criteria \parencite{Hashemi2020}. HYDRUS captured how rainfall intensity/timing, initial groundwater depth, soil-N content, and submerged-pipe height controlled N loss \parencite{Tao2021}. A field-scale system-dynamics model matched cumulative nitrate loss (168.71 versus 176.68 kg\,ha$^{-1}$) and identified mineralization and denitrification rate constants as the most sensitive parameters \parencite{Matinzadeh2017}. The parsimonious NIT-DRAIN model simulated hourly nitrate concentrations with high performance (e.g., $R^2=0.83$, NSE 0.83, KGE 0.86), but its accuracy depended heavily on discharge inputs, degrading sharply when simulated rather than observed discharge was used \parencite{Chelil2022}. A FILTER-type model reproduced water tables and drainage nitrate for sewage irrigation \parencite{Yang2008}, and a CHAIN\_2D formulation with bimodal hydraulic functions improved simulation of preferential tile-flow nitrate transport \parencite{Mohanty1998}. At larger scales, SWAT estimated flow, NO$_3$-N, and sediment with monthly mean errors under 10\% and quantified farming-practice effects \parencite{Chaplot2004}, and a mechanistic denitrification-bed model coupling Forchheimer, Michaelis--Menten, and Arrhenius terms predicted outflow nitrate satisfactorily ($R^2=0.86$, NSE 0.85) \parencite{Ghane2015}. Groundwater flow-and-transport modeling showed that drainageway denitrification at 0.015 day$^{-1}$ could reduce upland nitrate to below detection at the catchment outlet, and that adding tile drainage would increase catchment drainage by 22.5 mm\,yr$^{-1}$ and deliver an additional 12.3 kg\,yr$^{-1}$ of nitrate \parencite{Schilling2007}.

18.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Across a broad range of climates, soils, and crops, the evidence consistently establishes that agricultural subsurface drainage and its managed variants (controlled drainage, subirrigation, and drainage water recycling) raise crop yields relative to undrained or poorly drained conditions, primarily by relieving excess-water (waterlogging) stress early in the season and, where water tables can be managed, by alleviating water-deficiency stress later on. The strongest, most transferable conclusions come from long-term field experiments, multi-site and meta-analytic syntheses, and calibrated simulation studies. A United States meta-analysis of 154 pairwise comparisons found that controlled drainage and subirrigation increased crop yield by an average of \(8.0\%\) relative to conventional free drainage \parencite{Singh2022b}, while a combined experiment-and-producer study reported soybean yield gains of \(275\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(8\%\)) in experiments and \(157\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(4\%\)) in producer fields under artificial drainage \parencite{Mourtzinis2021}. The authors of that study framed subsurface drainage explicitly as an effective practice for increasing yields in environments prone to excess water early in the season \parencite{Mourtzinis2021}.

Several headline findings recur throughout the literature. First, yield benefits are typically larger in wet years and on poorly drained, fine-textured or naturally waterlogged soils, and they can be modest, absent, or even negative where existing drainage is already adequate or where over-drainage induces drought stress \parencite{JORJANI1991,Wiersma2010,Cordeiro2015,Kladivko2005}. Second, the response depends strongly on drain spacing, depth, and design, which mediate how quickly the water table is lowered \parencite{HARDJOAMIDJOJO1982,Skaggs2006,Rui2024,Acharya2019}. Third, in arid and semi-arid irrigated systems the yield benefit is often inseparable from salinity control and improved irrigation water-use efficiency \parencite{ELMOWELHI1988,Mathew2001,Yang2022,Liu2025}. Fourth, controlled drainage and subirrigation can retain water for the crop and deliver additional gains when precipitation is limiting \parencite{Kaur2021,Gunn2018,Gunn2019,Nelson2011}. This section synthesises these consolidated findings, the mechanisms behind them, and the quantified outcomes reported across the source studies.

\subsection{Magnitude of yield gains from subsurface drainage}

The clearest body of evidence concerns direct comparisons of drained versus undrained (or surface-drained/control) systems, which repeatedly document substantial yield increases. In a long-term Indiana experiment on Clermont soil, corn yields remained stable in the undrained control but progressively increased in all three drained treatments across 24 corn years, with the drainage effect becoming more pronounced in the last ten corn years than in the first ten; soybean yields, by contrast, were not significantly affected by drainage spacing \parencite{Rui2024}. A companion long-term Indiana dataset found whole-plot average corn yields of 9.8, 9.7, 9.5, and \(9.2\ \mathrm{Mg\ ha^{-1}}\) for the 5-, 10-, and 20-m spacings and the nondrained control, respectively, and concluded that drainage improvements are a long-term investment that may not yield benefits in every year \parencite{Kladivko2005}.

The largest relative gains are reported in heavily stressed environments. In the Nile Delta, subsurface drainage at 20-m spacing and 1.5-m depth doubled cotton and rice yields and increased wheat and clover yields by \(50\%\) over ten years relative to no subsurface drainage \parencite{ELMOWELHI1988}. In the acid saline "kari" soils of Kuttanad, India, subsurface drainage up to 30-m spacing raised average grain yield by \(1.36\ \mathrm{t\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(61\%\)) over the control, with grain yield and 100-grain weight significantly superior \parencite{Mathew2001}. In waterlogged saline clay soils of Bangladesh, drainage increased sunflower achene yield by \(30\textrm{--}64\%\) across drained treatments in two experiments, while in a third experiment the individual surface-drainage (SD) and subsoil-drainage (SSD) treatments increased yield by \(91\%\) and \(81\%\), respectively, and the combined subsoil-plus-surface drainage (SSD+SD) added only a further \(24\%\) and \(31\%\) increase relative to SD and SSD; an earlier companion study reported up to a \(95\%\) increase \parencite{Islam2025,Islam2022}. In Alberta, a shallow subsurface system on land out of production for 20 years raised barley grain yield from \(1310\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\) in 1977 to \(3900\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\) in 1978, with plant stand up \(43\%\) \parencite{BENNETT1982}.

More moderate but statistically robust gains dominate temperate rainfed systems. Soybean yield was \(8\%\) higher under artificial drainage in experiments and \(4\%\) higher in producer fields across the United States \parencite{Mourtzinis2021}, while shallow drainage in North Carolina increased corn yield by \(0.4\ \mathrm{Mg\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(4\%\), or \(6.6\%\) excluding an anomalous 2016 season) and soybean yield by \(0.46\ \mathrm{Mg\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(14.3\%\)) relative to free drainage \parencite{Watkins2024}. In Iranian subsurface-drained paddies, all drainage plots exceeded the control grain yield by \(13.4\textrm{--}31.3\%\) for rice \parencite{DarziNaftchali2017b}, and separate rice experiments reported grain yields \(22\textrm{--}66\%\) higher (2011) and \(32\textrm{--}70\%\) higher (2012) than the control, with the Bilevel treatment producing the highest yields \parencite{DarziNaftchali2014}. In China, blind-ditch and pipe drainage raised rice yield by \(8.0\%\) and \(6.0\%\) over the control, with the 3-m blind-ditch spacing producing the maximum yield of \(7.501\ \mathrm{Mg\ ha^{-1}}\) \parencite{Qi2025b}. Canola yield in Iran improved by roughly \(60\%\) under subsurface drainage relative to surface drainage, with mean grain yield higher by 697 and \(952\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\) in the two seasons \parencite{Asgari2021}. Cotton, sunflower, and potato experiments likewise reported increased actual yields under subsurface drainage \parencite{Yang2022,Zhang2024c,SerranoCoronel2018}.

\subsection{Controlled drainage, subirrigation, and drainage water recycling}

Managed drainage practices deliver yield benefits beyond free drainage when they retain water for crop use during dry periods. The U.S. meta-analysis quantified an \(8.0\%\) average yield increase from controlled drainage and subirrigation over conventional free drainage across 154 comparisons, drawn from 30 peer-reviewed articles and combined with fuzzy-logic mapping to identify suitable areas, including regions outside the well-studied Midwest \parencite{Singh2022b}. In Missouri, free drainage and drainage water recycling increased corn grain yield by \(20\%\) and \(26\%\), respectively, over a nondrained control averaged over 2015--2020, with recycling gains reaching \(17\%\), \(34\%\), and \(48\%\) in individual years and drainage water recycling positioned as a means to avoid drought losses in tile-drained landscapes \parencite{Kaur2021}. In North Carolina, drainage water recycling increased corn and soybean yields in all study years by \(0.52\ \mathrm{Mg\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(8\%\)) and \(0.45\ \mathrm{Mg\ ha^{-1}}\) (\(17\%\)); the soybean response was significant, subirrigation raised the groundwater table by an average of 15 cm, and the reservoir met irrigation demand in three of four seasons \parencite{Moursi2024}.

Subirrigation performance is strongly site-dependent, governed by lateral hydraulic conductivity. Across three Ohio sites, subirrigation raised the water table effectively at Fulton and Van Wert (growing-season water-table differences of 17--52 cm) and improved relative yield to \(87.3\textrm{--}93.2\%\) and \(80.4\textrm{--}90.8\%\), but had almost no hydrologic or yield effect at Defiance because of very low lateral hydraulic conductivity, where yields stayed near \(63\textrm{--}64\%\) \parencite{Gunn2018,Gunn2019}. Historic maize relative-yield responses to subirrigation ranged from essentially none at Defiance to \(26.5\%\) at Fulton, and simulations projected that subirrigation would increasingly outperform free drainage under future climate, raising its relative-yield advantage by \(2\%\), \(10\%\), and \(8\%\) at the three sites \parencite{Gunn2018}. In Minnesota, controlled drainage and subirrigation kept soil water content in optimal ranges, with the largest benefits observed in both a wet year (2013) and a dry year (2017), and subirrigation producing yields above the county average in a dry year \parencite{Niaghi2019}. In claypan-soil soybean systems, drainage plus subirrigation improved yield at both 6.1- and 12.2-m spacings with no difference between spacings, reduced interannual yield variability relative to drainage-only or nondrained controls, and allowed earlier planting where the nondrained control was delayed up to 17 days \parencite{Nelson2011}. Combining drainage water management with foliar pyraclostrobin produced synergistic soybean yield increases of up to \(1070\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\), with drainage-only or drainage-plus-subirrigation gains of \(18\textrm{--}22\%\) over the nondrained, nonfungicide control \parencite{Nelson2011b}. In Uzbekistan, controlled drainage managed the groundwater table so as to reduce water stress between irrigation events and increase grain yields, supporting the view that controlled drainage delivers good short-term returns through reduced water-deficiency stress \parencite{Dukhovny2018}.

\subsection{Drain spacing, depth, and system design}

Yield response is mediated by drainage intensity, set by spacing and depth. In the long-term Indiana experiment the narrowest 5-m spacing produced the highest average corn yield, followed by 10-m and 20-m spacings, with the undrained control lowest; narrower spacing also reduced the negative effect of post-planting rainfall on corn \parencite{Rui2024}. Distance-from-drain effects were more common at 20-m than at 10- or 5-m spacings, generally showing greater growth and yield near the tile \parencite{Kladivko2005}. On a Missouri claypan, the highest soybean yield occurred directly above the 6.1-m drainage tile line, all tile spacings and distances outyielded the nondrained control, and spatial yield variability was structured by distance from the tile line, suggesting narrower spacing to reduce variability in low-yielding environments \parencite{Nelson2017}.

Optimal design is soil- and rainfall-specific. Modelling for a Portsmouth sandy loam indicated that a maximum long-term relative corn yield of \(80\%\) required drain spacing of 40 m or less under good surface drainage but 30 m or less under poor surface drainage; at 100-m spacing, 25-year average relative yield fell to \(47\%\) (poor) versus \(61\%\) (good surface drainage) \parencite{HARDJOAMIDJOJO1982}. Across four soils, drain spacings that maximised returns ranged from 19 m (Normania) to 40 m (Portsmouth), with maximum returns near a drainage intensity of \(1.1\textrm{--}1.2\ \mathrm{cm\ day^{-1}}\) for most soils \parencite{Skaggs2006}. In Iowa, DRAINMOD predicted significant yield gains over undrained conditions for both Okoboji and Canisteo soils; Canisteo yields improved with closer spacing whereas Okoboji's largest improvement occurred at 18.3-m spacing \parencite{Arora2024}. In Minnesota, relative crop yield rose from \(43\%\) undrained to \(99\%\) at 23-m spacing and 135-cm depth, with profitability generally increasing with drain depth and spacing \parencite{Sands2015}. In the Canadian Prairies, however, over-drainage was a risk: a 15-m spacing could promote over-drainage and yield reductions, 40 m emerged as a threshold avoiding negative impacts on the sandy loam studied, and 60- and 90-m spacings were ineffective in wetter years \parencite{Cordeiro2015}. In North Dakota, spacing and depth effects depended on rainfall and crop: narrower (9 m) spacing gave the highest corn and soybean yields in the wet year and 3-year average, wider (15 m) spacing suited the dry year and sugarbeet, and a 1.2-m drain depth outperformed 0.9 m for sugarbeet \parencite{Acharya2019}. In China, deeper pipe burial and closer spacing generally raised yield, desalination, and irrigation water productivity, with 1.4-m burial identified as optimal in one region and D2 (45 cm) or a 45--60 cm range optimal under greenhouse conditions in another \parencite{Liu2025,Yang2022,Guo2025,Hou2018}. For sugarcane, a system able to maintain the water table between 0.3 and 1.5 m was concluded to increase cane and sugar yield \parencite{CAMP1982}.

\subsection{Mechanisms: waterlogging relief, aeration, roots, and physiology}

The proximate mechanism is relief of excess-water stress and restoration of root-zone aeration. Excess water quickly removed from the root zone provided sufficient aeration for barley growth \parencite{BENNETT1982}, and mid-season subsurface drainage lowered the water table in the rice root zone and improved aeration relative to the control \parencite{DarziNaftchali2014}. Growth-stage sensitivity matters: for cotton, the flowering and boll-forming stage was most waterlogging-sensitive and the budding stage most waterlogging-prone, and surface waterlogging caused greater yield loss than subsurface waterlogging, with combined stress less than additive \parencite{Qian2021}.

Improved aeration propagates through crop physiology and growth. Subsurface drainage increased cotton seedling emergence, root vigour, chlorophyll, plant height, leaf area, dry matter, and reproductive-organ weight, and path analysis identified seedling emergence rate as the largest direct contributor to yield—so drainage raised yield mainly by improving emergence through reduced soil salinity \parencite{Yang2022}. In sunflower, drainage decreased leaf \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) and \(\mathrm{Na^{+}/K^{+}}\) ratio and raised leaf \(\mathrm{K^{+}}\), stomatal conductance, and chlorophyll—all positively associated with achene yield—with early gains driven by waterlogging/salinity relief and late-season gains by improved water availability and solute potential \parencite{Islam2022}. In canola, subsurface drainage raised leaf area index, total dry weight, relative growth rate, and crop growth rate \parencite{Asgari2021}. In rice, subsurface drainage shifted biomass partitioning toward panicles, increased N uptake and translocation to reproductive tissue, and improved nutrient-use efficiency, raising NUE for grain by \(16.1\%\) and KUE by \(16\%\) \parencite{DarziNaftchali2022b}. Path analysis of blind-ditch rice systems attributed the largest direct contribution to yield to root biomass \parencite{Qi2025b}. A wheat root-trait review noted that dense adventitious roots, thin sparse roots, and aerenchyma-forming laterals are superior under waterlogging combined with other stresses, and that integrated subsurface tile-drained systems improve rhizosphere water status in poorly drained clay soils, with drainage among the agronomic strategies that relieve multiple-stress yield loss \parencite{Chen2025b}. Greenhouse and field work on root rot showed that watering intensity increased root rot and that no-drainage and shallow-drainage plots had greater root rot than conventional drainage in a wet year \parencite{Han2017}.

Drainage also raises crop water use where growth improves. In North Dakota, subsurface drainage increased peak growing-season evapotranspiration (e.g., 239 vs \(183\ \mathrm{mm}\) for July--August corn) and seasonal ET by \(16\%\) (2009) and \(7\%\) (2010), with higher crop coefficients in the drained field \parencite{Rijal2012}. Regionally, tile drainage slightly decreased annual cumulative ET (mainly early season) but drained areas showed slightly higher peak-season ET and generally higher LAI, indicating better crop growth \parencite{Yang2017,Yang2022}. Boundary-line analysis of cranberry fields identified an optimum water-table drawdown rate near \(1.25\ \mathrm{cm\ h^{-1}}\), with yield declining when drawdown deviated from that optimum, and soil hydrodynamic properties explaining nearly half the variance in drawdown and yield \parencite{Bulot2017}.

\subsection{Salinity control and reclamation in irrigated arid systems}

In arid and semi-arid irrigated agriculture, subsurface drainage improves yield largely by leaching salts and reclaiming soils. In the Nile Delta, one year of drainage reduced soil salinity in the top 1.5 m from 5.3 to \(2.2\ \mathrm{dS\ m^{-1}}\) without water beyond normal irrigation and rainfall \parencite{ELMOWELHI1988}. In Kuttanad, drainage leached sodium, calcium, and magnesium, drastically reduced chloride, lowered acidity, and homogenised soil chemistry within two years, alleviating the acidity, salinity, and cation/anion toxicity that had depressed rice yields \parencite{Mathew2001}. In Alberta, a shallow system controlled the water table (receding to pre-irrigation levels within 48 h) and reduced ECe and SAR mainly in the upper 0--0.3 m, removing \(2.2\ \mathrm{t\ ha^{-1}}\) of salt from the surface layer \parencite{BENNETT1982}. In Chinese saline systems, subsurface drainage improved root-zone desalination (\(13.54\%\) higher for the same irrigation), improved moisture uniformity, and raised sunflower and cotton yields, with deeper burial and closer spacing enhancing desalination \parencite{Zhang2024c,Yang2022,Liu2025,Guo2025}. Crop-straw drains (maize stalk, rice straw) removed salt and water more efficiently than perforated plastic pipes, improved desalination and root development, and improved maize grain yield—suggesting a low-cost organic substitute during initial reclamation of saline coastal soils \parencite{Lu2018}. Salinity management also has limits and risks: deficit irrigation in South Africa salinised upper soil layers, root-zone ECe exceeded the sugarcane threshold at all sites (implying \(>25\%\) yield reductions), and observed water-table depths and drainage discharges fell short of design, underscoring that installation alone is insufficient without maintenance \parencite{Malota2016}. Modelling for cotton showed that supplemental irrigation of \(\geq 100\ \mathrm{mm\ yr^{-1}}\) kept the root-zone salt balance favourable under a 1.2-m water-table control, whereas lower irrigation allowed salinity to accumulate \parencite{Li2018}. In Pakistan, water quality governed outcomes: good canal water maintained relative transpiration above 0.90 for wheat and cotton, whereas poor tubewell water dropped it as low as 0.60, with wheat more affected than cotton \parencite{Sarwar2001}.

\subsection{Water-use efficiency and irrigation water savings}

Controlled drainage repeatedly improves irrigation water-use efficiency and saves water without sacrificing yield. In Egypt, simple management concepts for existing subsurface systems, evaluated with DRAINMOD-S, improved irrigation water-use efficiency by \(15\textrm{--}20\%\) without yield reduction; crop-specific WUE gains were about \(16\%\) (wheat), \(23\%\) (maize), \(15\%\) (alfalfa), and \(20\%\) (cotton), with potential water savings of hundreds to over a thousand \(\mathrm{m^{3}\ ha^{-1}}\) per crop and roughly \(0.4\ \mathrm{BCM}\) over a two-year rotation in the Western Delta \parencite{Wahba2005}. A separate Egyptian study reported that controlled drainage cut applied irrigation water by \(15.6\%\) (wheat) and \(14\%\) (maize), raised water productivity to 1.03 and \(1.33\ \mathrm{kg\ m^{-3}}\), increased average maize relative yield by about \(6\%\), reduced drainage discharge by \(24.5\%\), and could save roughly \(1.33\ \mathrm{BCM}\) for El-Beheira governorate without affecting soil salinity \parencite{Mahmoud2021}. Modified drainage layouts reduced applied irrigation water by about \(30\textrm{--}40\%\) while restricted outflow had no adverse effect on soil salinity or crop yield for rice, maize, and cotton \parencite{ELATFY1991}. Controlled-water-table wheat in Egypt achieved the highest crop water productivity and large water savings at \(50\%\) available-water depletion with a 60-cm water table (identified as the best management practice) \parencite{ElHassanin2022}. In Iranian paddies, subsurface drainage raised water productivity and economic water productivity, with WUE increases of up to \(0.49\ \mathrm{kg\ m^{-3}}\) for one cultivar, while controlled subsurface systems increased water productivity without markedly harming irrigation application efficiency \parencite{DarziNaftchali2017b,Asgari2021}. In Pakistan, controlled subsurface drainage maintained groundwater near design depth, produced a maximum wheat yield of \(6.5\ \mathrm{t\ ha^{-1}}\) with water-use efficiency of \(0.93\ \mathrm{kg\ m^{-3}}\), and allowed large irrigation savings (23--129\% of the required depth) that could be reserved for drought \parencite{Khan2003}, while a water-conservation schedule could save up to \(25\%\) of scarce canal water \parencite{Sarwar2001}. In China, buried pipes raised irrigation water productivity over the control, highest at 1.4-m burial \parencite{Liu2025}.

\subsection{Weather dependence, timing, and earlier planting}

A consolidated finding is that drainage yield benefits scale with excess-water pressure and thus with weather. In Indiana, only the undrained control showed a significant negative corn-yield response to rainfall in the 14 days after planting, and growing-season rainfall was positively related to yield in drained systems, especially at 5-m spacing \parencite{Rui2024}. Wet years tended to favour narrower spacing while dry years favoured intermediate or wider spacings \parencite{Kladivko2005,Acharya2019}. Simulated yield reductions in the Canadian Prairies were more strongly associated with excess moisture early in the season than with late-season drought \parencite{Cordeiro2015}. For sugarcane, wet years favoured subsurface drainage whereas a dry year favoured a high, constant water table \parencite{CAMP1982}. In Japan, yield declined with the number of heavy-rain days (\(>30\ \mathrm{mm}\)) in fields without subsurface drainage but showed no such relationship where drainage was present, and mean buckwheat yield was much higher with subsurface drainage; raised-bed planting compensated for the absence of drainage except under exceptionally heavy rainfall \parencite{Takeshima2023}. Historically, comparisons on undrained versus artificially drained land showed corn yields more sensitive to precipitation on undrained land, with the largest physical yield gains from drainage on naturally poorly drained soils \parencite{JORJANI1991}.

Drainage also captures value through earlier field operations. Under producer conditions, artificial drainage was associated with sowing about 7--8 days earlier than natural drainage; combined with sowing-delay yield penalties of \(13\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}\ d^{-1}}\) (subsurface) and \(18\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}\ d^{-1}}\) (surface), one week's earlier sowing implied a \(91\textrm{--}126\ \mathrm{kg\ ha^{-1}}\) benefit \parencite{Mourtzinis2021}. Subsurface drainage similarly allowed earlier soybean planting and avoided the \(29\textrm{--}52\%\) harvest plant-population losses seen in nondrained controls in wet years \parencite{Nelson2011}.

\subsection{Modelling, design tools, and economics}

Simulation models—principally DRAINMOD—are well established for predicting drain flow, water-table depth, and relative yield, providing a validated basis for design. Reported performance is generally good: a modified DRAINMOD explained \(63\%\) of corn-yield variation over 12 years with close agreement on water-table elevations \parencite{HARDJOAMIDJOJO1982}; Indiana calibration/validation achieved Nash--Sutcliffe efficiencies of 0.70--0.85 for drain flow, with monthly flow predicted well in most plot-years and relative-yield patterns generally reproduced \parencite{Ale2009,Wang2006b}; Chinese predictions matched field water tables with a correlation of 0.97 \parencite{Li2018}; and Canadian Prairie simulations of water table and drainage outflow were rated good to excellent \parencite{Cordeiro2015}. In South Dakota, DRAINMOD reproduced measured subirrigated corn yields (representative \(R^{2}=0.53\)) and was judged the only assessment of DRAINMOD corn yields under predominantly irrigated conditions \parencite{DeBoer2006}. A simple steady-state Hooghoudt-based method gave reasonable estimates of drain depth and spacing to optimise profits, with a pooled drainage-rate regression (\(R^{2}=0.63\)) and soil-specific relationships up to \(R^{2}=0.94\) \parencite{Skaggs2006}. Detailed water-flow models (D-HYSAM) using a macroscopic root-extraction term reproduced soil-water pressure, moisture, and phreatic-surface positions in California \parencite{KARAJEH1994,KARAJEH1994b}. Design tools such as spreadsheet performance-index calculators enable multi-objective design that balances profitability against drainage volume and runoff \parencite{Sands2015}. Historically, the optimal water-table depth was recognised to depend on soil profile, crop, and permeability, and drain spacing could be indicated satisfactorily from soil permeability and root-zone conditions \parencite{HOOGHOUDT1952}.

Economically, drainage investments are frequently justified but soil- and crop-dependent. Iowa payback periods were seven years or less for all spacings, varying with soil type, spacing, and corn price \parencite{Arora2024}. Returns to drainage solely for higher corn yield were low on heavy soils (high installation cost) and highest on lighter soils, with the biggest payoff coming from enabling a shift from oats to corn on poorly drained land—so drainage is most attractive when it enables a switch from low- to high-value crops \parencite{JORJANI1991}. In Iran, installation costs were readily justified by annual increases in rice and canola yields, and subsurface drainage even enabled canola after rice (impossible under waterlogged surface drainage), raising both yield and cropping intensity \parencite{DarziNaftchali2014}. Combination subsurface irrigation-and-drainage systems in South Dakota appeared economically viable on suitable soils, with expected corn-yield increases of roughly \(40\textrm{--}100\%\) over dryland yields \parencite{DeBoer2006}. Managed strategies that minimise pumped water (e.g., continuous subirrigation from a defined growth stage with an optimised weir setting) reduced water use and labour costs while sustaining yield, enhancing the economic case for subirrigation \parencite{Ale2009,Gunn2019}.

\subsection{Agronomic side effects, integrated management, and adoption}

Beyond grain yield, drainage management affects grain quality, nutrients, and disease. Grain nutrient removal was greater under free drainage and drainage water recycling, though grain quality concentrations were largely unaffected in Missouri corn \parencite{Kaur2021}. In rice, milling recovery and protein content varied modestly across drainage treatments \parencite{DarziNaftchali2017b}. Notably, subsurface drainage that did not raise grain yield still increased grain protein: in spring wheat and soybean, protein rose with drainage coefficient even though yields were unchanged \parencite{Wiersma2010}. Foliar pyraclostrobin reduced Septoria brown spot and frogeye leaf-spot severity by \(2\textrm{--}8\%\) and interacted synergistically with drainage water management, while drainage itself did not affect disease severity \parencite{Nelson2011b}.

Drainage is increasingly embedded in integrated soil-and-water conservation. Reviews conclude that no single practice resolves difficult (e.g., duplex) soils, which require systems approaches combining tillage, rotations, ameliorants, and subsurface drainage; drainage was reported to eliminate waterlogging and deliver the largest, most reliable production gains on soils with stable subsoils \parencite{GARDNER1992}. Waterlogging-mitigation syntheses place combined drainage and crop management as the foremost step for severe waterlogging, with tolerant varieties and agronomic practices for minor cases, and the best strategy depending on severity, soil, crop, and local conditions \parencite{Manik2019}. Landscape-integrated frameworks combine tile drainage with terraces, water-retention structures, supplemental irrigation, and conservation tillage to improve field drainage while storing and reusing runoff and tile water \parencite{Li2021b}. Efficient drainage is also framed as a precondition for realising the benefits of conservation practices and improved genetics \parencite{Rui2024}, and cover crops or perennial forage can modestly reduce early-season drainage flow (e.g., a \(32\%\) May reduction under orchardgrass) with implications for flood mitigation in drained landscapes \parencite{Goeken2015,Daigh2014}. Broader land-use hydrology remains less certain, with catchment-scale flood impacts of drainage and land-use change identified as poorly understood and needing systems-thinking and connectivity-based upscaling \parencite{Rogger2017}.

Adoption evidence confirms drainage as a leading adaptive practice. Roughly three-fourths of surveyed U.S. producers reported at least some artificially drained cropland (22.6\% fully drained), and warm-season precipitation and experience with saturated soils were positive predictors of drainage adoption \parencite{Morton2015}. Finnish farmers prioritised well-functioning subsurface drainage and soil organic content as their main climate-adaptation measures—ahead of irrigation—with close to \(90\%\) rating subsurface drainage important, though many considered maintenance too expensive and called for subsidies \parencite{PeltonenSainio2021}. Japan's evolving rice-field drainage standards illustrate how design and policy co-evolve, including new uses of subsurface facilities for irrigation and ecosystem restoration \parencite{Ogino2007}.

\subsection{Boundary conditions and limits}

The evidence also delineates where drainage yields little or must be managed carefully. In the Red River of the North Basin, subsurface drainage did not improve spring wheat or soybean grain yield across drainage coefficients tested—only protein rose—because existing land leveling and surface drainage were already adequate, indicating limited applicability there \parencite{Wiersma2010}. Yield response also varies by soil texture: the meta-analysis found medium-textured soils most responsive to controlled drainage and subirrigation, with fine- and coarse-textured soils showing no clear effect, and the largest benefits under drier growing seasons in coarse and medium soils \parencite{Singh2022b}. Over-drainage can reduce yield through induced drought, as seen where 15-m spacing promoted yield loss and closer spacings and deeper weirs required careful management to avoid excessive water table lowering \parencite{Cordeiro2015,DeBoer2006}. Site hydraulic properties can nullify subirrigation benefits entirely, as at Defiance, Ohio \parencite{Gunn2018,Gunn2019}. Historic water-table experiments established the trade-off directly: yields increased as the water table was lowered from 40 to 150 cm, higher water levels produced earlier ripening and lower yield, and gains diminished at greater depths—so an optimum depth exists that varies with soil, crop, and permeability \parencite{HOOGHOUDT1952}. Finally, installation is necessary but not sufficient: without timely maintenance, observed water-table depths and drainage discharges can fall well short of design and fail to control shallow water tables \parencite{Malota2016}. Where soils are already well aerated—as in newly constructed sand rootzones—additional drainage produced no meaningful improvement in quality or rooting \parencite{Bigelow2001}. Collectively, these limits reinforce that the well-supported yield benefits of drainage are contingent on excess-water stress being present, on appropriate spacing and depth for the soil and climate, and on sustained system function.

5.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Agricultural subsurface (tile) drainage exerts a pervasive and quantifiable influence on streamflow, reshaping how water is partitioned among pathways and how the resulting hydrograph behaves across both event and non-event periods. The evidence base assembled here spans edge-of-field and plot monitoring, isotopic and geochemical tracer studies, hydrograph-separation and recession analyses, physically based and analytical modeling, regional data synthesis, and a global meta-analysis, and it converges on several well-supported conclusions. First, tile drainage constitutes a major component of watershed water yield, frequently supplying on the order of 40--65\% of streamflow during much of the year, with the largest contributions during spring and other wet, low-intensity periods \parencite{King2014,Thomas2016,Schilling2019}. Second, drainage systematically raises low flows and the baseflow fraction, dampens intermediate flows, and has comparatively little effect on the very largest floods \parencite{Sloan2017,Schilling2008,Rathore2025}. Third, artificial drainage \emph{homogenizes} landscape hydrologic response, shortening and stabilizing travel times and rendering hydrologic signatures more scale-invariant, with the distinction between tiled and non-tiled behavior fading above characteristic catchment sizes of roughly 2000~km$^2$ \parencite{BolandBrien2014,DaneshYazdi2016,FoufoulaGeorgiou2015}. Fourth, runoff generation in drained fields is governed by pathway-activation thresholds tied to antecedent moisture and rainfall, with tile flow typically the dominant and most persistent export pathway \parencite{Williams2019,Williams2018,Macrae2019}. Finally, effects on flashiness, peak flows, and streamflow drought are conditional on slope, soil, storm size, and drainage intensity, so drainage can either enhance or attenuate event response depending on setting \parencite{Zhou2025,Rathore2025,Adelsperger2024}. The subsections below synthesize these lines of evidence in turn, together with the mechanistic, tracer, and modeling work that underpins them.

\subsection{Tile drainage contributions to streamflow volume}

\subsubsection{Annual and event-scale partitioning}

A consistent body of watershed-scale work establishes that tile drains route a large and measurable share of precipitation to streams. In an Ohio watershed, 21\% of precipitation (206~mm) was recovered annually through tile drainage, which accounted for 41\% of the annual and 47\% of the monthly total watershed discharge; annual discharge averaged 498~mm, or 49\% of precipitation, split into 41\% baseflow and 59\% storm-event discharge \parencite{King2014}. Equivalent-medium modeling of an Iowa watershed in 2014 attributed 97~mm of tile drainage at the outlet---13\% of precipitation and 39\% of streamflow---within an annual water balance of $P=770$~mm, $ET=522$~mm, and $Q=248$~mm ($Q/P=0.32$) \parencite{Thomas2016}. In the Boone River (Iowa), end-member mixing analysis (EMMA) and SWAT bracketed the tile contribution at roughly 46\% to 54\% of discharge, with SWAT estimating about 53.7\% of Boone River and 65.8\% of Lyons Creek annual water yield over a 30-year simulation, and EMMA yielding 31--53\% (maximum) or 19--37\% (minimum) of streamflow over six years \parencite{Schilling2019}. Snowmelt-dominated SWAT modeling in a US--Canada watershed found tile flow equal to about 16\% of annual precipitation, or roughly 37\% of water yield, while tiling reduced surface runoff and increased subsurface flow, water yield, and drainage \parencite{Rahman2014}. Field-scale export can be even more tile-dominated: in Ohio, tile discharge accounted for $83\pm20$\% of water export where both surface runoff and tile flow were measured \parencite{Williams2018}, and most discharge from an Indiana closed depression was delivered by subsurface tile drains rather than surface flow \parencite{Williams2019}.

The partitioning between tile and total flow varies systematically with hydrologic condition and drainage area. In the Iowa equivalent-medium study, tile drainage represented on average 30\% of streamflow during precipitation events but 61\% during non-event periods; event-period $Q_T/Q$ increased with drainage area (mean slope $0.10$, mean $R^2=0.83$) whereas non-event $Q_T/Q$ decreased with area (mean slope $-0.17$, mean $R^2=0.82$), and the annual $Q_T/Q$ rose log-linearly from 0.29 at 6~km$^2$ to 0.39 at 45~km$^2$ ($R^2=0.98$) \parencite{Thomas2016}. Tile-flow magnitude also increased with contributing area, with full-pipe flow more frequent in smaller edge-of-field tiles than in larger mains \parencite{King2014}. The equivalent saturated hydraulic conductivity representing this behavior scaled with drainage density, from 164~m~d$^{-1}$ for collector drainage to 530~m~d$^{-1}$ at 10~m spacing ($R^2=0.99$) \parencite{Thomas2016}.

\subsubsection{Seasonal dynamics}

Tile contributions are strongly seasonal, peaking when soils are wet and evapotranspiration is low. Tile discharge in Ohio was seasonally variable, with median monthly summer discharge (0.9~mm) far below winter (23.4~mm), spring (10.2~mm), and fall (15.6~mm) values \parencite{King2014}. In the Boone River, tile drainage dominated flow from March through June, and October--November yields were largely groundwater plus tile discharge \parencite{Schilling2019}. DRAINMOD simulations in Iowa indicated the largest drainage effect on baseflow from March to July---more than 40\% more baseflow in drained watersheds and about 60\% more in April, May, and June \parencite{Schilling2008}. Expanded subsurface drainage was projected to decrease winter monthly flows, increase late-summer and fall flows, and have only a negligible effect on the following spring's flow volumes \parencite{Rahman2014}. In cold regions, tile flow generally occurred during snowmelt, tracking the synchrony between snow-water-equivalent depletion and drainage, with a seasonal absence of tile flow from May to September \parencite{Kompanizare2024}. Tile outflow can be a small fraction of rainfall during growing seasons except in continually wet years, reflecting how water-table position relative to tile depth gates drainage: in Iowa, mean water tables in a well-drained block lay below tile depth 72\% of the time and gradients steepened during wet periods, promoting lateral flow \parencite{Logsdon2020}.

\subsection{Modification of the flow regime}

\subsubsection{Low flows and baseflow}

A robust and repeated finding is that tile drainage elevates low flows and the baseflow fraction. Modeling and monitoring in Iowa showed drained landscapes carried far higher baseflow than undrained: average annual streamflow of 208~mm with 150~mm baseflow (72\%) at 24~m tile spacing versus 201~mm streamflow with 52~mm baseflow (25\%) with no tiles; tile drainage was concluded to primarily affect the baseflow portion of the hydrograph, though a detectable stormflow signal also appeared in tile hydrographs \parencite{Schilling2008}. Ohio simulations similarly found that tiles greatly increased low flow while decreasing higher flows to a lesser extent, raised annual average daily flow by about 5\% (via reduced evapotranspiration in the drier tiled soils), and lowered the probability of the water table entering the root zone ($<0.5$~m) from about 50\% without tiles to about 5\% with tiles \parencite{Rathore2025}. Routing analysis reinforced the general pattern that tile drains increase low flows, decrease intermediate or medium flows, and have minimal effect on the largest flows \parencite{Sloan2017}. In Latvia, drainage installation raised monthly mean and minimum river discharge, with significant increases in minimum monthly discharge in April, June, July, and August; mean runoff increased by 132--186\% during summer--autumn low-water periods (June--October) and by 53--286\% in the winter low-water period, while annual mean, minimum, and maximum discharge rose by 46\%, 158\%, and 26\%, respectively \parencite{Apsite2017}. Channelized (drained) headwater streams in Ohio likewise showed greater minimum and 10th-percentile discharge and far fewer zero-discharge days (8~d~yr$^{-1}$) than natural streams (87.5~d~yr$^{-1}$), inverting part of the accepted headwater paradigm \parencite{King2009}.

\subsubsection{Peak flows and flood response}

Effects on peak flows are consistently modest for the largest events but scale- and condition-dependent for smaller ones. Routing modeling found peakflow reductions concentrated at intermediate scales (100--1{,}000~km$^2$) and smaller at the largest scales ($>10{,}000$~km$^2$); for one event, reduction fell from 26\% at Wolf Creek watershed to 11\% at the Cedar River Basin outlet, and for another from 18\% at field scale to 10\% at the basin outlet, with negligible field-scale effect on the largest floods \parencite{Sloan2017}. The spatial placement of tiled hillslopes mattered: two watersheds with identical drained fractions could respond differently, and draining headwaters produced the greatest peak reduction because the width function clustered hillslopes there \parencite{Sloan2017}. Field-scale drained and undrained scenarios in Iowa showed no statistically significant difference in mean annual peak discharge for any of six soil types, indicating that drainage chiefly alters the flow pathway rather than the largest peaks \parencite{Sloan2016}. In Ohio ATS simulations, tiles could either enhance or attenuate event peaks depending on event size and antecedent conditions---enhancement for sub-threshold or dry-condition events, reduction for larger events---and streamflow was reduced by drainage in 23 of the 25 largest events, with a decision boundary separating positive from negative peak reduction 75\% of the time \parencite{Rathore2025}. Under expanded tiling, smaller peaks became more frequent while larger peaks declined (turning point at a 3.44-yr return period, not statistically significant), and steeper rising limbs with slightly shorter time-to-peak implied that reduced large-event peaks arose from reduced flow volumes rather than altered timing \parencite{Rahman2014}. Distributed hillslope modeling similarly found that drainage tends to decrease peak flow while increasing recession volume, reproducing observed behavior even with only 21\% of the area tiled \parencite{Fonley2021}.

\subsubsection{Flashiness}

Flashiness responds to drainage in a soil- and slope-dependent manner rather than monotonically. A global meta-analysis of 464 watersheds in 13 countries found that stream flashiness (Richards--Baker Index, RBI) was similar overall regardless of tile-drainage extent, but diverged by setting: in flat ($<2$\% slope) watersheds, medium and high drainage extents had higher RBI than low extent, whereas in sloping watersheds low extent had the highest RBI; in low-infiltration soils RBI was higher in lightly drained watersheds, while in high-infiltration soils RBI was similar across classes but the baseflow index (BFI) was higher where drainage was low \parencite{Zhou2025}. Drainage extent itself was positively associated with agricultural land use and clay content and negatively with slope and depth to water table \parencite{Zhou2025}. Field-scale Iowa simulations showed drainage decreased the flashiness index for low-conductivity soils and increased it for high-conductivity soils, thereby homogenizing response across soil types, with a nonlinear, U-shaped dependence on drain spacing and an optimal spacing near 15~m for the Webster soil \parencite{Sloan2016}. Ohio modeling found overall flashiness decreased with tiles, with a nonmonotonic minimum near 50~m spacing \parencite{Rathore2025}. Across 30 Nordic and Baltic catchments, the flashiness index was negatively related to BFI and positively related to the share of agricultural area under artificial drainage and to drainage intensity; computing flashiness from hourly rather than daily discharge increased it by nearly a factor of three on average (range 1.2--4.8) \parencite{Deelstra2014,Deelstra2014b}.

\subsubsection{Streamflow drought and long-term trends}

At the regime scale, tile drainage reshapes streamflow-drought characteristics. Across US watersheds, drainage significantly increased streamflow drought duration and intensity while reducing drought frequency, with effects comparable in magnitude to water-table depth and precipitation seasonality; each 10\% increase in tile-drained area lengthened drought duration by 0.03~d, raised intensity by 12\%, and lowered frequency by 0.10 events~yr$^{-1}$, and the result was robust to $\pm25$\% variation in the drained-area estimate \parencite{Adelsperger2024}. Regional trend analysis in Indiana found increasing low- and medium-flow trends, whereas high-flow trends were less linked to precipitation in heavily drained northern Indiana; accounting for autocorrelation and long-term persistence sharply reduced the count of significant low- and medium-flow trends, and the weakened precipitation linkage in drained areas suggested a drainage influence on streamflow trends warranting further study \parencite{Kumar2009}. A synthesis of seven Midwestern rivers concluded that inter-annual variability in streamflow, baseflow, and nitrogen load was controlled primarily by current- and previous-year precipitation rather than land cover or fertilizer, with adjusted $R^2$ up to 0.77 for baseflow in the Minnesota River, implying that recent river N-load increases are largely climate-driven and that tile-drained water will require remediation to reduce loads \parencite{Baeumler2020}.

\subsection{Scale dependence and hydrologic homogenization}

A central, well-supported theme is that artificial drainage homogenizes catchment response and reduces the scale dependence of hydrologic signatures. Data synthesis across Iowa showed that non-tiled watersheds exhibit decreasing area-normalized peak discharge and increasing baseflow ratio with scale, whereas tiled basins behave scale-invariantly; within-basin variability in recession coefficients declines as drained fraction rises, flow-duration curves show greater variability in non-tiled than tiled regions, and the tiled/non-tiled distinction disappears above roughly 2200~km$^2$, even though mean annual runoff ratio does not vary significantly with tiling \parencite{BolandBrien2014}. Travel-time analysis in Minnesota's Redwood basin found time-averaged mean travel time fell 41\%---from 77~d before land-use change to 45~d after, with the largest reduction in the growing season---while year-to-year variability was substantially reduced and travel time became independent of catchment size above about 200~km$^2$, indicating a more homogenized response under extensive tiling \parencite{DaneshYazdi2016}. Companion analysis showed streamflow dynamics became more linear after 1976 even though precipitation dynamics changed little, with strengthened dependence of streamflow on previous-day rain and more punctuated falling limbs \parencite{FoufoulaGeorgiou2015}. Isotope and transit-time work in a managed UK catchment found short mean transit times ($<1$~yr) with even faster artificial drains, and concluded that management---particularly artificial drainage---homogenized distributed catchment responses despite variation in subcatchment characteristics \parencite{DimitrovaPetrova2020}. In an Indiana tile-drained watershed, subwatersheds shared very similar spatial distributions of available water storage and hydrograph shape, so the basin behaved as an effectively homogeneous unit describable by a single exponential travel-time distribution with a 2--3~d mean \parencite{Basu2010}. Even so, tile contributions to annual discharge remained detectable as basins grew---beyond 16{,}000~km$^2$ in the Boone River---while diminishing at the largest scales \parencite{Schilling2019}.

\subsection{Runoff generation, flow pathways, and thresholds}

\subsubsection{Pathway activation and thresholds}

Runoff generation in drained fields is governed by thresholds linking antecedent moisture and rainfall to pathway activation. In an Indiana closed depression monitored over 289 rainfall events, rainfall amount alone related positively and linearly to cumulative flow with no visible threshold, but a clear threshold emerged when antecedent soil-moisture deficit (SMD) and rainfall were summed; thresholds varied by pathway and landscape position and were nearer zero at shoulder-slope than footslope sensors \parencite{Williams2019}. Subsurface tile flow generally began earlier, peaked earlier, and lasted longer than surface flow, and connected localized zones of saturation to enable rapid connectivity \parencite{Williams2019}. Isotopic work at the same site showed tile-drain celerity ($Q/P$) was largely controlled by antecedent wetness, exhibiting threshold behavior in SMD-plus-rainfall and in maximum groundwater depth relative to tile depth, with preferential flow more important under dry conditions and stored pre-event water dominating when wetter \parencite{Williams2021}. In near-level vertisolic clay in Canada, overland flow generally activated before groundwater and, in the tiled field, before tile flow in 14 of 18 events; tile-flow activation time related significantly to maximum rainfall intensity ($r^2=0.68$) and antecedent precipitation ($r^2=0.57$), improving to $r^2=0.78$ combined, and direct preferential flow into tiles was uncommon, implying a largely top-down sequence and tiles somewhat decoupled from the surface \parencite{Kokulan2019}.

Across US and Canadian sites, surface runoff was less frequent than tile flow: of 795 events, 43\% produced both surface and tile flow and 57\% produced tile flow only, and among dual-pathway events surface flow contributed a median of 12\% of total flow (range $<$1--97\%) \parencite{Macrae2019}. Surface-runoff frequency varied by texture and region (34--54\% of events on clay; 24--32\% at Ontario silt-loam sites; 48--59\% at Ohio silt-loam sites; 29\% at the clay-loam site), and event-response types (Tile, Surface, Simultaneous, Saturation) partitioned systematically by soil texture and season \parencite{Macrae2019}. In Kansas tile-outlet-terrace systems, baseflow contributed 38\% of discharge at one site, 51\% at another, and 46\% at a third, with short events dominated by quickflow and long events carrying larger baseflow components; antecedent conditions and storm size were stronger drivers of hydrologic and biogeochemical response than tile-inlet density alone \parencite{Stops2022}.

\subsubsection{Preferential versus matrix flow}

Tracer and geochemical studies partition tile flow into fast preferential and slower matrix components whose balance shifts with season and wetness. In a French field, seasonal runoff generation differed: under dry conditions rainwater transferred directly to tile drains through macropores, whereas under saturated conditions pre-event soil water dominated and rainfall contributed up to 25\% of tile-drain water; surface and tile drainage transferred 44\% and 20\% of annual effective rainfall, respectively, and 65\% of infiltrating water was not captured by tiles \parencite{Gaillot2023}. Catchment-scale monitoring there identified two soil-column transfer pathways---slow drainage of saturated soils and fast preferential flow through macropores/cracks---with the fast peak contributing about 15\% of water and sediment fluxes on average \parencite{Grangeon2021}. In Ohio, master-recession analysis distinguished two reservoirs (recession coefficients 0.9 and 0.25~d$^{-1}$; Reservoir~1 accounting for 54\% of subsurface flow), and a coupled pathway-connectivity separation apportioned annual tile discharge into 9\% quick-old, 39\% quick-new, 42\% slow-old, and 10\% slow-new, showing that new water routes through both quick- and slow-flow reservoirs and that groundwater recharge contributes importantly to tile fluxes \parencite{Nazari2021}. In Canada, preferential flow estimated from temperature and electrical conductivity agreed strongly ($r=0.83$), accounted for a higher fraction of tile flow in clay than loam soil, and correlated with phosphorus loads, indicating a disproportionate role in P export \parencite{Pluer2020}. Swedish drain-outflow modeling attributed autumn flow events to preferential flow and spring events to matrix flow under saturated conditions, with DRAINMOD-predicted cumulative infiltration explaining most variation in phosphorus concentrations \parencite{Salazar2011}. Isotopic hydrograph separation in Indiana showed groundwater was the primary source to the tile drain, followed by soil water, with minimal direct precipitation contribution and mean transit time increasing with depth (12--20~d at 10~cm to 245~d for tile drainage) \parencite{Williams2021}.

\subsubsection{Temporal concentration of flow and export}

Tile and surface flow are highly intermittent and temporally concentrated. In Ohio fields, surface runoff occurred on average $41\pm42$~d~yr$^{-1}$ and tile drainage $240\pm69$~d~yr$^{-1}$; 80\% of annual surface runoff occurred in 2.5\% of the year (9~d) and 80\% of tile discharge in 21\% (77~d), with Gini coefficients of 0.97--0.98 (surface) and 0.79--0.88 (tile) including zero-flow days \parencite{Williams2018}. Nutrient loads were similarly concentrated, and flow-duration/Lorenz analysis showed a 30\% cut in annual tile NO$_3$-N load would require treating tile flow 83\% of the time if targeting low flows but only 3\% if targeting high flows---a framework for sizing conservation practices to short windows of opportunity \parencite{Williams2018}. Managed-drainage monitoring reinforced this concentration: over a study period 287~mm of tile discharge (40.3\% of precipitation) occurred, 94\% of it when the outlet valve was open, and event discharge correlated strongly with the fraction of the event the valve was open (Pearson $r=0.86$) \parencite{Miller2022}.

\subsection{Travel times, water age, and groundwater interactions}

Modeling and tracer studies quantify how drainage shortens groundwater travel times and reorganizes the subsurface. In Iowa, groundwater travel-time distributions remained exponential across simulated drainage densities and incision depths; holding incision at 1.2~m, raising density from 0 to 0.0038~m$^{-1}$ shortened mean travel time and raised the tile contribution to baseflow to an upper bound of 37\% ($R^2=0.98$), while holding density constant and increasing incision from 0.3 to 2.7~m lowered mean travel time near-linearly (slope 1.45~yr~m$^{-1}$) and raised tile contribution from 30\% to 54\% \parencite{Schilling2012}. In Bear Creek, mean groundwater travel time fell from 5.57~yr at low drainage intensity to 1.07~yr at high intensity, far below the presettlement value of 181.6~yr, and intensive drainage short-circuited flow to riparian buffers---reducing land draining to buffers and cutting mean buffer residence time to 36.7~d or 15.5~d depending on the drainage-initiation scenario---thereby lowering buffer effectiveness for nitrate reduction \parencite{Schilling2015}. Analytical solutions confirm the mechanism: under tile drainage, groundwater levels are lower and decline faster, total groundwater discharge to the river increases, and the tile contribution to total discharge grows with tile density, though direct groundwater discharge to the river during recession is reduced because flow is diverted to the tiles \parencite{Liang2015}. Recession behavior of heavily tiled watersheds is correspondingly more linear than that of less-tiled watersheds \parencite{Schilling2008}, and differences in master recession curves were proposed as a diagnostic of tile contribution \parencite{Schilling2008}. Classical analytical treatments of transient recharge (Boussinesq solutions) provide the theoretical basis for such water-table and drain-flow dynamics \parencite{SINGH1980,Bouarfa2000}. Coexisting hydrograph-separation methods (SC-EMMA versus BFI-type baseflow programs) can nonetheless diverge substantially---bi-annual slowflow and baseflow indices differed by 5\% to 55\% across streams---underscoring method sensitivity when quantifying tile-influenced baseflow \parencite{Kronholm2015}.

\subsection{Controlled and managed drainage}

Controlled and managed drainage strongly modulate outflow volume and timing. In Sweden, controlled drainage reduced total drain outflow by 73--95\% relative to conventional drainage (e.g., 36, 14, and 120~mm versus 172, 240, and 450~mm over three years), concentrated more than 60\% of daily outflow in the 0--0.5~mm class, and produced lower retention constants and near-zero final storage; reduced storage was linked to higher peak flow, shorter lag, and shorter recession, effects that grew with groundwater level \parencite{Wesström2003}. Raising the drainage outlet significantly slowed water-table recession---by 29\% and 62\% in two site pairs---and lengthened the time for the water table to recede to 30 and 60~cm depths, whereas free-draining plots showed no such difference, confirming the effect was not seasonal \parencite{Saadat2017}. Agricultural drainage water management in Ohio was most effective at retaining water and reducing tile discharge during low-intensity events and dry antecedent conditions; the outlet valve was open on average 18.6\% of each three-day event yet passed 96\% of tile discharge, though average groundwater, soil moisture, and surface-flooding indicators did not differ significantly from free drainage \parencite{Miller2022}. In the Netherlands, subsurface water-infiltration systems significantly influenced water-table depth, reducing fluctuations at four of five sites and raising or deepening average water tables depending on applied water level and drainage base, with quadratic and delayed responses at several sites \parencite{vandeCraats2025}. Analyses of controlled-drainage and unreplicated watershed experiments highlight that method choice matters: paired-watershed, BACIP, beta/gamma-distribution, and time-series approaches can yield differing significance conclusions, with a beta distribution deemed most appropriate in one Minnesota dataset \parencite{Pagliari2021}. Cross-drain spacing further tunes recession, being most effective at 20~m spacing (reducing water-table recession by 13--32\%) and negligible by 40~m \parencite{Vaiciukynas2015}. Comparisons of drainage installation methods under Nordic conditions likewise revealed detectable performance differences: trenchless (T0) sections held groundwater levels 0.14--0.25~m higher than trench-installed (T1) sections in 60--90\% of observations, with the clearest differences when water levels rose above the 1.0~m drain depth and during autumn and winter \parencite{Salo2019}.

\subsection{Drain hydraulics, pressurization, and system design}

At the pipe scale, network pressurization introduces storage and delayed secondary peaks. In France, pressurization of the collector network during intense rainfall limited and sometimes reversed drainage discharge, temporarily storing infiltrated water as the water table rose and generating a four-stage sequence (first peak, backflow, second peak, recession) that increased the lag to the catchment outlet to 10--25~h \parencite{Henine2010}. A coupled groundwater--pipe model reproduced this behavior, with secondary peaks about 11--30~h after rainfall ended and estimated storage of 11--51~m$^3$ (38--46\% of stored recharge), the downstream network being most affected \parencite{Henine2014}. Drain flow rates were shown analytically to be governed by the system parameter $\sigma = K/(f^2 L^2)$, with maximum drain flow reaching 0.56~mm~h$^{-1}$ for $\sigma=10$~m$^{-1}$~h$^{-1}$ versus 0.025~mm~h$^{-1}$ for $\sigma=0.01$~m$^{-1}$~h$^{-1}$, and the constant-water-table-shape approximation improving as $\sigma$ increased \parencite{Bouarfa2000}. Combined tile/mole systems modeled with SEEP/W reproduced observed discharge well (e.g., $R^2=0.811$, index of agreement 0.94 in validation); a tile-only system was least effective, reducing drain discharge by 63\% and mean water-table depth by 72\% relative to the best combined systems, whose performance reflected the hydraulic conductivity above the mole channel \parencite{Tuohy2018}. Spatial patterns of drain flow were driven by topography and geology, with higher drain fractions in local depressions and amplified flow where hydraulic conductivity was higher \parencite{Mahmood2023}.

Component- and material-scale studies address inlets, media, and specialized drainage hardware relevant to flow and sediment. Surface-water-management structures such as tile risers and blind inlets altered flow duration and cumulative flow without necessarily changing discharge frequency, with direction of effect depending on the field, and blind-inlet infiltration declined from about 12--13 to 4--5~cm~h$^{-1}$ over six years, implying an 8--10-year service life under repeated tillage \parencite{Williams2020,Williams2019}. Replacing tile risers or open inlets with blind or gravel inlets substantially reduced sediment and phosphorus losses (e.g., TSS of 14.4 versus 40.4~kg~ha$^{-1}$~event$^{-1}$) \parencite{Feyereisen2015b}, and among alternative inlet designs a clear trade-off emerged between high flow capacity and sediment control \parencite{Li2017b}. In recycled concrete aggregate drainage media, permissible flow was strongly reduced by fines content (up to $\sim$73\% flow decline at 4\% fines), and filter-fabric opening size governed clogging potential \parencite{McCulloch2017}. Sheet-pipe subsurface drainage matched conventional pipe capacity and drained roughly 45\% more water with 55\% lower percolation, lowering water tables and improving aeration and rice yield \parencite{Tamura2021,Arif2021}. Numerical experiments on distribution pipes in saturated buffers showed two pipes could raise 10-day cumulative infiltration by up to $\sim$133\% relative to a single field-edge pipe, though at the cost of shorter subsurface travel distances \parencite{Jaynes2019b}. Deep-percolation and recharge estimation beneath drained lands, while uncertain, has long been recognized as part of the drainage water balance \parencite{BOOKMAN1959}.

\subsection{Coupled sediment, solute, and contaminant dynamics}

Because streamflow pathways carry material, hydrologic partitioning shapes sediment and solute export. In dual-drainage French fields, mean suspended-sediment yield was 0.79~t~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$ with strong inter-annual variability (0.49 to 1.08~t~ha$^{-1}$~yr$^{-1}$); 89\% originated from surface drainage, and tile drainage contributed 46\% and 21\% of runoff but only 9\% and 11\% of sediment yield in the two years, with most export during the intense drainage period \parencite{Gaillot2023b,Grangeon2021}. A spatially distributed sediment-connectivity index captured surface source, pathway, and sink areas well but did not adequately represent connectivity where subsurface tile drainage controlled vertical flow \parencite{Calsamiglia2018}. Solute behavior in tile systems tended toward chemostatic transport, with antecedent conditions and storm size---not inlet density---dominating the response \parencite{Stops2022}. Tile drainage also delivered elevated concentrations of antibiotic-resistance genes relative to surface water, with concentrations correlated to daily flow rate \parencite{Rieke2018}. Converting surface runoff to subsurface drainage did not necessarily improve water quality: an infiltration-type raingarden retained 61\% of flow and 76--81\% of pollutants, whereas a drainage-type raingarden retained only 36\% of flow and could export more phosphorus and nitrate than it received, indicating that short-circuiting runoff through subsurface pipes can lower pollutant removal \parencite{Xu2022}.

\subsection{Specialized applications: salinity, bank stability, and valley-bottom drainage}

Beyond humid croplands, subsurface drainage governs streamflow-relevant processes in salinized, erosion-prone, and valley-bottom settings. In saline Chinese soils, subsurface drains lowered surface electrical conductivity over time and suppressed resalination better than undrained controls---daily resalination rates of 0.19--1.55\% under drainage versus 1.82\% for the control at one day after irrigation---while remaining economically favorable \parencite{Yan2013,Chang2012}. Passive drains lowering the water table stabilized gully banks in Ethiopia, cutting cumulative bank retreat by 52--84\% and soil loss by about 70\% at low cost, with a drained-bank groundwater table about 20\% lower than controls \parencite{Zegeye2021}. In a Burkina Faso valley bottom, under-phreatic permeability increased downstream from about $0.10\pm0.10$ to more than $20\pm10$~cm~h$^{-1}$, so no single infiltration process described the whole toposequence---an important consideration for efficient drainage design \parencite{Keita2014}.

\subsection{Modeling tile-drainage streamflow dynamics}

The synthesis rests on a spectrum of models whose consolidated performance is itself an established result. Equivalent-medium representations reproduced explicit tile simulations within 2\% of drained-volume targets and provided calibrated equivalent properties across tile densities for watershed-scale application \parencite{Thomas2016}. Physically based cold-region modeling captured tile timing, peak magnitude, and the seasonal absence of summer flow, improving when hourly steps and capillary rise were represented, and showed the capillary fringe strongly controlled drainable water and tile flow \parencite{Kompanizare2024}. A modified ODE hillslope formulation improved watershed-scale recession and flood-threshold timing specifically at artificially drained gauges, providing generalized seepage--storage relationships with physically interpretable thresholds \parencite{Fonley2021}. SWAT was rigorously tested against daily tile flow (NSE~$=0.5$, PBIAS~$=-1.4$\%) and combined with response analysis to assess basin-scale drainage impacts \parencite{Rahman2014}. A calibration-free time-expansion (travel-time) model matched calibrated SWAT skill in an intensely drained Indiana watershed using a single exponential distribution \parencite{Basu2010}, and multiple travel-time modeling approaches of differing sophistication converged closely in Bear Creek \parencite{Schilling2015}. Coupled SC-EMMA and hydrograph-recession frameworks using inexpensive sensors improved prediction of dissolved reactive phosphorus relative to any single method \parencite{Nazari2021}, and coupled groundwater--pipe models successfully represented pressurization, temporary retention, and flooded-outlet recovery beyond design capacity \parencite{Henine2014}. Collectively, these tools demonstrate that the streamflow signature of tile drainage---elevated low flows and baseflow, damped intermediate flows, modest large-flood effects, shortened and homogenized travel times, and scale-dependent flashiness and peak response---is both mechanistically explicable and reproducible across observational and modeling lines of evidence.

11.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Denitrifying woodchip bioreactors---buried beds or walls of carbon-rich media that intercept subsurface (tile) drainage and support microbial denitrification---have become one of the best-characterised edge-of-field practices for reducing nitrate export from artificially drained cropland. The evidence base spans controlled microcosm and column studies, pilot- and full-scale field installations, and global syntheses, and it consistently establishes that woodchip bioreactors remove nitrate reliably across a wide range of climates and designs. Consolidating full-size systems worldwide, \textcite{Christianson2021} report a mean nitrate-N load reduction of $40 \pm 26\%$ (median 46\%; $n=15$) for bed-style bioreactors and nitrate-N removal rates on the order of $7.2 \pm 9.6$ g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ (median 5.1 g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$; $n=27$), and the practice has been assessed as removing between 20 and 40\% of annual nitrate-N loss in the Midwest. The largest single compilation to date, covering 36 site-years at ten full-size Illinois bioreactors, found a median annual removal of 49 kg N per bioreactor per year (4.1 kg N ha$^{-1}$ y$^{-1}$), a median edge-of-field N removal efficiency of 17\%, and removal in all years, reinforcing the dependability of the practice while pointing to room for design optimisation \parencite{Christianson2026}.

Four broad lines of evidence organise what is known. First, field-scale monitoring quantifies nitrate concentration reductions, load reductions, and volumetric removal rates and shows their dependence on hydraulic residence time (HRT), temperature, flow, and nitrate loading. Second, mechanistic and microbial studies establish denitrification as the dominant removal pathway, sustained over years by woodchip carbon and enhanced denitrifier populations. Third, an active design literature refines sizing, hydraulics, and performance-enhancement strategies (alternative carbon sources, P-sorbing media, pumping, electrical stimulation, and mineral amendments). Fourth, work on secondary effects documents by-products---nitrous oxide, methane, sulfate reduction, dissolved organic carbon (DOC), and phosphorus dynamics---and generally concludes that these ``pollution-swapping'' risks are real but small at landscape scale. The remainder of this section synthesises these lines of evidence, with the recurring limitation that additional study is still needed for phosphorus, pesticides, and greenhouse gases \parencite{Mitchell2023}.

\subsection{Field-scale nitrate removal: rates, efficiencies, and load reductions}

Field installations consistently reduce nitrate, but the magnitude spans a wide, well-explained range. In a controlled Illinois field experiment, bioreactors reduced nitrate loads by an average of 63\%, from a minimum of 20\% at 2-h HRT to a maximum of 98\% at 8-h HRT, with an average removal rate of 11.6 g NO$_3$-N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ (range 5--30) \parencite{Bell2015}. Under cold-climate conditions in Quebec, a three-year evaluation reported effluent nitrate-N reduced by 99\% and total-N by 72--73\% relative to influent, a 99\% reduction in flow-weighted nitrate-N load, and median removal rates of 6.84 g NO$_3$-N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ (by volume) and 2.99 g NO$_3$-N m$^{-2}$ d$^{-1}$ (by area); nitrate-N represented 84\% of total-N in the influent, non-compliance events fell by 96\%, and roughly 55\% of drainage passed through the bioreactors each year \parencite{Husk2017}. Across four South Dakota field bioreactors, nitrate-N concentration reductions ranged from 7\% to 100\% with removal rates of 5.2--26.5 g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ and load reductions of 39--89\% \parencite{Thapa2023}. A New Zealand system lowered outflow far below inflow, with removal efficiency of 31\% at 2.8 d HRT rising to 93\% at 6.4 d HRT \parencite{Rivas2020}, and a Missouri bioreactor typically produced effluent below 10 mg NO$_3$-N L$^{-1}$, cutting spring influent means of 17 mg L$^{-1}$ to 5 mg L$^{-1}$ and post-fertilisation means of 69 mg L$^{-1}$ to 21 mg L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Aide2016}.

Long-term and multi-site datasets place these numbers in context. In the Illinois ten-bioreactor compilation, systems removed a median 51\% of the N routed into them at a median annual rate of 4.6 g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$; standardised designs achieved a median flow-weighted HRT of 2.8 h (near the 3.0-h design target), and only annual N-loss removal differed significantly between standardised and hydraulic-maximised designs (4.7 versus 3.2 kg N ha$^{-1}$; $p=0.031$) \parencite{Christianson2026}. An early nine-year Iowa denitrification-wall study found annual nitrate loss of 24.5 kg NO$_3$-N ha$^{-1}$ under the wall versus 54.5 kg ha$^{-1}$ under conventional drainage, with no significant trend over time in either treatment \parencite{Moorman2010}. Not all field systems meet performance targets: a Virginia bioreactor at the low end of N inputs cumulatively removed only an estimated 9.5\% of influent NO$_3$-N (0.56 $\pm$ 0.25 g m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$) in its second year---greater in the growing (20.4\%) than non-growing (5.4\%) season---below the NRCS 25--45\% target, with removal rates from $-2.25$ to 8.10 g m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ and net export on 17\% of paired-sample days \parencite{Bock2018}. Similarly, a paired Illinois system removed only 6--28\% of edge-of-field nitrate (1--55\% for individual units), equivalent to 1.8--5.6 kg N ha$^{-1}$ (52--161 kg N field$^{-1}$) annually, and met the federal 20\% edge-of-field target in only one of three years \parencite{Maxwell2022}. Controlled column and laboratory studies confirm the achievable ceiling: complete nitrate removal at low flow (2.9 cm d$^{-1}$) declining to 30\% at 13.6 cm d$^{-1}$ \parencite{Greenan2009}, and 100\% removal over 100 days at 20 mg N L$^{-1}$ and 24-h HRT (10.1 g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$) \parencite{Hua2016}.

\subsection{Environmental and hydraulic controls on performance}

The dominant, repeatedly quantified control is hydraulic residence time, which trades off against the fraction of flow captured. Removal efficiency rises with HRT across studies---from 20\% (2 h) to 98\% (8 h) in Illinois \parencite{Bell2015}, from 31\% to 93\% over 2.8--6.4 d in New Zealand \parencite{Rivas2020}, and with efficiency increasing at longer daily HRT but trending negative below about 2 h in a paired Illinois system \parencite{Maxwell2022}. In the ten-bioreactor Illinois dataset, annual flow-weighted HRT correlated strongly with flow captured ($\rho=-0.67$) and with treated-water N removal efficiency ($\rho=0.60$) but not with edge-of-field efficiency ($\rho=0.03$), and increased HRT increased efficiency for treated water as the standardised-design theory predicts \parencite{Christianson2026}. Column work shows removal rate per unit wood increasing with flow while efficiency falls ($y=4.299x^{-1}$, $r^2=0.96$) \parencite{Greenan2009}, and heated-treatment tests showed removal rising from 1.5 mg NO$_3$-N L$^{-1}$ at 1 h to $>$18.6 mg L$^{-1}$ at 8 h \parencite{Ma2021}. Influent load also matters: higher loads raise removal \emph{rate} but lower percentage efficiency and outlet-concentration reductions, a pattern seen for bioreactors \parencite{Rivas2020,Christianson2026} and echoed in constructed-wetland syntheses \parencite{Mendes2021}. A meta-analysis of solid-carbon denitrification similarly found higher influent nitrogen associated with higher rates, and---for those studies---inferior removal above 5-h HRT \parencite{Zhang2021b}.

Temperature exerts a strong, consistent effect. In the only controlled Illinois field experiment holding influent nitrate and HRT constant, exponential functions of water temperature and HRT explained 73\% of the variance in load reduction and 43\% in removal rate \parencite{Bell2015}. In South Dakota, average removal was 56\% in March--June versus 81\% in July--August, falling by about 30\% below 12\,$^{\circ}$C \parencite{Thapa2023}; laboratory columns reached 96\% at 21.5\,$^{\circ}$C/24-h HRT versus 29\% at 10\,$^{\circ}$C/12-h HRT \parencite{Soupir2018}; and Michaelis--Menten $V_{max}$ dropped sharply from 22 to 5\,$^{\circ}$C for composted and fresh woodchips \parencite{Kouanda2021}. Cross-study analysis found low temperature ($<$20\,$^{\circ}$C) significantly depressed removal while intermediate and high temperatures did not differ \parencite{Zhang2021b}. Flow dynamics and antecedent conditions further modulate performance: removal was lower during high-flow events and at high inflow nitrate, and samples taken immediately after rainfall showed higher load removal than later samples \parencite{Thapa2023}. In Quebec, influent nitrate correlated with soil moisture ($r=0.64$) and prior-day temperature ($r=0.67$) and negatively with flow rate ($r=-0.53$), while in-bioreactor reduction tracked lagged temperature ($r=0.58$) and influent concentration ($r=0.71$), with no clear HRT--percent-removal relationship at that site \parencite{Husk2017}. Reaction kinetics underpin these observations: removal follows zero-order kinetics when nitrate is non-limiting (rate constants 1.42--1.80 mg N L$^{-1}$ h$^{-1}$) and shifts to first-order ($0.49$ h$^{-1}$) once nitrate becomes limiting \parencite{Hua2016}, and can be treated operationally as zero-order when nitrate greatly exceeds $K_m$ \parencite{Kouanda2021}.

\subsection{Carbon substrates, media, and removal mechanisms}

Denitrification is firmly established as the dominant nitrate-removal pathway. In Iowa microcosms it accounted for $>$98\% of transformed NO$_3$-N in all treatments (except cardboard, $>$96\%), with immobilisation $<$2.4\% and DNRA $<$1\% \parencite{Greenan2006}, and flow-through columns confirmed denitrification as the primary fate with immobilisation minor \parencite{Greenan2009}. Denitrification potential in woodchips is sustained and far exceeds surrounding soil: over nine years wood increased denitrification potential 31- to 4000-fold over subsoil, denitrifier populations exceeded $10^8$ g$^{-1}$ wood, and in situ push--pull testing gave nitrate half-lives of 0.64--0.81 d, implying 24-h HRT suffices to bring 20--25 mg L$^{-1}$ influent below 10 mg NO$_3$-N L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Moorman2010}. Reviews likewise identify denitrification as the main mechanism ahead of biological uptake and settling \parencite{Mendes2021}, while noting that DNRA (favoured under low NO$_3$-N and high carbon) and, more tentatively, anammox also occur \parencite{Hartfiel2022}.

Carbon quality and longevity govern long-term performance. Cellulose is the most rapidly consumed woodchip compound, making cellulose metabolism central to persistence \parencite{Hartfiel2022}. Woodchip decomposition is depth- and position-dependent: in an Iowa wall, wood loss reached 75\% at 90--100 cm (half-life 4.6 y) but $<$13\% at 155--170 cm (half-life 36.6 y) \parencite{Moorman2010}, and along a Minnesota bed the inlet showed greater decomposition (lower C/N, higher lignocellulose index) than downstream, with aged four-year woodchips more recalcitrant than fresh \parencite{Ghane2018}. Alternative and blended substrates can raise performance. Corncob-amended systems removed more nitrogen than woodchips alone: pilot CC75 reached 61.2\% at 16-h HRT versus 37.9\% for WC100, and modelled full-scale CC75 gave the highest annual load removal (15.6\%) and lowest cost, remaining cost-advantageous even with periodic corncob replacement \parencite{Law2023}. Corn stover blended up to 10\% with woodchips enhanced denitrification (mean concentration reductions of 86\% for corn stover, 89\% for the blend, 77\% for woodchips), though pure corn stover raised ammonium and total inorganic N and is not recommended as the main medium \parencite{Oladeji2023}. Microcosm rankings placed cornstalks $>$ cardboard $>$ wood plus soybean oil $>$ woodchips alone, with grinding and soybean-oil additions boosting removal \parencite{Greenan2006}, and Canadian-prairie testing found agricultural residues (barley straw best) outperforming woodchips for nitrate removal, though woodchips gave better hydraulic efficiency \parencite{Kohn2023}. Amendments further tune performance: activated carbon raised removal at low temperature ($<$10\,$^{\circ}$C) and cut organic-carbon losses while flaxseed cake did not improve removal despite greater carbon release \parencite{Povilaitis2020}; biochar increased nitrate removal in proportion to dose \parencite{Ahmadvand2020}; and composting woodchips before installation raised denitrification capacity 1.20--2.15-fold at 22\,$^{\circ}$C with better cold tolerance \parencite{Kouanda2021}. Broadly, synthetic solid carbon sources outperform natural ones, while mixed and single carbon sources did not differ significantly in a meta-analysis, indicating species matters more than mixing \parencite{Zhang2021b}. A recurring minor by-product is nitrite accumulation (9.6--18.7\% of reduced nitrate), which increases as HRT shortens and influent nitrate rises \parencite{Hua2016,Kouanda2021}.

\subsection{Microbial ecology of denitrifying bioreactors}

Bioreactor microbial communities are variable and shaped by substrate availability, depth, season, moisture, pH, temperature, inlet nitrate, dissolved oxygen, and HRT \parencite{Hartfiel2022}. Culture and molecular work has identified the responsible organisms, especially those active in cold conditions. From Minnesota bioreactors, screening of 206 isolates yielded three confirmed denitrifiers (\textit{Cellulomonas} sp. WB94, \textit{Delftia} sp. BE1.2, \textit{Microvirgula} sp. BE2.4), two of which denitrified aerobically and whose genomes carried denitrification and polysaccharide-degradation genes, marking WB94 and BE2.4 as bioaugmentation candidates \parencite{Anderson2020}. A parallel study identified denitrifiers active at cold woodchip conditions---enriching \textit{Pseudomonas}, \textit{Polaromonas}, and \textit{Cellulomonas}---and confirmed \textit{Cellulomonas} sp. WB94 (98\% ANI to \textit{C.\ cellasea}) reduces nitrate and degrades cellulose \parencite{Jang2019}. Nitrate-reducing fungi are also abundant and cold-adapted: densities reached 845,000 and 524,000 CFU g$^{-1}$ in soil and woodchips at 15\,$^{\circ}$C, 91 of 349 isolates produced gaseous N (frequently $^{29}$N$_2$, suggesting co-denitrification), 89\% showed cellulase activity, and \textit{nirK} and \textit{p450nor} were detected in 38\% and 12\% of strains \parencite{Aldossari2021}. Bioaugmentation shows conditional promise: pre-grown \textit{Pseudomonas} isolates from Danish woodchips did not help in the first assay but increased nitrate removal by 27--37\% at 5\,$^{\circ}$C in the second, with \textit{P.\ chlororaphis} PSS4 most effective \parencite{Jeglot2022}; however, inoculation alone often fails owing to rapid washout \parencite{Wang2025b}.

Community function tends to be robust. Under fluctuating water levels, denitrification rates and denitrifier communities were generally resistant to disturbance, and nitrous-oxide production rates were consistently about ten-fold lower than nitrate-removal rates \parencite{Hathaway2017}. Mineral amendment can steer the community: heated tourmaline reduced biofilm growth (lowest DNA) yet increased the relative abundance of \textit{nirS}-, \textit{nirK}-, and \textit{nosZ}-encoding denitrifiers, reducing clogging while giving the highest removal rates (52.1, 43.1, 48.0 g NO$_3$-N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ at 1, 4, 8 h) \parencite{Ma2021}. Biofouling deposits in drainage wells were controlled chiefly by dissolved iron rather than aerobic bacterial presence or dissolved oxygen, with stalked bacteria only where fouling occurred \parencite{Stuetz2004}.

\subsection{Bioreactor design, sizing, and hydraulics}

Sizing procedures aim to balance flow treatment against removal efficiency. Using 20\% of the Manning's-estimated peak flow generally treated the majority of annual volume (54\% at Hamilton, 72\% at SERF), and modelling supports design guidance of 10--20\% of peak flow at 6--8-h design retention times; for fixed volume, longer, narrower designs removed a greater percentage of load and had higher rates than short, wide designs \parencite{Christianson2013}. Load-duration analysis across three watersheds---where roughly 50\% of annual NO$_3$-N moved in the largest 30\% of daily base flows---showed that bioreactors sized to a 0.5-d HRT could remove about 44--52\% of base-flow nitrate and at least 20--30\% of total annual loss while occupying at most 0.27\% of watershed area \parencite{Moorman2015}. Notably, treating the majority of annual flow is not universally necessary where goals are load-based: a similar design flow rate ($\sim$1.7--1.8 L s$^{-1}$) corresponded to 50\% non-exceedance in small drainage areas but only 5\% in large ones, so treated volumes differed greatly by area \parencite{Christianson2025}. Loading-density guidance from the Conservation Practice Standard was not supported by the ten-bioreactor data, which showed no significant relationship between loading density and removal efficiency \parencite{Christianson2026}.

Hydraulic behaviour links design to performance. Across 14 tracer tests at six full-size bioreactors, high-aspect-ratio conventional designs behaved most like plug flow (tanks-in-series), the wide design showed the most dispersion and short-circuiting, and an advanced wide design with baffles achieved higher volumetric efficiency and trended toward the highest removals; dispersion/short-circuiting and volumetric/hydraulic-efficiency metrics were each strongly correlated \parencite{deOliveira2023}. A wide baffled Illinois bioreactor nonetheless did not raise the fraction of flow treated ($\sim$40\%) or removal relative to conventional designs---attributed partly to flashy drainage (Richards--Baker index 0.74--0.91)---yet still achieved consistent 22--24\% edge-of-field reduction with 54--69.5\% internal efficiency and clear along-path nitrate declines \parencite{Dougherty2020b}. Expanding cross-sectional area improved flow capture: a paired short-and-wide system captured 84--92\% of annual discharge from a 29-ha field \parencite{Maxwell2022}. Wood-filled bioreactors showed better hydraulic efficiency than other feedstocks in the Canadian prairies \parencite{Kohn2023}. For buffer-diverting designs, more diverted flow did not mean more nitrate removal, buffer width reduced diversion nonlinearly, and neglecting exit head loss overestimated diverted flow and removal, motivating a process-based design that includes a removal component \parencite{Abdalaal2023}. Physical durability is quantifiable: hand-held LiDAR at 17 full-size bioreactors captured surface subsidence, which correlated with age (median 1.0 cm y$^{-1}$ at soil-covered versus 7.3 cm y$^{-1}$ at non-covered sites; maximum 73 cm) and with proximity to the drainage outlet \parencite{Christianson2025b}.

\subsection{Design innovations and performance-enhancement strategies}

Several modifications extend or intensify treatment. Pumping can supply additional nitrate-laden water and lengthen the operating season: a retrofitted dual-chamber system pumped at 1.06--2.17 L s$^{-1}$ per chamber, extended operation by three weeks, and raised the combined daily removal rate to 6.58 g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ (from 3.62 without pumping), albeit bypassing 41\% of pumped flow untreated \parencite{Hartfiel2023}. At an underloaded Illinois bioreactor, pumping increased treated volume, loading, and removal---removing 2.8 kg N in a 29-d 2022 period (versus 0.69--2.0 kg N unpumped) and 7.2 kg N over 93 d in 2023---at daily rates of 7.5 and 5.2 g N m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$ and efficiencies of 57\% and 64\%, with nightly batching giving 100\% instantaneous removal and a small heating benefit \parencite{DíazGarcía2025}. Electrical stimulation improved denitrification at 500 mA (carbon anode--cathode best) but not at 100 mA, increased efficiency at the cost of current efficiency, and raised removal cost, so gains in efficiency would be needed for cost-effectiveness \parencite{Law2018}. Heated tourmaline both reduced clogging and gave the highest removal across tested HRTs \parencite{Ma2021}. Scaling up, a three-bed catchment-scale Minnesota bioreactor treated 55\% of catchment discharge and removed 713 kg nitrate-N (12.5\% of catchment load; 23\% of throughflow load), but sediment clogging during high flow reduced performance and required a sediment-exclusion system \parencite{Feyereisen2023}. Beyond tile drainage, spring bioreactors in the Chesapeake region averaged 41\% annual load reduction (up to $>$65\% in summer), and treating hundreds of springs was estimated to remove 322--771 kg d$^{-1}$ at low unit cost \parencite{Easton2019}. Non-biological alternatives include anion-exchange resin, which removed nitrate at both low and high flow with near-identical breakthrough volumes (favouring high-flow scale-up) but was hindered by sulfate selectivity and regenerant-disposal issues \parencite{MAGETTE1990}. Reviews add that carbon dosing and woodchip--corncob mixtures can substantially raise performance, though carbon dosing risks leaving unused effluent carbon and causing bioclogging or pollution swapping \parencite{Wang2025b}.

\subsection{Pollution swapping and greenhouse-gas by-products}

Nonideal conditions can generate N$_2$O, CH$_4$, CO$_2$, sulfate reduction, and methylmercury, but reported risks are generally low \parencite{Hartfiel2022}. For nitrous oxide, column and field studies consistently find emissions small relative to nitrate removed: N$_2$O production was 0.003--0.028\% of N denitrified in columns \parencite{Greenan2009}; an Iowa wall showed no significant N$_2$O difference from controls, indirect emission factors of $\sim$0.0085--0.0088 kg N$_2$O-N kg$^{-1}$ NO$_3$-N, and indirect losses equivalent to tile drainage without the bioreactor \parencite{Moorman2010}; and a Virginia bioreactor had an average N$_2$O emission factor of 0.07\% (median 0.01\%), well below the 0.75\% IPCC comparison, with GHG emissions not environmentally concerning \parencite{Bock2018}. Across five Illinois bioreactors, dissolved N$_2$O was produced at four sites but consumed at one, surface N$_2$O fluxes were much smaller than dissolved fluxes, only a small fraction of removed nitrate was emitted as N$_2$O, and modelled watershed-scale indirect N$_2$O emissions were lower with a bioreactor than without \parencite{Brunton2024}. Methane and sulfate responses are more variable. In Illinois, three of five bioreactors produced notable CH$_4$ (which drove global-warming potential where present) while soil covers significantly lowered surface CH$_4$ \parencite{Brunton2024}; in an Iowa dual-chamber system both chambers were significant CH$_4$ sources, one chamber removed sulfate and dissolved N$_2$O while the other did not, yet the landscape-scale impact was a $<$1\% increase in CO$_2$e, with pollution swapping more likely above 50-h HRT \parencite{Hartfiel2023b}. Sulfate reduction under non-N-limiting conditions is common and increases with HRT---occurring in 83\% of Illinois runs \parencite{Bell2015}, highest in activated-carbon media and associated with lower nitrate removal at long HRT \parencite{Povilaitis2020}, and indicating strongly reduced, potentially H$_2$S-producing conditions in New Zealand \parencite{Rivas2020}. Carbon leaching, especially at start-up, is a further concern: DOC shows an initial flush then decline \parencite{Abusallout2017,Rivas2020}, is dominated by hydrophobic, high-molecular-weight (10--100 kDa) fractions that are strong disinfection-by-product precursors with TOX yields comparable to Suwannee River Fulvic Acid, and can be removed by steel-chip filters or coagulation (alum and PAC more effective than ferric chloride) \parencite{Abusallout2017}. Ammonium and DOC also leach at higher flow \parencite{Greenan2009}, and a catchment-scale system net-exported 212 kg C (22\% of inlet DOC load) \parencite{Feyereisen2023}.

\subsection{Phosphorus co-removal and enhanced media}

Because woodchips remove little phosphorus---and often leach it at start-up---phosphorus co-treatment has driven the development of coupled media \parencite{Christianson2021}. Steel-based media are consistently effective P sorbents. Steel turnings achieved $>$90\% orthophosphate removal, and placing iron both upstream and downstream of woodchips overcame arrangement conflicts to enhance both nitrate and orthophosphate removal (woodchips reduce nitrate best downstream of iron, while iron re-captures P released by woodchips) \parencite{Goodwin2015}. Steel byproducts showed batch Langmuir $q_{max}$ of 1.30--5.81 mg P g$^{-1}$ and a continuous-flow capacity of 3.70 mg P g$^{-1}$, completely removing residual phosphate (1.0 g P m$^{-3}$ d$^{-1}$) and reducing effluent nitrite in a two-stage woodchip-then-steel system \parencite{Hua2016}. Fly-ash pellets removed on average 71.4\% of orthophosphate while paired woodchip--pellet configurations held total phosphate removal at 81.7--84.5\% \parencite{Li2018b}, and iron filings cut drainage P loads by 19\% alongside nitrate removal \parencite{Oladeji2023}. Field bioreactors can also become net P sinks over time: Quebec woodchips-only and mixed-media systems released P initially but became sinks within a year, with mixed media removing $\sim$19 times more total-P load and cutting soluble reactive P by 97\% (versus 53\% for woodchips only), though neither met the 0.030 mg L$^{-1}$ criterion \parencite{Husk2018}. A New Zealand bioreactor unexpectedly retained DRP in its second year \parencite{Rivas2020}, and a catchment-scale system removed DRP and TP for most events but exported TP at peak flow \parencite{Feyereisen2023}. Some systems are inconsistent: a Minnesota modular design acted as both P sink and source depending on month and media (fragmented limestone $>$ crushed concrete $>$ steel slag for apparent P-sorbing capacity), with media requiring annual replacement \parencite{Strock2025}, and P removal was not statistically significant in the Virginia system \parencite{Bock2018}. A promising biological route is drying--rewetting: cyclic draining and re-flooding of woodchip columns more than doubled orthophosphate removal (peaking 10--12 h after re-flooding), driven by biological uptake---confirmed because autoclaved woodchips did not take up phosphate and \textit{ppk} transcripts of \textit{Accumulibacter} and \textit{Pseudomonas} peaked with uptake---offering a simple co-treatment strategy for N and P \parencite{Zhang2024b}. Microbial P uptake, rather than woodchip sorption alone, may also explain variable DRP behaviour in columns \parencite{Soupir2018}.

\subsection{Secondary water-quality benefits and monitoring context}

Beyond nitrate, woodchip bioreactors provide co-benefits. Columns effectively removed \textit{Salmonella} and \textit{E.\ coli} under all conditions (greater at 21.5 than 10\,$^{\circ}$C), with temperature more influential than HRT, indicating a pathogen-removal co-benefit for systems run for nitrate \parencite{Soupir2018}. Reliable performance assessment depends on well-defined monitoring: bioreactors offer discrete influent and effluent points for direct measurement \parencite{Easton2019}, and standardised monitoring set-ups and minimum reporting measures have been recommended to compare annual load-removal performance \parencite{Christianson2021}. Supporting analytical methods have also been validated---for example the Dumas method for total N and $^{15}$N ratios of finely ground plant material and evaporatively concentrated drainage samples (highest reported recovery 97.4\%, improved with copper shot) \parencite{Schindler1999}---and resin-capsule methods can intercept nitrate moving via macropore and bypass flow, the primary transport pathway to subsurface drainage \parencite{Pampolino2000}.

\subsection{Cost-effectiveness and comparison with other edge-of-field practices}

Bioreactors are generally low-cost per unit nitrogen removed. Installation costs have ranged from under \$5,000 to \$27,000, with cost efficiencies generally from $<$\$2.50 to $\sim$\$20 kg$^{-1}$ N y$^{-1}$ (up to \$48) \parencite{Christianson2021}. Corncob (CC75) systems were the most cost-efficient carbon option (\$10.56--13.89 kg$^{-1}$ N, versus up to \$88.11 for woodchips) and remained advantageous even under conservative replacement assumptions \parencite{Law2023}. For pumped systems, unit cost fell as lifespan, mass-removal rate, and operating period rose---raising mass-removal rate from low to high cut unit cost by 91\%---with best-case costs of $\sim$\$4.80--23.81 kg$^{-1}$ NO$_3$-N (worst case $\sim$\$140--619), and increasing removal rate and operating period beyond six months identified as the strongest cost levers \parencite{Hartfiel2024}. Spring bioreactors achieved \$0.54--7.60 kg$^{-1}$ N y$^{-1}$, typically $<$\$5 for higher-flow, higher-concentration springs \parencite{Easton2019}, whereas electrical stimulation raised costs above the base case \parencite{Law2018}.

Set against other edge-of-field practices, bioreactors are effective for nitrate but comparatively limited in co-benefits. A US Corn Belt review of 119 records found all five practices (controlled drainage, water-quality wetlands, bioreactors, drainage water recycling, saturated buffers) effective at removing nitrate, but with differing additional benefits and disservices: water-quality wetlands gave the highest nitrate reduction plus phosphorus removal and habitat value, drainage water recycling offered the greatest yield and co-benefit potential, and denitrifying bioreactors removed nitrate effectively but with limited extra benefits and a need for further study of phosphorus, pesticides, and greenhouse gases \parencite{Mitchell2023}. Global synthesis places bioreactor flow-season removal at 14--66\%, alongside constructed wetlands ($\sim$39\% average TN removal) and saturated buffers and controlled drainage ($\sim$30--50\% annual N-load reduction), while noting that agricultural drainage's high-nitrate, low-carbon, low-temperature character complicates direct transfer of municipal treatment approaches \parencite{Wang2025b}. Constructed-wetland and paired-system studies reinforce the theme that design and sizing dominate outcomes: about 40--50\% N removal is generally achievable at area ratios of 1--4\% with minor gains beyond \parencite{Mendes2021}, and paired surface-flow wetland plus woodchip systems acted as nutrient sinks (TN removal 8--51\%, TP 0--80\%), with the woodchip component contributing more removal than the sedimentation basin and requiring smaller footprints than standalone wetlands \parencite{Pugliese2020}. Collectively, this evidence establishes woodchip bioreactors as a dependable, cost-effective nitrate-reduction practice whose performance is well predicted by HRT, temperature, flow, and loading, and whose principal remaining design frontiers concern phosphorus, greenhouse gases, and pesticides.

4.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Manure and other organic amendments sit at the intersection of two functions of subsurface (tile) drainage systems: they enrich soils with nutrients and organic matter, and they introduce fecal microorganisms, resistance genes, hormones, and trace elements that drainage networks can rapidly route to receiving waters. The evidence assembled here establishes several consolidated conclusions. First, the \emph{method} of manure application and the \emph{moisture state} of the soil, rather than manure presence alone, largely govern how much fecal contamination reaches tile drains, with preferential (macropore) flow acting as the dominant transport mechanism \parencite{Truhlar2015,Thiagarajan2007,Collins2007,Ding2021}. Second, manure and organic amendments deliver measurable agronomic and soil-structural benefits---reclamation of saline--sodic soils, higher yields, and, when combined with subsurface drainage and biochar, improved soil physical properties and nitrogen-use efficiency---though some physical benefits are transient \parencite{Qadir2001,Tian2023b,Liu2025f,Xiong2025,Brikmans2025}. Third, drainage systems and their engineered analogues (denitrifying bioreactors, constructed wetlands, reservoirs, and reconstructed oxbows) host distinct and dynamic microbial communities and can substantially attenuate nitrate and pathogen loads \parencite{Andrus2014,Porter2015,Hathaway2015,Knorr2023,Schilling2017,Haverstock2017}. Fourth, drainage water can concentrate and mobilise trace elements---most infamously selenium at Kesterson, but also arsenic, boron, uranium, and micronutrients---with documented consequences for wildlife \parencite{SAIKI1987,Ohlendorf2002,Ryu2010,Gao2007,Tayfur2010,Gul2013}. Finally, subsurface drainage reshapes the ecology of agricultural landscapes, from soil fauna to birds, amphibians, and semi-aquatic species \parencite{Nuutinen2001,Marja2013,Dreyfuss2018,Campbell2023}. This section synthesises the mechanisms and quantified outcomes underpinning each of these lines of evidence.

\subsection{Manure Application Method, Soil Moisture, and Microbial Transport to Tile Drains}

\subsubsection{Application method controls the transport of fecal bacteria}
Controlled comparisons demonstrate that how manure is placed on the field determines the microbial signal reaching drains. \textcite{Truhlar2015} found that roughly 45\% of \textit{E.\ coli} isolated from manure produced curli (an adhesive amyloid fibre) before field application, and that curli-producing \textit{E.\ coli} were far more abundant in tile drains from farms that surface-applied manure than in the drain of a farm that incorporated it. The same pattern held in soil-column leachate: the proportion of curli-producing \textit{E.\ coli} was greater under surface application than under incorporation or no manure, while total \textit{E.\ coli} recovered from leachate was higher in both manure treatments than in controls but did not differ between surface-applied and incorporated manure. Because the column manure came from a single source, and because the functional gene \textit{csgA} was present in all seven tested strains regardless of curli phenotype, the authors ruled out strain composition as the sole explanation and concluded that application method itself shapes the phenotype of \textit{E.\ coli} transported to drains---a result consistent across two surface-applied periods with different rainfall \parencite{Truhlar2015}. Complementary indicator work found that faecal coliforms, coliphages, and \textit{Salmonella} behaved differently: \textit{Salmonella anatum} was not detected in any of 36 leachate samples, male-specific coliphages were detected in all 36 samples, somatic coliphages were generally lower, and phage counts exceeded bacterial indicator counts in all plots; indicator numbers were similar in manure-treated and control plots, peaking in weeks 1--2 and declining thereafter \parencite{Malik2004}.

\subsubsection{Tillage, preferential flow, and pathogen loads}
Tillage governs pathogen delivery mainly through its effect on macroporosity and flow partitioning. Across two Canadian sites, zero tillage (ZT) generally produced higher subsurface flow volumes and greater \textit{E.\ coli} loads than conventional tillage, which the authors attribute to macropore development and preferential flow \parencite{Thiagarajan2007}. At Streets Ridge, combined-drainage \textit{E.\ coli} loads showed no overall significant tillage effect and the direction of differences switched between years, but when surface and subsurface drainage were separated, subsurface drainage under ZT contributed the greatest \textit{E.\ coli} losses in 2003--2004. At BEEC, tillage did not significantly affect \textit{E.\ coli} concentrations, yet subsurface-drainage loads were significantly higher under ZT in 2003--2004. Most samples exceeded drinking-water guidelines, and recreational-guideline exceedances were concentrated soon after manure application and were greater at Streets Ridge than at BEEC \parencite{Thiagarajan2007}. Hydrologic partitioning by tillage was also evident for manure-borne hormones: \textcite{Jenkins2009} reported that no-till plots had greater drainage and lower runoff than conventional-till plots in June 2002, and that November 2001 runoff loads of estradiol and testosterone were lower from no-till than conventional-till plots.

\subsubsection{Soil moisture, water table, and drain-flow contamination}
Antecedent soil-moisture state and water-table position exert first-order control on contamination episodes. In New Zealand pastoral systems, effluent applied to wet soil produced drain-flow \textit{E.\ coli} of $10^{5}$--$10^{7}$ per 100\,ml, whereas a greater soil-moisture deficit reduced concentrations to about $10^{4}$ per 100\,ml \parencite{Collins2007}. Peak drain-flow \textit{Campylobacter} exceeded $10^{3}$ per 100\,ml following effluent application at negligible soil-moisture deficit, and where preferential flow occurred, drainage concentrations approached those of the applied effluent. Over reported drainage events, \textit{E.\ coli} yield from a 1080\,m$^{2}$ plot ranged between $10^{8}$ and $5\times10^{10}$; installing subsurface drains on Pallic Soils reduced surface runoff by 60--80\%, redirecting water (and its microbial load) to the subsurface pathway \parencite{Collins2007}. Winter reuse of undisinfected wastewater in Ohio showed the same water-table dependence: during a non-irrigated control winter, \textit{E.\ coli} was detected in 2 of 6 subsurface samples when the water table was within the drain depth and briefly after it dropped below the drains; during the winter irrigated season, \textit{E.\ coli} was detected three times, always when the water table rose above the drain under cold, wet soil conditions \parencite{Ding2021}. Soil moisture and temperature were significantly positively related at a given depth, sunlight exposure of wastewater in metal pans for 2--3\,h produced up to 3-log \textit{E.\ coli} reductions, and \textit{E.\ coli} was not detected in groundwater 15\,m from the field---leading the authors to conclude that winter wastewater reuse poses a public-health risk to surface water via subsurface drainage, with irrigation rate and disinfection as key management levers \parencite{Ding2021}. At the pathway scale, \textcite{Collins2007} further documented that direct herd crossings of the Sherry River temporarily raised \textit{E.\ coli} to more than 100 times background (about $10^{11}$ \textit{E.\ coli} deposited during one crossing over 25 defecation events), and estimated a 60--75\% probability of \textit{Campylobacter} infection over an irrigation season from observed groundwater concentrations.

\subsubsection{Antibiotic resistance genes and hormones carried by manure}
Manure is a reservoir of antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) that persist through drained soils into drainage water. Shotgun metagenomics identified 320 unique ARGs, with 173 in manure, 176 in soil, and 85 shared; manure treatment significantly shifted the soil and drainage-water resistomes away from the control-soil resistome \parencite{Smith2019b}. Of 88 ARGs unique to the manure resistome, 75 appeared in manure-treated soil and 69 in manure-treated effluent, and 25 showed strong persistence across at least half of samples and all time points. Manure-derived ARGs dominated the abundance in manure-treated effluent (manure-enriched 0.612, water-enriched 0.308, manure persisters 0.071), with \textit{tet}(33) the most abundant persisting sequence, rising over time to become most abundant by day 108. Whereas untreated-soil ARGs were dominated by multidrug-efflux genes, many manure-derived ARGs were associated with mobile genetic elements in food- and water-borne pathogens---evidence that manure-derived genes have strong potential to persist in soil and water in tile-drained landscapes \parencite{Smith2019b}. For steroid hormones, poultry litter did not increase the flow-weighted concentration or load of estradiol or testosterone in subsurface drainage or surface runoff relative to conventional fertiliser/control treatments; both hormones were detected in drainage and runoff but total loads were generally not different among treatments, soil concentrations declined with depth (indicating stratification), and mean bulk-soil testosterone was greater in poultry-litter-amended plots while soil estradiol did not differ \parencite{Jenkins2009}.

\subsection{Organic Amendments and Soil Amelioration in Drained Soils}

\subsubsection{Manure and organic fertiliser for saline--sodic reclamation}
Farm manure is an effective amendment for reclaiming saline--sodic soils. In Pakistan, active desalinisation and desodication occurred in all treatments with non-significant differences among them; after the final wheat crop the 1.2\,m profile reached ECe $\approx 7\pm0.5$\,dS\,m$^{-1}$ and SAR $\approx 15\pm2$, indicating comparable amelioration by phytoremediation, gypsum, sulphuric acid, and farm manure \parencite{Qadir2001}. Farm manure nonetheless produced the best yields, giving 4188\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ for the first wheat crop versus 3462\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ (100\% gypsum), 2854\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ (phytoremediation), 2786\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ (sulphuric acid), and 2754\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$ (50\% gypsum); infiltration rate declined by about 18\% (from $2.03\times10^{-6}$ to $1.67\times10^{-6}$\,m\,s$^{-1}$) with non-significant treatment differences, and phytoremediation required minimal capital input \parencite{Qadir2001}. Under monsoonal Russian conditions, drainage and organic fertiliser had stronger effects on soil pore volume than biochar, with organic fertiliser increasing porosity in 2019 \parencite{Brikmans2025}. A North Dakota case study showed that a legacy of combined subsurface drainage, no-till, gypsum, and cover crops protected crops from salinisation through soil processes not revealed by soil indices alone: in the soil-health-legacy zone, 6 of 9 chemical, 5 of 14 microbial, and none of 3 physical properties differed between areas with dead/damaged plants and living plants, with higher ECe, Olsen-P, Ca, Na, and SAR (and lower pH) where plants were dying, and lower protein, POXC, arbuscular-mycorrhizal fungi, and G+:G- ratio \parencite{Daigh2025}.

\subsubsection{Biochar combined with subsurface drainage on saline--alkali soils}
A consistent body of Chinese coastal-soil experiments shows that pairing biochar with subsurface drainage improves saline--alkali soils beyond either practice alone. Subsurface drainage plus biochar (B-S) significantly reduced soil electrical conductivity and water content relative to no drainage, and increased soil total nitrogen, available phosphorus, nitrate, and organic matter relative to both control and drainage-alone; B-S raised alkaline phosphatase and urease activity, increased bacterial alpha diversity (Chao1, Shannon), and shifted community composition (Proteobacteria dominant), with available phosphorus and organic matter identified as dominant drivers of enzyme activity and bacterial abundance \parencite{Tian2023b}. Narrower drain spacing and higher biochar rates lowered bulk density and raised porosity, field capacity, and soil moisture in the plow layer, affected the entire 0--80\,cm profile, and reduced salinity---with drainage a stronger salinity control than biochar; the S1C3 combination (6\,m spacing, 15\,t\,ha$^{-1}$ biochar) gave the most satisfactory salt reduction and, with the S1 spacing, the largest yield gains \parencite{Liu2025f}. Using $^{15}$N-labelled urea, \textcite{Xiong2025} showed that narrower spacing and more biochar promoted $^{15}$N absorption across all three alfalfa harvests (drainage having the more persistent effect); S1C3 produced the highest uptake (6.8, 15.0, 12.8\,kg\,ha$^{-1}$) and raised $^{15}$N use efficiency to 46.0\% (versus 21.6--28.4\% in controls), with 21.6--46.0\% of applied $^{15}$N taken up by crops, 38.6--67.5\% retained in soil, and 8.5--18.1\% lost. In contrast, biochar's physical benefits in clay-loam soils were transient: dose-dependent decreases in bulk and aggregate density in 2018 dissipated by 2019, total porosity showed no sustained biochar effect, and intra-aggregate pore structure (persistent mesopore dominance) resisted amendment, consistent with biochar occupying inter-aggregate spaces temporarily rather than reshaping intra-aggregate pores \parencite{Brikmans2025}.

\subsection{Soil Physical and Hydraulic Properties under Drainage and Management}

Management practices exert clearer effects on soil structure than tile drainage does in several settings. In South Dakota silty clay loam, no-till significantly increased bulk density, soil organic matter (SOM), and wet aggregate stability (WAS) while decreasing field-saturated hydraulic conductivity (Kfs) relative to conventional tillage; more diverse rotations decreased bulk density and increased SOM and WAS; and cover crops and tile drainage had minimal or no significant impact on SOM, bulk density, WAS, or Kfs \parencite{Saha2024}. The tillage$\times$rotation interaction was significant (e.g., higher Kfs under conventional tillage than no-till in the 2-year rotation), bulk density was negatively correlated with SOM and WAS, SOM was strongly negatively correlated with pH, WAS was positively correlated with SOM, and stacking no-till with cover crops had no significant added effect \parencite{Saha2024}. Grass ley consistently improved surface aggregate stability of both drained and undrained soils relative to spring barley underseeded with clover, with improvement significantly correlated with rising soil organic carbon; the grass/drained treatment gave the greatest structural improvement, while the grass/undrained treatment matched it only when the soil was dry, and aggregate stability varied seasonally (lower in early spring, higher in fall) with soil water content \parencite{Hermawan1996}. On an Australian sodic soil, a drainage system functioned adequately (rapid post-rainfall drainage, clear drainage water), sunflower seed yield averaged 1.2\,t\,ha$^{-1}$ and was unaffected by drainage or gypsum, but wheat establishment was much lower on the undrained fallow, no-gypsum treatment (45 versus 214 plants\,m$^{-2}$); gypsum raised EC by $\sim$0.2\,dS\,m$^{-1}$, lowered pH from 5.8 to 5.5, minimised dispersed clay, and air-filled porosity was lowest on fallow, no-gypsum plots (8.3\% versus 15.1\% after sunflowers) \parencite{GARDNER1991}. Drainage itself altered texture and pore structure in a Russian silt loam, increasing the silt fraction, reducing the sand fraction, and driving opposite seasonal shifts in pore volume \parencite{Brikmans2025}.

\subsection{Perennial Crops, Rotations, and Nutrient/Water Loads in Tile Drainage}

Introducing perennials into annual rotations reduces subsurface nutrient export, with the strongest benefits during high-loading periods. A before--after control--impact (BACI) analysis in Ohio found that alfalfa significantly affected mean monthly subsurface discharge at Site A ($F=4.3$, $p=.04$) but not Site B; alfalfa significantly reduced subsurface NO$_3$-N, TN, and DRP loads at Site A but not Site B, significantly reduced TP at both sites, and reduced the variability of discharge and nutrient loads (smaller post-planting IQR increases in impact fields) \parencite{Arrueta2022}. Seasonally, alfalfa lowered median weekly NO$_3$-N loads in spring and winter at Site A while showing no significant seasonal effect at Site B \parencite{Arrueta2022}. A storm-event analysis at the same sites---grouping events into dry, wet, and large types---found alfalfa had little effect on discharge but significantly lowered subsurface NO$_3$-N and TN across all event types at Site A; during large events, DRP and TP loads were lower in the alfalfa field at \emph{both} sites, identifying large storms as the most important period for phosphorus-export reduction \parencite{Arrueta2025}. Collectively these results support partial, targeted conversion of marginal cropland to perennial cover to cut nutrient loads (especially dissolved and particulate P) and increase system resilience, ideally alongside reduced fertiliser rates and gypsum \parencite{Arrueta2022,Arrueta2025}. Drainage adequacy is itself indexed by perennial performance: on a sloping Cabot silt loam, subsurface drains (especially at 30.5\,m spacing) improved alfalfa growth and survival, percent alfalfa in summer cuttings correlated with plant density ($r=0.93$), percent winterkill correlated with spring ($r=0.79$) and fall ($r=0.84$) soil water, and draining to the 0.1-bar water content rendered winterkill negligible ($\approx$10\%), such that 30.5\,m spacing would ensure $\geq$50\% survival under severe conditions \parencite{BENOIT1967}. At the catchment scale, crop composition modulated phosphorus loss: summer crops occupied 63\% of the agricultural area and winter crops 34\%, summer-rape area correlated positively with summer P loss, winter-wheat area correlated strongly negatively with summer P load, grassland area correlated with winter P load, and no significant correlation linked N load to crop area \parencite{DambenieceMigliniece2018}. Long-term fertilisation and liming further shaped productivity: unlimed soil acidity changed with fertiliser rate (from $+0.3$ pH units unfertilised to $-1.1$ units at the highest rate over 34 years), liming raised pH to about 6.0--6.4, and fertiliser rate was the main control on perennial-grass productivity and sward botanical composition \parencite{Vigovskis2016}.

\subsection{Microbial Communities in Drainage Systems and Denitrifying Bioreactors}

Microbial communities in drained soils, bioreactors, and wetlands are spatially structured, temporally dynamic, and strongly shaped by the contemporary environment. In agricultural soil, the general bacterial community showed significant spatial structure by depth and transect, whereas the denitrifying community did not; directional geostatistics gave bacterial correlation ranges of 5.6\,m overall, 10.4\,m along the flow path, and 10.7\,m at 1.52\,m depth, supporting efficient sampling design (formalised in the \texttt{COMMUNITYCORRELOGRAM} R package) \parencite{Andrus2014}. In denitrifying bioreactors, depth and season shaped bacterial, fungal, and denitrifying communities; communities at 1.52\,m were more temporally variable than at 0.76\,m; bacterial communities cycled with a $\sim$125-day correlation time and roughly annual periodicity; denitrifiers recurred at $\sim$300 days; and fungi were more stable ($\sim$150--200-day autocorrelation, no significant annual cycling), with seasonal flow/moisture (Jan--Jun) and temperature/precipitation (Jul--Dec) gradients evident \parencite{Porter2015}. Comparing habitats, bacterial and clade-I \textit{nosZ} denitrifier communities were distinct among soils, bioreactors, and wetlands (PERMANOVA: significant location and habitat effects), with bioreactor communities more similar to wetlands than to fields---evidence that favourable contemporary environmental conditions can be engineered to develop effective denitrifier assemblages \parencite{Hathaway2015}. In a Canadian agricultural stream network, source locations (tile drains and the drainage ditch) had the highest N and P cycling functional potential and the most abundant nitrification/denitrification genes, storm sampling was associated with elevated nutrient concentrations and functional capacity, archaeal N-cycling capacity and NH$_4$-N rose after harvest, microbial P-mobilisation potential tracked P availability, and water passing through the wetland generally showed reduced nutrient concentrations and functional capacity, though the wetland response was variable \parencite{Knorr2023}.

\subsection{Drainage Water Treatment: Wetlands, Reservoirs, and Reconstructed Oxbows}

Engineered receiving features can substantially attenuate drainage-borne nitrate, though their effectiveness for pathogens is more variable. A reconstructed Iowa floodplain oxbow fed primarily by tile drainage retained nitrate with May--September efficiencies of 44\% (2014) and 47\% (2015) by N:Cl ratio, with retention greatest in late summer/early fall (monthly maxima of 92\% in August and 78\% in September 2015); nitrate concentrations were highest in tile water, similar in upgradient groundwater and the oxbow, and lowest downgradient, and groundwater contributed only a small fraction of discharge---placing oxbow performance on par with bioreactors, wetlands, and saturated buffers as a viable tile-drainage BMP \parencite{Schilling2017}. A Canadian water-and-reservoir-for-irrigation (WRI) constructed wetland reduced mass loads by 67.6\% for nitrate-nitrogen and 63.3\% for \textit{E.\ coli}; outflow nitrate averaged 1.2\,mg\,L$^{-1}$ (below the drinking-water guideline but exceeding the aquatic-life guideline on 25\,d), outflow \textit{E.\ coli} averaged 178\,CFU\,100\,mL$^{-1}$ (exceeding the irrigation guideline on day 119, higher in the warm season), measured residence time was 15.0\,d (design 14.5\,d), and areal uptake rates were 8.0\,m\,y$^{-1}$ (nitrate-N) and 6.4\,m\,y$^{-1}$ (\textit{E.\ coli}); the wetland captured 15{,}600\,m$^{3}$ (14{,}064\,m$^{3}$ after evapotranspiration, $\approx$780\,mm over 1.8\,ha), and a relatively small wetland (5.7\% of drainage area) sufficed \parencite{Haverstock2017}. Not all wetlands attenuate pathogens, however: a New Zealand wetland was consistently a net exporter of \textit{E.\ coli}, with loads increasing by a median 10.2-fold (range 1.5--26$\times$) across ten flow events (median inflow $3.8\times10^{8}$ versus outflow $2.5\times10^{9}$\,MPN/event), and monthly monitoring likewise showed higher outflow than inflow concentrations \parencite{Stott2023}. Selenium-removal wetlands treating drainage water in California retained about 59\% of inflow Se (outflow 35\%, seepage 4\%, volatilisation 2\%), achieving 21--55\% concentration reduction and 48--76\% mass removal in 2000, with the nonvegetated cell least effective---indicating that vegetation-promoted reducing conditions and retention in the organic detrital layer and surface sediment drive removal \parencite{Gao2003}.

\subsection{Trace Elements and Salinity in Agricultural Drainage Water}

\subsubsection{Selenium and the Kesterson legacy}
The Kesterson Reservoir remains the definitive case of trace-element hazard from agricultural drainage. Waterborne selenium at Kesterson averaged 0.122\,mg\,L$^{-1}$---about 230 times the $<$0.001\,mg\,L$^{-1}$ reference at Volta---and mean tissue selenium ran 10--200 times higher, biomagnifying from water to sediment to plants to animals; detritus reached 440\,$\mu$g\,g$^{-1}$ dry weight and some mosquitofish 370\,$\mu$g\,g$^{-1}$, levels judged probably toxic to sensitive consumers and consistent with the observed dead and deformed bird embryos \parencite{SAIKI1987}. \textcite{Ohlendorf2002} documented that 40\% of 347 nests in 1983 held one or more dead embryos and 20\% held severely deformed embryos or chicks; across 1983--1985 at least 39\% of 578 nests contained a dead or deformed embryo; bird-liver selenium was $\sim$10$\times$ and egg selenium 20--30$\times$ the Volta reference; food-chain organisms averaged 20--332\,$\mu$g\,g$^{-1}$ dry weight ($\sim$100$\times$ Volta); inflow selenium averaged 300\,$\mu$g\,L$^{-1}$; and coots showed mortality, reproductive failure, and selenium-toxicosis lesions. These integrated field and laboratory studies fulfilled the equivalent of Koch's postulates, identified selenium as the sole causative agent, and are regarded as a gold standard of retrospective ecological risk assessment \parencite{Ohlendorf2002}. Remediation drew on natural attenuation: groundwater beneath Kesterson was protected by biogeochemical immobilisation (selenium exceeded 10\,$\mu$g\,L$^{-1}$ in only 16 wells in 1985, clustered in two areas, within a $\sim$650\,ha drainage plume fed by $\sim$430\,ha-m\,yr$^{-1}$ of seepage during 1981--1986), and a risk-based strategy left the selenium inventory in place and filled low-lying areas to prevent wildlife-attracting ephemeral pools \parencite{BENSON1993}.

\subsubsection{Arsenic and uranium in evaporation ponds}
In agricultural evaporation ponds, evapoconcentration and redox chemistry jointly control trace-element mobility. Total dissolved arsenic rose from 108--132\,$\mu$g\,L$^{-1}$ at the central inlet to as high as 2{,}099\,$\mu$g\,L$^{-1}$ toward terminal cells; As increased with electrical conductivity yet arsenate As(V) remained dominant ($>$67\% of dissolved As in all cells, $>$95\% at the inlet), while reducing conditions toward terminal cells and in deeper layers lowered dissolved oxygen and nitrate and raised As(III), Fe(II), and sulfide \parencite{Ryu2010}. Sequential extraction showed carbonate-associated As as the largest sediment sink under both oxidising and reducing conditions, with soluble and adsorbed fractions much larger in the reduced terminal cell, so arsenic mobility was controlled by evaporation and by high-DOC reducing conditions \parencite{Ryu2010}. \textcite{Gao2007} corroborated this: pond-water As increased linearly with chloride (behaving more conservatively than selenium), speciation shifted toward As(III) and organic/methylated forms along the flow path and in reducing cells, sediments accumulated As but not strongly enough to prevent rising dissolved concentrations, and DOC increased with chloride and reducing conditions. Uranium showed analogous behaviour: sediment U decreased abruptly with depth (concentrated at the surface), was greatest in the most saline ponds, and correlated with sediment total organic carbon, implying association with algal organic matter; the measured Pond-14 K$_d$ of $\sim$50 far exceeded the isotherm-predicted 2, salt crusts held up to 5.8\,mg\,U\,kg$^{-1}$, and 75\% of surface-sediment U was oxidised versus 25\% reduced \parencite{Duff1997}.

\subsubsection{Boron and micronutrient balances}
Boron transport in agroforestry soils was successfully simulated by a two-dimensional finite-element model (mean absolute error 1.5\,mg\,L$^{-1}$, relative error 6.5\%), which reproduced Mendota field data for 1990 and 1992 and captured the effects of Langmuir constants: higher adsorption equilibrium constant, maximum adsorptive capacity, or root-absorption coefficient lowered soil-solution boron, while higher irrigation-water boron raised it, and adsorption was greater in silt loam than in sandy or clay loam \parencite{Tayfur2010}. Boron isotopes offer a manure/fertiliser tracer: hog manure held 0.4--8.2\,mg\,L$^{-1}$ B (mean 2.9) with $\delta^{11}$B of 7.2--11.2\textperthousand, cattle-feedlot runoff averaged 23.2\textperthousand, and Cobb River subsurface drainage averaged 0.06\,mg\,L$^{-1}$ B with $\delta^{11}$B of 5.3--15.1\textperthousand; the comparatively narrow hog-manure range makes $\delta^{11}$B useful for tracing feedlot contamination and mixing between water sources \parencite{Komor1997}. Micronutrient budgets can run negative under intensive drainage: in Pakistan, drainage-water removal of Cu, Fe, Mn, and Zn exceeded inputs from irrigation and fertiliser at both sites, giving negative balances and net soil depletion; Cu and Mn losses exceeded Fe and Zn (Zn least), losses were generally greater in maize (Kharif) than wheat (Rabi), and extractable concentrations rose with depth \parencite{Gul2013}.

\subsubsection{Major ions and salinity}
The ionic composition of central-Iowa drainage water was dominated (in decreasing order) by bicarbonate, calcium, magnesium, nitrate, chloride, sodium, and sulfate \parencite{Zimmerman2017b}. Bicarbonate, calcium, and magnesium were higher post-growing season while nitrate and chloride were higher in-season; corn-based systems had greater magnesium and chloride and lower calcium than soybean; bicarbonate correlated positively with calcium and negatively with nitrate and chloride; and flow, pH, and temperature did not differ among cropping systems, so they did not explain the ionic differences \parencite{Zimmerman2017b}. Salinity itself drove nonlinear soil responses in a North Dakota case study, where NO$_3$-N increased nonlinearly and Olsen-P linearly with salinity, while POXC, protein, and arbuscular-mycorrhizal fungi decreased \parencite{Daigh2025}; and subsurface drainage proved a stronger salinity control than biochar across the 0--80\,cm profile in coastal Chinese soils \parencite{Liu2025f}.

\subsection{Ecological Responses to Subsurface Drainage}

\subsubsection{Soil fauna}
Subsurface drainage patterns measurably shape earthworm populations. \textit{Lumbricus terrestris} abundance was typically higher above drains than between them---above the pipes the median individual count was twice as high and total fresh mass five times as high, with mean differences of 2.5 individuals\,m$^{-2}$ (95\% CI 1.0--4.0) and 6.6\,g\,m$^{-2}$ (95\% CI 3.6--9.6)---and stained burrows reached the 1\,m drain depth and below, so drainage pattern is a factor affecting population density and should inform sampling design (with worm activity potentially affecting subdrain function) \parencite{Nuutinen2001}. A long-term inoculation study confirmed both the above-drain preference (significantly higher densities above tile lines under no-till and ploughing) and strong management and dispersal gradients: by 2009 density reached 41 individuals\,m$^{-2}$ near margins versus 13 inside constantly unploughed plots, plot margins exceeded no-till which exceeded ploughing, density and fresh weight declined with distance from the inoculation area (14 to 1 individuals\,m$^{-2}$ from 5--9\,m to 56--60\,m), and an indicative colonisation rate of 4.6\,m\,yr$^{-1}$ was estimated---underscoring that undisturbed margin habitat and long-term ($>$10-year) monitoring are decisive for evaluating inoculation success \parencite{Nuutinen2011}.

\subsubsection{Birds, amphibians, and semi-aquatic species}
Drainage type is associated with farmland-bird outcomes. In this first long-term, large-scale Finnish comparison, skylark populations increased significantly on open-drained fields (ODF) but were stable on subsurface-drained fields (SDF); meadow pipit increased significantly on ODF (its SDF decline non-significant) and bred in significantly greater numbers on ODF; both species favoured ODF over SDF, with abundance not differing statistically between drainage types overall but territories fewer on arable than grassland fields \parencite{Marja2013}. Tile-drainage-associated pesticides posed hazards to amphibians: tile wetlands had cumulative pesticide concentrations 4.7 times higher than reference wetlands (neonicotinoids dominant), with clothianidin $\sim$8$\times$ and thiamethoxam nearly 9$\times$ higher (thiamethoxam detected at all tile wetlands and no reference wetlands); juvenile frogs from tile wetlands carried $\sim$2.5$\times$ more imidacloprid in brain tissue and nearly 2$\times$ more desnitro-imidacloprid in liver, and reference-wetland frogs had slightly longer standardised cerebellum lengths---the first field quantification of imidacloprid in juvenile amphibian brains and evidence that these chemicals can alter brain structure \parencite{Campbell2023}. Subsurface drainage of meadows was also identified, alongside ditch cleaning and gyro-crushing, as a leading cause of decline in the semi-aquatic snails \textit{Galba truncatula} and \textit{Omphiscola glabra}, whose occupied habitats fell by 30\% and 38\% respectively by 2016--2017, with the largest losses in vernal pools and significant reductions in drainage-furrow habitat area \parencite{Dreyfuss2018}.

\subsubsection{Vegetation and restored aquatic habitats}
Over 24 years, drained UK grassland shifted from a \textit{Lolium perenne}-dominated community with \textit{Juncus} patches toward one of \textit{Lolium perenne} and \textit{Trifolium repens}; plant diversity depended on topography and spatial effects rather than drainage, total N and C generally decreased with drainage and depth (but only interactively), the C/N ratio was drainage-independent, and $\delta^{13}$C depended significantly on drainage (depleted in drained plots) whereas $\delta^{15}$N did not \parencite{Harris2018}. In restored Iowa oxbows, tile drainage had negligible effects: physical variables did not differ significantly between multipurpose (tiled) and non-tiled oxbows, no biotic variable differed, and macrophyte, macroinvertebrate, and fish assemblages were similar (44 invertebrate taxa, 35 fish species, and 2{,}682 Topeka shiners recorded), providing preliminary but promising evidence that multipurpose oxbows can enhance biodiversity without ecological penalty \parencite{Leberg2024}. Wetland restoration outcomes improved with sediment excavation, which initially reduced invasive cover (notably hybrid cattail and reed canary grass) and increased diversity and evenness, though the invasive-suppression benefit was lost by $\sim$6 years; community composition differed significantly by wetland age, with younger and excavated wetlands favouring native perennials and submerged macrophytes \parencite{Winikoff2020}.

\subsection{Amendments, Cover Management, and Nutrient Retention: Additional Practices}

Two further practice classes complete the picture. Polyacrylamide (PAM) reduced downward phosphorus transport in soil columns, with the 0.20\% treatment optimal (cumulative soluble-P loss 348 versus 541\,mg\,tube$^{-1}$; mean soluble-P concentration 87 versus 138\,$\mu$g\,ml$^{-1}$ in the control); in field plots all PAM treatments significantly cut runoff volume and total-P loss and significantly reduced leachate volume to tile drains, though total-P losses in leachate were not significantly different (attributed to low surface-application rate and high variability); PAM alone maximised wheat growth and plant-P uptake, whereas PAM plus calcium amendments best reduced runoff-P losses \parencite{Jiang2010}. In Spanish citrus, combining anti-weed mesh with a drainage system improved vegetative growth, flower production, and yield relative to the traditional control: netted treatments generally produced larger canopies (the largest canopy volume 2256.17\,L and trunk diameter 40.38\,mm under one treatment) and higher juice yield, while the uncovered control produced the heaviest individual fruits; metabolomic profiling showed a shift from 27 to 25 detected metabolites between seasons (proline the most abundant amino acid, quinic acid the most abundant organic acid), with certain netted-and-drained treatments best maintaining metabolomic homeostasis, and the authors recommend anti-weed mesh for commercial plantations in water-scarce regions for its reduced water, herbicide, and labour costs \parencite{GiménezValero2024,GiménezValero2025}.

17.body:
\subsection{Overview}
The timing of runoff and drainage events relative to herbicide application is the single most powerful control on peak concentrations and mass losses observed in both surface runoff and subsurface (tile) drainage. Synthesising more than thirty North American field studies, \textcite{Kladivko2001} established the consolidated picture that frames this theme: pesticides are commonly detected in subsurface drainflow during the first drainage event(s) after application, concentrations usually decline rapidly during that event or in successive events, and most mass losses are very small---generally less than 0.5\% and often less than 0.1\% of applied. Because subsurface drainage lowers runoff and sediment losses, pesticide concentrations and mass losses in tile drainage are usually much lower than in surface runoff, frequently by an order of magnitude \parencite{Kladivko2001}. The evidence base spans plot- and field-scale monitoring, catchment-scale mass balances, controlled tracer experiments, and numerous simulation models, with strong convergence on several mechanisms: preferential (macropore) flow delivers a fraction of applied compound to drains almost immediately after application; early events dominate the seasonal loss; and year-to-year weather variability governs whether substantial losses occur at all \parencite{Kladivko2001}. Against this well-supported backdrop, one methodological gap is explicit in the literature: there are no reports quantifying how flux-averaged pesticide concentrations in drainage water differ from concentrations between the drains, so evidence-based conclusions on that specific comparison cannot yet be drawn \parencite{Jacobsen2007}.

\subsection{Timing of loss relative to application and drainage onset}

\subsubsection{First drainage events and rapid concentration decline}
The recurring observation across sites is that runoff events shortly after application produce the greatest herbicide concentrations and losses in both surface runoff and subsurface drainage \parencite{GAYNOR1995}. In Louisiana, atrazine and metolachlor concentrations in runoff declined rapidly during the first 34 days after application and then remained consistently low (below 10~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$) from day 54 to season end; early runoff events contributed disproportionately to seasonal loss, and most atrazine and metolachlor loss occurred within the first 61 days after application \parencite{SOUTHWICK1990}. In Illinois tile systems, most atrazine loss occurred within three months of application, and higher application rates increased subsurface losses \parencite{Kalita2006}. Catchment-scale work reinforces this: more than 80\% of total herbicide losses from a whole catchment occurred during just two rain events, 23 and 28 days after a controlled application \parencite{Leu2004}. In France, applying pesticides after the start of drainage increased the exported ratio for all compounds studied, and annual exported flux was highly correlated with maximum drainwater concentration for all four herbicides \parencite{Julien2023}.

Late-season losses are comparatively minor but occur primarily through tile discharge: in Ontario, up to 7\% of applied atrazine and 5\% of applied metolachlor was lost when runoff occurred within a week of application, whereas late-season losses were only 0.1 to 2.0\% and moved mainly via drains \parencite{GAYNOR1995}. Tracer experiments confirm near-instantaneous arrival: bromide and both herbicides produced breakthrough curves typical of preferential flow, with all chemicals arriving at tile drains at the same time, and even the first drainage after application contained all three compounds \parencite{Fortin2002}. Similarly, herbicides first arrived at drains after less than 1~cm of net drainage, interpreted as rapid transport through about 3\% of the pore volume \parencite{Dousset2004}.

\subsubsection{Seasonal partitioning, snowmelt, and carry-over timing}
Timing is not confined to the days after application. In a boreal Finnish study, none of the herbicides were detected in subsurface drainage during the first 200 days; ethofumesate and glufosinate-ammonium were detected only in April, and most herbicide loss occurred during snowmelt and soil thaw in spring rather than immediately after application \parencite{Siimes2006}. In structured French soils, metolachlor losses followed two seasonal phases: high concentrations in relatively small spring/summer drainage volumes, and large autumn/winter drainage volumes with low concentrations \parencite{Novak2001}. In the Vertic Cambisol, 91\% of metolachlor losses occurred within three months of treatment in 1996/97, compared with 32\% on the Stagnic Luvisol \parencite{Novak2001}. Comparable two-phase behaviour appeared elsewhere: preferential flow during spring--summer with fast herbicide migration, and matrix flow during fall--winter with slower migration \parencite{Dousset2004}. In the Paris basin, triazine herbicides stayed in the first 40~cm of soil during dry periods and moved downward mainly during the first water infiltrations when drains resumed working in autumn and winter \parencite{Garmouma1998}. Herbicide detections can also carry over between seasons: residual dinoseb appeared in tile outflow in spring and again in fall at a site where none was applied that year, with carry-over confirmed for up to 23 months at one site while another showed no measurable residue after 42 months \parencite{ONEILL1989}. Field studies leaving pesticide selection and application to the farmer, with rainfall dictating drainage, provide realistic timing information of this kind \parencite{ONEILL1989}.

\subsection{Magnitude of mass losses in drainage versus surface runoff}
Quantitatively, mass losses to tile drainage are consistently small. \textcite{Kladivko2001} report that losses are usually below 0.5\% and often below 0.1\% of applied, the main exception being a Woodslee, Ontario site with herbicide losses as high as 3\%. Site-specific values corroborate this range. In Illinois, long-term average atrazine losses were 1.13, 1.68, 1.48, and 0.93~g~ha$^{-1}$ at four sites, corresponding to only 0.14\%, 0.15\%, 0.22\%, and 0.11\% of application \parencite{Kalita2006}. In New York plot studies, mass loss to drainage was much smaller for herbicides than for chloride: chloride loss was 1.3\% of applied, whereas 2,4-D loss was 0.08\% and atrazine loss was 0.02\% \parencite{Shalit1996}. In Minnesota tile water, mean atrazine concentrations ranged from about 0.29 to 1.24~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ across 1986--1991 with estimated annual losses of only 0.4 to 4.1~g~ha$^{-1}$ \parencite{BUHLER1993}. Tracer comparisons showed less than 1\% of applied herbicide recovered in tile drains over two years, even though 3.2 to 28.2\% of applied bromide was recovered \parencite{Fortin2002}. In Finland, ethofumesate, glufosinate-ammonium, and glyphosate losses to runoff over 302 days were 1.0\%, 0.2\%, and 0.1\% of applied \parencite{Siimes2006}. Annual herbicide losses in Ontario ranged from below 0.3 to 2.7\% of application \parencite{Gaynor2001}.

The magnitude contrast between drainage and surface runoff is a robust finding. Because subsurface drainage lowers runoff and sediment losses, pesticide concentrations and mass losses are usually much lower in tile drainage than in surface runoff, often by an order of magnitude \parencite{Kladivko2001}. In Louisiana, seasonal runoff losses of both herbicides were less than half from plots with subsurface drains than from undrained plots, mainly because of lower runoff volume with a smaller contribution from lower first-event concentrations \parencite{SOUTHWICK1990}; for the first runoff event, concentrations were 65\% greater for atrazine and 60\% greater for metolachlor from undrained plots \parencite{SOUTHWICK1990}. Early irrigated-field work reached the same conclusion: tailwater contained much higher residue concentrations than tile drainage effluent (ratios up to about 68:1 during one flooding period), and the total residue in tile effluent did not exceed the quantity applied in the irrigation water alone \parencite{JOHNSTON1967}. An open drain collecting both surface and subsurface waters carried residues about ten times those in tile effluent \parencite{JOHNSTON1967}.

\subsection{Preferential and macropore flow as the dominant transport mechanism}
The mechanism reconciling near-immediate arrival with small overall losses is preferential (macropore) flow. \textcite{Kladivko2001} conclude that preferential flow is the most likely mechanism for pesticide movement to tile drains soon after application. Tracer experiments make this explicit: breakthrough curves for bromide and herbicides were typical of preferential flow, atrazine and metolachlor concentration peaks occurred earlier than bromide peaks, and 50\% of herbicide relative cumulative mass was reached after less than 50~mm cumulative drainage, whereas bromide required a much larger share of cumulative drainage \parencite{Fortin2002}. Rapid arrival after less than 1~cm of net drainage indicated transport through only about 3\% of the pore volume \parencite{Dousset2004}, and more residues were detected from the silty clay soil than the silt loam, attributed to preferential flow and more intensive drainage \parencite{Dousset2004}.

Soil structure, rather than sorption or degradation, dominated the difference between soils in several studies. Total metolachlor losses were greater in the clayey Vertic Cambisol than in the loamy Stagnic Luvisol, with peak drainwater concentrations consistently higher in the Vertic Cambisol; the authors conclude that differences were much more influenced by soil structure and macropore flow than by adsorption or degradation \parencite{Novak2001}. Modelling supported this: macroporosity had a much greater effect on bromide and metolachlor losses in the Vertic Cambisol, where losses were 2.1 and 1.3 times greater when macroporosity was set at 10\% than when not simulated, and macroporosity and sorption kinetics were identified as the main factors controlling metolachlor transport \parencite{Novak2003}. In Iowa, atrazine concentrations were higher in one plot than others, suggesting preferential movement, with deethylatrazine-to-atrazine ratios indicating shorter residence time and minimal microbial contact where the greatest concentrations occurred \parencite{JAYACHANDRAN1994}. At catchment scale, the largest spatial differences in herbicide losses were linked mainly to fast transport mechanisms---surface runoff and preferential flow to tile drains---rather than to compound properties alone, with topography, soil permeability, subsoil texture, and the location of drainage systems identified as key controls \parencite{Leu2004}. Where nonequilibrium transport prevailed, the equilibrium advection-dispersion equation failed to reproduce drainwater concentrations, whereas a mobile--immobile (MIM) nonequilibrium model gave much better agreement across three sites, and a silty clay soil required modified hydraulic functions to represent preferential flow through drying cracks \parencite{Boivin2006}.

\subsection{Concentrations, peaks, and exceedance of water-quality thresholds}
Although mass losses are small, transient concentrations can be high and can exceed regulatory thresholds. In Ontario, maximum concentrations reached 763 and 721~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ for atrazine and metolachlor in surface runoff and 287 and 210~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ in tile discharge; the USEPA atrazine maximum contaminant level (MCL) was exceeded in 74\% of surface runoff samples and 60\% of tile drainage samples from conventional tillage, and metolachlor exceeded its health advisory level in 4 to 14\% of samples \parencite{GAYNOR1995}. In Illinois, long-term flow-weighted atrazine concentrations were below 3~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ for most events but occasionally reached about 25~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ at three sites and about 90~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ at a fourth \parencite{Kalita2006}. In Iowa, atrazine concentration exceeded the drinking-water MCL in more than 40\% of samples, and total atrazine residue (atrazine plus degradates) could exceed the MCL even when atrazine alone was below 3~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ \parencite{JAYACHANDRAN1994}. Glyphosate work in a multi-basin study recorded the highest measured glyphosate concentration (430~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$) at an overland-flow site, while an Iowa subsurface drain had higher median concentrations than surface-water sites and maxima above 250~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ \parencite{Coupe2012}. Persistent low-level occurrence is also documented: atrazine was detected in 97\% of Minnesota tile samples across 1985--1990 and 78\% in 1991, versus alachlor in only a handful of samples \parencite{BUHLER1993}. Toxic thresholds were exceeded episodically for other compounds, with dinoseb in tile outflow exceeding the maximum acceptable toxic concentration of 0.5~$\mu$g~L$^{-1}$ on nineteen occasions \parencite{ONEILL1989}.

\subsection{Degradates, metabolites, and residual persistence}
Herbicide degradates contribute meaningfully to the loss signal and can be more mobile than parent compounds. Deethylatrazine was found in both surface runoff and tile drainage and accounted for 10 to 27\% of combined atrazine and deethylatrazine loss \parencite{GAYNOR1995}, and desethyl atrazine comprised 7 to 39\% of total triazine loss in a related study \parencite{Gaynor2001}. N-de-ethylated atrazine and cyanazine II moved through the soil profile into subsurface drainage in greater proportions than their parents, and N-de-ethylated atrazine persisted at 0.015--0.02~ppm twelve months after application \parencite{MUIR1978}. In Iowa, atrazine, deisopropylatrazine (DIA), and deethylatrazine (DEA) were consistently detected in tile drainage and shallow groundwater, with DEA generally greater than DIA \parencite{JAYACHANDRAN1994}. Parent persistence varies widely: atrazine dissipation in surface soil was biphasic, with rapid loss over the first 60 days followed by very slow dissipation, and detection frequency in the surface 7.5~cm exceeded 95\% over 1991--1995, whereas metribuzin dissipated much faster (first-order half-lives of 15 and 21 days) and was rarely detected after one season \parencite{Moorman1999}. After atrazine use stopped in Minnesota, tile concentrations did not decline during the first year, then fell by more than 50\% in the second and continued to decline while remaining detectable, with residual atrazine persisting in soil 12 and 26 months after last application \parencite{BUHLER1993}. Metabolite fate can also diverge from parents under management change: predicted losses of the metolachlor degradate OXA decreased with residue removal, in contrast to increased parent-herbicide transport \parencite{Shipitalo2016}. Solubility differences drive contrasting mobility, as when lindane---about 100 times more soluble than DDT---leached readily and appeared at higher concentrations in drainage effluent \parencite{JOHNSTON1967}.

\subsection{Representativeness of flux-averaged drainage concentrations}
A distinct and less-developed line of evidence concerns whether drainage-water concentrations represent conditions in the field. \textcite{Jacobsen2007} emphasise that no literature reports quantify how the flux-averaged concentration of pesticides in drainage water differs from that found between the drains, so evidence-based conclusions on that comparison cannot be drawn. The existing literature suggests that concentration in drainage water does not always correspond to the concentration at drain depth between drains, and depending on conditions may be higher or lower \parencite{Jacobsen2007}. Representativeness is most questionable on very well-drained soils and on poorly drained soils with little capacity for lateral transport beneath the plough layer, whereas relatively porous soils and moderate climatic conditions favour representative concentrations; drainage water is also more representative for weakly sorbed than for strongly sorbed pesticides \parencite{Jacobsen2007}. Despite these caveats, drainage monitoring offers the practical advantages that samples integrate spatial variability across the whole field and that existing drains can be used without disturbing the soil or installing new equipment \parencite{Jacobsen2007}.

\subsection{Controls on variability: soils, tillage, drainage design, and weather}
The literature identifies several interacting controls on the magnitude and partitioning of loss. Weather and hydrology are paramount: year and source-by-treatment effects were more important than tillage alone, with no tillage effect detected for total atrazine loss and only one instance of metolachlor loss reduced 20\% by conservation tillage \parencite{GAYNOR1995}; year-to-year weather variation strongly controls whether and how much loss occurs \parencite{Kladivko2001}. Losses can be higher in drier years because wetter years lengthen the interval between treatment and the first drainage event \parencite{Dousset2004}.

Tillage effects on partitioning are documented but modest for total loss. Conservation tillage increased surface runoff 42\% and decreased tile discharge 15\% relative to conventional tillage, yet total field runoff was unchanged; averaged over years, herbicide concentration was 1.3 and 1.8 times greater from conservation than conventional tillage in surface runoff and tile discharge, respectively \parencite{GAYNOR1995}. In Minnesota, tillage system had minimal impact on atrazine concentration or loss in tile water, with differences between moldboard plow and no-till generally small and often not significant \parencite{BUHLER1993}. In a tracer study, mean maximum bromide concentration was significantly higher under reduced than conventional tillage (19.9 versus 10.7~mg~L$^{-1}$) in one year \parencite{Fortin2002}. Drainage design and water-table management also matter: herbicide concentrations in runoff from drained plots showed no trend with drain spacing \parencite{SOUTHWICK1990}, and drain lines intercepted a reasonable amount of dissolved pesticide and reduced concentrations below the drainage depth, with predicted concentrations lower under 30 and 60~cm water tables than under a 90~cm water table \parencite{Kalita1998}. Controlled drainage-subirrigation increased herbicide loss in surface runoff but decreased loss through tile drainage, so total loss did not differ between drainage treatments \parencite{Gaynor2001}.

\subsection{Management and remediation of herbicide loss}
Several management interventions with quantified effects emerge from the evidence. Reducing surface transport is effective: soil-saver tillage combined with a ryegrass intercrop reduced herbicide loss in tile drains and surface runoff by 46 to 49\% relative to moldboard plow in one year and had the potential to decrease total loss by at least 49\% by reducing surface transport, while band application reduced loss irrespective of environmental factors \parencite{Gaynor2001}. Practices that decrease atrazine use, such as banding or rotations, and that decrease runoff and increase atrazine retention in soil, have the potential to improve water quality and reduce subsurface losses \parencite{Moorman1999}. Application timing offers a direct lever: a soil wetness index (SWI) threshold of 85\% separated low- and higher-risk application periods, with exported ratios below 0.2\% below the threshold but reaching up to 3.23\% above it; the SWI-based restriction reduced risk metrics, was reported as more efficient than other restriction factors, and can be calculated easily from local weather and soil data \parencite{Julien2023}.

By contrast, corn stover harvest can worsen drainage quality: a 50\% residue-removal scenario, combined with reduced surface crust conductivity and macroporosity, increased atrazine and metolachlor transport to tile drains \parencite{Shipitalo2016}. Edge-of-field and end-of-pipe treatments show promise. Denitrifying woodchip bioreactors removed both nitrate and atrazine, with removal increasing with hydraulic residence time; biochar-amended bioreactors removed 93\% of atrazine at 72~h versus 55\% for woodchip-only, and atrazine removal was primarily abiotic since aeration and influent nitrate did not affect effluent atrazine \parencite{Hassanpour2019}. Wood-chip bioreactors also rapidly dissipated atrazine, enrofloxacin, and sulfamethazine, mainly by sorption, without inhibiting denitrification by atrazine \parencite{Ilhan2011}. Constructed remediation zones exploit sorption and degradation: retention capacity ranked straw $\gg$ sediments $>$ soils, with straw showing the highest adsorption coefficients and the largest non-desorbable residues \parencite{Vallée2014}, while total pesticide dissipation over 120 days ranged from 1.4\% to 100\% and constructed wetlands were shown to alternate retention during drainage and degradation during non-drainage periods \parencite{Vallée2016}. Purpose-made adsorbents are also effective: canola-stalk activated carbon achieved a maximum 2,4-D uptake of 135.8~mg~g$^{-1}$, removed 100\% of the herbicide from real agricultural drainage water, and retained 83.79\% efficiency after five regeneration cycles \parencite{Amiri2020}.

\subsection{Modelling herbicide transport in drained fields}
A substantial modelling literature supports and extends the field observations. Simplified event-based models can reproduce drainage and pesticide dynamics: PESTDRAIN simulated both surface runoff and tile-drainage flow with associated concentrations, achieving relative errors of 14\% for drainage discharge and 4\% for surface runoff and reproducing high-resolution IPU concentrations, with an exponential transfer function separating fast and slow transport \parencite{Branger2009}. Process models emphasise sorption kinetics and macroporosity: adsorption-desorption kinetics (ADK) reproduced observed metolachlor export patterns better than instantaneous reversible adsorption, and export was more sensitive to ADK rates than to degradation \parencite{Novak2003}. HYDRUS-2D simulated drain discharge well for homogeneous soils but required modified hydraulic functions and a mobile--immobile transport formulation for a cracking silty clay, with the solute mass-transfer coefficient critical for matching bentazone transport \parencite{Boivin2006}. RZWQM accurately simulated metolachlor transport and reasonably matched drainage volume, giving confidence for scenario prediction of residue-removal effects \parencite{Shipitalo2016}. At catchment scale, SWAT+ simulated discharge very well (calibration NSE 0.86, validation NSE 0.74), with performance improving with pesticide mobility; subsurface drainage was an important pathway (on average 15.7\% tile flow, rising to 21.2\% in a wet winter), and tile flow became increasingly important after application for moderately and very mobile compounds and their transformation products, whereas surface runoff dominated peak losses for non-mobile pesticides \parencite{Wendell2024}.

Rainfall resolution and process representation shape predictions. Rainfall intensity and duration affected transport in runoff more than in subsurface drainage, and hourly rainfall mattered most when rain fell shortly after application; however, for long-term mean and upper-percentile subsurface losses, temporally discrete rainfall data gave limited benefit, with less than 2\% difference in percent loss and hourly or average-duration events needed to stay within about 7\% of predicted loss \parencite{Fox2007}. Mixing-layer models successfully described drainage outflow, especially for incorporated treatments where mixing was more complete, and predicted plausible partition coefficients (1.44~cm$^3$~g$^{-1}$ for 2,4-D and 12.40~cm$^3$~g$^{-1}$ for atrazine) and a mixing-layer depth matching the plow zone \parencite{Shalit1996}. The same framework performed especially well for the preferential-flow portion of outflow and offered a way to distinguish preferential and matrix components; the ratio of outflow to mixing-layer depth can estimate the fraction of matrix participating in fast macropore flow \parencite{SHALIT1995,Shalit1996}. The ADAPT model reproduced observed atrazine and alachlor groundwater concentrations reasonably, with predicted concentrations decreasing with soil depth and with shallower water tables, demonstrating the water-quality benefit of water-table management \parencite{Kalita1998}.

\subsection{Related contaminants: biosolids-borne compounds and emerging chemicals}
Beyond conventional herbicides, tile drainage transports a broader suite of contaminants whose behaviour mirrors the timing and preferential-flow patterns above. After dewatered municipal biosolids application, selected PPCPs were detected only transiently in tile drainage and shallow groundwater, incorporated aggregates retained compounds up to one year with generally exponential decay, and the high-rate application did not significantly impact tile drainage or groundwater quality \parencite{Gottschall2012}. Hormones were detected only briefly in tile drainage at low ng~L$^{-1}$ levels while sterols such as coprostanol persisted and served as useful fecal-contamination indicators strongly related to fecal bacteria in tile water \parencite{Gottschall2013}. PBDEs and perfluoroalkyl acids were detected in tile drainage and groundwater both before and after application, with statistically significant post-application increases for several BDE congeners and substantial in-aggregate dissipation (many congeners reduced by more than 90\% within a year; half-lives about 30 to 223 days) \parencite{Gottschall2017}. A cross-study review found that surface runoff and tile-drainage concentrations of microcontaminants varied widely and were generally lower than in secondary municipal effluent, with the highest tile-drainage concentrations (above 1000~ng~L$^{-1}$) for ibuprofen, naproxen, triclosan, gemfibrozil, and carbamazepine, and with liquid biosolids moving more rapidly and producing higher, earlier tile-drain concentrations than dewatered biosolids \parencite{Ghirardini2019}. Seed-treatment neonicotinoids showed clothianidin in 88\% of tile-drain and 95\% of ditch samples, generally higher in ditch than tile water especially in the first five weeks after planting, with an approximately 40- to 50-fold dilution reported between ditch and stream water \parencite{Schaafsma2019}. Finally, at regional scale, glyphosate use in the western Lake Erie basin was significantly correlated with rising annual dissolved reactive phosphorus (DRP) loads---which increased from 101 to 566~Mg~P~y$^{-1}$ between 1994 and 2020---and with flow-weighted mean DRP concentrations, though not with total phosphorus loads \parencite{Spiese2023}.

13.body:
\subsection{Overview}
Investment in agricultural subsurface (tile) drainage is well documented to generate substantial, multi-dimensional socio-economic returns, spanning higher crop yields, greater cropping intensity, increased farm and household income, higher land value, employment, reduced rural out-migration, and agro-industrial development \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007,AbdelDayem2007}. The evidence base assembled here draws on several complementary lines: (i) national and regional programme evaluations documenting yield and income gains after drainage installation \parencite{AbdelDayem2007,Gopalakrishnan2007,Datta2000,Datta2002,Ghumman2011,Wichelns2002}; (ii) formal financial analyses reporting benefit--cost ratios, internal rates of return, net present values, and payback periods \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007,Datta2000,Datta2002,Wichelns2002,Ghumman2011,ROBINSON1992,Bozic2014}; (iii) cost-effectiveness and least-cost design studies for drainage networks, evaporation basins, and disposal options \parencite{Gabr2024,Singh2001,Bakour2021,Bethune2008,HANSON1994,DINAR1989,Napierala2024,Imran2021}; (iv) analyses of how applied research, improved materials, mechanization, and capacity development modernize practice and deliver considerable savings \parencite{Ritzema2007,Ritzema2008,Ritzema2006,AbdelDayem2007,TeKrony2004,Yannopoulos2020}; and (v) regional decision-support and environmental-co-benefit assessments that frame investment and planning \parencite{Burkhalter2006,ROBINSON1992,Quinn2009,Letey1999,McDowell2021,Upadhyay2019,Ayars2015}. Headline findings recur across regions: drainage typically raises average crop yields by roughly 10--30\% and lifts net farm income, while its financial returns are generally favourable where leaching water and sustainable effluent disposal are assured \parencite{AbdelDayem2007,Datta2000,Wichelns2002}.

\subsection{Yield, income, and productivity gains}
The most consistently reported socio-economic benefit is a marked improvement in crop productivity following reclamation of waterlogged and salt-affected land. In Egypt, drainage implementation was associated with crop yield increases of 10 to 30\% on average, higher net annual farm income, and improved gross production values \parencite{AbdelDayem2007}. Using the Nile Delta example, \textcite{Wichelns2002} report that annual gross production values increase by 500 to 550 per hectare in both saline and non-saline areas after drainage installation, that annual net farm incomes rise by 375, 200, and 180 per hectare in non-saline, saline, and rehabilitation areas respectively, and that yield improvement accrues progressively---50\% of the potential improvement in the first year, rising to 75\% in year two, 90\% in year three, and 100\% in year four.

In India, drainage provisions in irrigated areas were reported to deliver many positive socio-economic impacts, including increased crop yields, higher cropping intensity, greater employment, reduced migration to urban areas, higher land value, and development of agro-industries \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. For the Uttar Pradesh Land Reclamation Project, more than 175{,}000 ha of unproductive sodic land were reclaimed, of which over 110{,}000 ha were brought under cultivation; annual farm income increased by over 150\%, cropping intensity rose from 49\% to over 200\%, and annual household income increased by 53\% \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. Small-scale pilot projects in Haryana produced a considerable increase in cropping intensity, a shift toward more remunerative crops, and a remarkable rise in crop yields and farm incomes \parencite{Datta2002}. At Ismila, drainage sharply reduced fallow land and increased annual cropping intensity relative to both the pre-drainage condition and the non-affected control, with wheat yields increasing by about 50\% at Bhana-Brahamana-I and even more strongly at Ujhana \parencite{Datta2002}; in the same project wheat yields in drained fields rose by more than 200\% over 1986--87 to 1992--93, as formerly fallow land was cultivated and barley was replaced by more remunerative wheat in the rabi season \parencite{Datta2000}. This yield response tracks rapid desalinization: at Sampla farm topsoil salinity declined from about 50 dS\,m$^{-1}$ in June 1984 to about 5 dS\,m$^{-1}$ by November 1985 \parencite{Datta2002}, consistent with the fall from about 50 to about 5 dS\,m$^{-1}$ between 1984 and 1991 reported by \textcite{Datta2000}. For the Haryana Operational Pilot Project, subsurface drainage on 1946 ha raised cropping intensity from 140\% in 1991--92 to 185\% in 1995--96 \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}.

Long-term subsurface drainage also improves the soil resource base that underpins these gains. In waterlogged saline Vertisols, drainage reduced salinity and improved multiple physico-chemical and biological properties, raising organic carbon, soil organic carbon stock, dehydrogenase and alkaline phosphatase activity, bulk density, and saturated hydraulic conductivity, with sugarcane yield increasing substantially and positively associated with the soil quality index \parencite{Mukhopadhyay2023}. Across three sites, drainage reduced bulk density from 1.58 to 1.52 Mg\,m$^{-3}$ and ECe from 9.72 to 2.18 dS\,m$^{-1}$, increased saturated hydraulic conductivity from 3.19 to 5.07 cm\,day$^{-1}$, soil organic carbon from 0.22 to 0.34\%, dehydrogenase from 15.44 to 31.65 $\mu$g\,g$^{-1}$\,24\,h$^{-1}$, and alkaline phosphatase from 16.86 to 40.11 $\mu$g\,P-NP\,g$^{-1}$\,h$^{-1}$ \parencite{Mukhopadhyay2023b}. Surface ECe (0--30 cm) fell to 4.23, 1.00, and 1.31 dS\,m$^{-1}$ at Kahni, Siwana Mal, and Jagsi under drainage, versus 13.86, 7.13, and 8.17 dS\,m$^{-1}$ in the controls, and the soil quality index improved by 76\%, 136\%, and 114\% respectively (overall 102\% above the control value of 0.34) \parencite{Mukhopadhyay2023b}. Rice--wheat system yield (rice-equivalent) rose from 1.89 to 8.01, 1.55 to 8.77, and 0.88 to 6.72 Mg\,ha$^{-1}$ across these sites, corresponding to increases of 328\%, 465\%, and 665\% \parencite{Mukhopadhyay2023b}. These gains carry additional carbon co-benefits: soil organic carbon stock increased from 36.49 to 47.91 Mg\,ha$^{-1}$ (0--90 cm), carbon sequestration potential reached up to 4.43 Mg\,C\,ha$^{-1}$\,yr$^{-1}$ at Kahni, and the authors position drainage as supporting land degradation neutrality and the Sustainable Development Goals \parencite{Mukhopadhyay2023b,Mukhopadhyay2023}.

Broader socio-economic uplift is also documented in Pakistan, where a drainage project lowered the groundwater table by an average of 40\% between the pre-project (1996) and post-project (2006) years, raised annual cropping intensity from 102\% to 157\% (a net 55\% increase), and increased the socio-economic standard of living of the project population by 30\% \parencite{Ghumman2011}. At the aggregate scale, \textcite{Valipour2014} notes that in India, despite less than 10\% of cultivated area being drained, salinity has not increased while waterlogging has decreased by more than 70\%, and in Australia limited drainage nonetheless coincides with low salinity and waterlogging.

\subsection{Financial and economic returns}
Formal appraisals confirm that these physical gains translate into favourable financial performance. For the RAJAD project in India, subsurface drainage was installed on 14{,}925 ha using over 3.04 million metres of locally produced corrugated perforated PVC pipe; the financial analysis reported a cost--benefit ratio of 1.28 to 2.87, a net present worth of 12{,}780 to 66{,}170 Rs\,ha$^{-1}$, a payback period of 4.5--6.9 years, and an internal rate of return of 18--35\% \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. The Ismila case provides especially strong evidence because investment costs and benefits over the first seven years were measured and the remainder assessed conservatively \parencite{Datta2000}: it yielded an internal rate of return of 8\% with actual historical costs and 9\% under an alternative benefit scenario \parencite{Datta2000,Datta2002}, rising to almost 10\% and 10.5\%, and 10.5\% and 11.5\%, under present and future investment levels \parencite{Datta2000}. Constructing farm ponds to temporarily store drainage effluent on 5\% of the farm area would reduce the internal rate of return by 1.5\% \parencite{Datta2000}. The Ismila results are presented as hard evidence of the financial feasibility of small-scale, manually installed subsurface drainage for reclaiming salt-affected areas, provided sufficient water is available for leaching and irrigation and effluent can be disposed of sustainably \parencite{Datta2000}.

From both farm-level and public perspectives, returns to Nile Delta drainage investment appear substantial: the present value of enhanced net returns is 2895 per hectare in non-saline areas and 1544 per hectare in saline areas, against a present value of annual payments of 280 per hectare, giving farm-level benefit--cost ratios of 10.3 and 5.5 respectively \parencite{Wichelns2002}. Not all appraisals are uniformly high; in the Pakistan FDP case the benefit per hectare recovered in 10 years was only 17\% of the investment, or 1.7\% per year, even though cropping intensity and living standards rose markedly \parencite{Ghumman2011}. At the project-planning scale, applying the Agricultural Benefit Evaluation model to Cameron County, Texas, indicated substantial agricultural benefits: the present value of project benefits was 83.1 million dollars at the 50\% flood-frequency protection level, rising to 88.2 and 90.9 million dollars at the 20\% and 10\% levels; benefits from lowering the water table alone were about 75 million dollars, with flood-protection benefits of about 8, 13, and 16 million dollars \parencite{ROBINSON1992}. County-wide agricultural gross returns increased by about 23--24.5 million dollars, and for the 20\% protection level the projected regional impacts included a 43.4 million-dollar increase in household income, a 55.4 million-dollar increase in regional economic activity, and over 46 new jobs \parencite{ROBINSON1992}. In Serbia, MODFLOW combined with benefit--cost analysis served as a decision-making tool: any project phase could be accepted, but Phase 5 yielded the greatest benefit-to-cost multiple, and complex protection of Pancevacki Rit demanded high investment yet gave the lowest unit costs and best benefit--cost ratio while reducing the threatened area by up to 40\% \parencite{Bozic2014}.

\subsection{Scale of national and regional drainage programs}
The socio-economic importance of drainage is reflected in the scale of national programmes. In Egypt, drainage expansion became a large national programme, with improved main drainage serving about 2.4 million hectares, subsurface pipe drainage about 1.9 million hectares, and rehabilitated systems about 310{,}000 hectares \parencite{AbdelDayem2007}; the achievements of the Egyptian Public Authority for Drainage Projects are described as a success story, reflecting continuous commitment to economic growth, livelihood and poverty reduction, and environmental challenges \parencite{AbdelDayem2007}. India faced roughly 4.5 million ha of waterlogged and around 6.73 million ha of salt-affected area, of which about 1.39 million ha were reported reclaimed countrywide (1.3 million ha sodic, 0.04 million ha saline-sodic, 0.05 million ha waterlogged), and under the Centrally Sponsored CAD Programme about 57{,}123 ha from 446 schemes in nine states were approved, of which 44{,}135 ha were reclaimed by 2004 \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. In Maharashtra, two pilot schemes covered 2000 ha and about 200 ha of subsurface drainage had been installed by farmers on their own without government support \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. Field experiences from the UP Land Reclamation Project, HOPP, and RAJAD collectively demonstrate that the technology can be successfully adopted to reclaim saline and waterlogged lands in India \parencite{Gopalakrishnan2007}. In the United States, Reclamation constructed over 20{,}930 km of surface and subsurface drains in its first century to protect an estimated 400{,}000 ha of irrigated land from waterlogging and salinity \parencite{TeKrony2004}. Globally, the percent of total cultivated area drained is 12\% and 17\% based on FAO (2013) and IDD (2013) data respectively, with the USA reporting about 32\% drainage together with low salinity \parencite{Valipour2014}.

\subsection{Cost-effectiveness of design and technology choices}
A large body of work shows that careful design and site selection can sharply reduce the cost of achieving drainage benefits. For subsurface drainage networks in the old lands of the Nile Delta, \textcite{Gabr2024} developed novel design charts spanning lateral diameters of 2.5--12.5 cm, hydraulic conductivities of 0.5--4 m\,day$^{-1}$, lateral lengths of 100--300 m, a constant 1000 m collector, recharge of 1--5 mm\,day$^{-1}$, and excavation depths of 1.5--2.0 m. Minimum network costs at a 300 m lateral length were 205--374 per hectare across diameters for baseline conditions, rising to 274--489, 420--717, and 598--984 per hectare under higher recharge and lower conductivity combinations \parencite{Gabr2024}; critically, the maximum lateral length of 300 m decreased cost by 37\% relative to 100 m, and the charts give lower overall costs than conventional design approaches \parencite{Gabr2024}. In Syria, an optimization model coded in MATLAB with an interior-point algorithm found vertical drainage economically better than horizontal drainage for the case study, with the optimum favouring the largest possible spacing between pipes and wells (the dominant cost factor) and the minimum feasible pumping duration over the project lifespan \parencite{Bakour2021}.

Disposal infrastructure follows similar economies of scale. For evaporation basins in Australia, cost per hectare and per Ml of storage declined with increasing basin size, with earthworks, geotechnical investigation, and interception of lateral leakage as the major cost constituents \parencite{Singh2001}. Full geotechnical assessment increased net present cost by about 8--10\% over minimum investigation, while choosing a site that avoids leakage control can roughly halve the cost of a large basin; square or rectangular basins outperformed triangular ones, greater cell size and bank height reduced unit cost, and pipe drains were cheaper than open drains for smaller basins \parencite{Singh2001}. Site selection, basin geometry, and leakage control are therefore identified as critical to cost minimization \parencite{Singh2001}. Comparing drainage-management options in the Shepparton region, conversion from border-check to sprinkler irrigation had the lowest net present cost and, together with tubewell drainage with farm reuse, produced positive financial outcomes, whereas tubewell or horizontal pipe drainage discharging to evaporation basins were much more costly and became still more expensive as deep percolation increased \parencite{Bethune2008}.

Field-level water management also modulates drainage volumes and costs. Reducing field length and irrigation set time was the most effective surface-irrigation upgrade for drainage reduction, in one example lowering subsurface drainage from 63 to 25 mm in clay loam and from 84 to 20 mm in sandy loam, while increasing furrow flow rate had only limited benefit and surge irrigation reduced drainage by about 30--40\% \parencite{HANSON1994}. Economic outcomes were strongly site-specific: traditional furrow irrigation was often more profitable when disposal costs were zero, but higher disposal costs favoured LEPA, linear-move, or drip systems, with break-even disposal costs of 263--1{,}042 \$/ha-m for the shift from traditional to improved furrow and 3{,}852--32{,}470 \$/ha-m for the shift to drip \parencite{HANSON1994}. Technology can also lower the cost of active water-table control: a new flashboard-riser design cut total operation time from 701--982 s to 136--144 s, sped up stop-log handling five- to seven-fold, reduced total working time by 16--23\%, and had the lowest annual amortization, giving an average controlled-drainage implementation cost of 12.93 per year per hectare \parencite{Napierala2024}. Redesigning the Paharang system to allow continuous gravity discharge of subsurface effluent---by lowering channel bed levels (11 of 24 reaches by about 3 m or more) and requiring 1435 and 1030 m$^{3}$ of earthwork for the main and branch drains---was intended to reduce dependence on pumping, electricity, labour, and maintenance and to make budget, theft, and law-and-order constraints irrelevant \parencite{Imran2021}. Modernization of Egypt's systems is likewise motivated by high maintenance costs and limited lifespan, with controlled drainage reducing over-drainage and improving irrigation efficiency, and switching from gravity to pumped drainage cutting open-drain size and operation-and-maintenance costs while supporting reuse \parencite{Ritzema2023}.

\subsection{Materials, mechanization, and modernization}
Technological change has been a persistent driver of lower drainage costs and higher quality. Since the 1950s, and especially since the 1960s, revolutionary installation equipment, materials, and techniques improved drainage-system quality and durability, with corrugated plastic pipes becoming the preferred standard and factory pre-wrapping of synthetic envelopes improving quality control while reducing installation costs by roughly 50\% \parencite{Yannopoulos2020}. The shift from manual to mechanized subsurface drainage required new materials, quality-control systems, and training: plastic PVC/PE pipes and synthetic envelopes lowered transportation and installation costs and improved construction quality relative to clay or concrete pipes and organic or gravel envelopes, while total quality-control systems and laser grade control maintained installation quality at high speed \parencite{Ritzema2006}. In Egypt, research and development led to adoption of corrugated PVC and HDPE pipes, synthetic envelopes, and region-specific design criteria, and trenchless drainage machines improved construction productivity and reduced installation costs relative to trencher machines \parencite{AbdelDayem2007}. New installation materials and methods---plastic pipes, laser grade control, synthetic envelopes, trenchless drainage, and improved quality control---together increased installation rates and reduced installation costs, while improved operation and maintenance, including modified layouts for rice areas and farmer participation, reduced maintenance and labour costs \parencite{Ritzema2007}. Over its first century, Reclamation's drainage practice evolved from an art into a science that removed most technical unknowns, with the development of reliable design drain spacing cited as probably its most significant contribution to the international knowledge base \parencite{TeKrony2004}.

\subsection{Applied research and capacity development as ``value for money''}
A distinct and strongly supported conclusion is that linking applied research and capacity development to drainage implementation is highly cost-effective. Applied research on drainage in developing countries was reported to have ``value for money,'' with benefits that easily outweigh costs when research is linked to implementation \parencite{Ritzema2007}. Over 40 years of partnership among institutions in Egypt, Pakistan, and India with Alterra-ILRI led to modernization of practice and considerable cost savings, and to some of the largest, most modern, and most effective subsurface drainage programmes \parencite{Ritzema2007}. Several concrete savings are documented: measuring soil salinity with the EM38 lowered costs and improved data quality relative to traditional sampling and laboratory analysis; a three-step performance assessment ensured rehabilitation was initiated only when confirmed necessary, producing considerable savings; interceptor drains were shown not to justify their large investment and their unnecessary implementation was prevented; cancelling negligibly beneficial canal lining saved substantial investment; groundwater modelling showed subsurface drainage was needed in only part of a project area and allowed reduced design discharge; and overly conservative design discharge rates and drain depths were reduced, lowering installation costs and effluent salt loads \parencite{Ritzema2007}. Controlled drainage was reported to maintain or increase yields while raising irrigation water-use efficiency and reducing downstream impacts, and modified layouts for rice areas cut irrigation water use, pumping costs, and design discharge \parencite{Ritzema2007}.

The complementary capacity-development literature reaches the same verdict. Five examples show that capacity development is strengthened when research, education and training, and advisory services are applied in an integrated manner to link tacit and explicit knowledge, supported by recurrent tailor-made courses and a publication series of more than 50 titles \parencite{Ritzema2008}. This process helped Egypt develop one of the largest and most modern subsurface drainage programmes in the world, produced site-specific recommendations for different agro-climatic regions in India, and shifted the focus toward farmers' participation in Pakistan \parencite{Ritzema2008,Ritzema2006}. Capacity development is characterized as a dynamic, long-term process that builds mutual trust through repeated engagement, and the combination of capacity development with applied research and application is explicitly judged ``value for money,'' having modernized practice and achieved considerable savings through new methods of investigation, design, planning, installation, and operation and maintenance \parencite{Ritzema2008}. In-service training and the Drainage Training Centre contributed substantially to mechanized implementation in Egypt \parencite{Ritzema2006}.

\subsection{Economic instruments and policy dimensions}
Because drainage water carries disposal costs and externalities, its economics interact with policy design. In California, under an unregulated policy applied water and drainage volumes exceeded economically efficient levels (for example, 138 cm applied water and 67 cm drainage at one price combination), whereas a direct drainage fee induced efficient volumes---at CUC = 72, Pw = 1, and Pd = 3 US\$/ha-cm the efficient solution was 97 cm applied water and 29 cm drainage, with a society net benefit of 1132 US\$/ha \parencite{DINAR1989}. A flat fee on irrigation water could induce efficient applications but generated excess revenues (361 US\$/ha in one case), while tiered pricing induced efficiency with deficient revenues (32 US\$/ha); results were highly sensitive to irrigation uniformity, justifying investment in uniformity-improving technology, especially as disposal costs rise \parencite{DINAR1989}. The San Joaquin Valley experience underscores that economics alone do not govern outcomes: at Kesterson Reservoir, selenium from drainage water caused very high bird mortality and deformities (20\% of nests with deformed birds, about 40\% of eggs with dead embryos), and subsequent drainage management was shaped by political and social constraints, leading to the central conclusion that factors other than scientific facts bear on decisions and that successful management plans must be technically sound, economically viable, and socially acceptable \parencite{Letey1999}.

\subsection{Regional benefit assessment, decision support, and co-benefits}
Finally, a set of regional-scale and modelling studies frames drainage investment within basin-wide productivity and environmental accounting. In Colorado, regional solution alternatives were predicted to substantially improve waterlogging and salinity, with combination alternatives producing the largest benefits: a maximum average water-table deepening of 1.93 m, a maximum average soil-salinity decrease of 950 mg/L, salt-load reductions to the river of about 30\% and 20\% for recharge and seepage reduction individually (reaching roughly 40--45\% for combinations), and relative crop-yield increases of up to 9.6 percentage points \parencite{Burkhalter2006}. Major benefits were expected as increased agricultural productivity, enhanced river water quality through reduced salt loading, and in some cases water conservation, with combined interventions producing the greatest gains \parencite{Burkhalter2006}. The Agricultural Benefit Evaluation model was proposed as an effective tool for estimating benefits and economic impacts while handling data inadequacy and post-treatment changes in productivity and crop distribution \parencite{ROBINSON1992}. Environmental co-benefits are also quantified: the Grasslands Bypass Project achieved average selenium-load reductions of 60\% relative to pre-project means over nine years and is described as one of the most successful non-point-source management initiatives undertaken by the US Bureau of Reclamation, with real-time monitoring and adaptive management as key contributors \parencite{Quinn2009}. At national scale in New Zealand, established mitigation actions could reduce N and P loads by 16\% and 23\% (larger on dairy land), and developing actions could reduce pastoral N, P, and sediment loads by 34\%, 39\%, and 66\% by 2035, informing rural-sector investment and planning \parencite{McDowell2021}. However, artificial drainage shifts diffuse phosphorus load from overland to subsurface flow, so a Bayesian-network analysis found only a modest net reduction in total phosphorus loss relative to an undrained farm \parencite{Lucci2014}, and tile drainage is identified as a connectivity pathway that can transport phosphorus carried downward by preferential (crack and burrow) flow to lake tributaries \parencite{Allaire2011,Dadfar2010}. Land-management scenario modelling of pothole wetlands provides a policy-support tool for alternative management and investment decisions \parencite{Upadhyay2019}.

Synthesizing across these strands, the design and operation of drainage systems increasingly must reconcile agronomic production with environmental values: drainage water quality is advanced as a necessary design criterion, and active management of groundwater-table position and discharge, improved field characterization, and decision-support systems are identified as key future directions for sustainable, cost-effective drainage \parencite{Ayars2015}. Deeper drains and wider spacing raise drain-water salinity, flow, and salt load, so system design itself carries downstream economic and environmental consequences that modern, decentralized, location-specific approaches seek to manage \parencite{Yannopoulos2020,Ritzema2023}. Taken together, the evidence establishes that well-designed subsurface drainage investment, supported by applied research, appropriate materials, and capacity development, reliably delivers strong agronomic, financial, and social returns while its net benefits are maximized when disposal costs, water-use efficiency, and environmental externalities are explicitly incorporated into design and appraisal.

